export const REVIEWED = {"Japan":{"slug":"japan","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Not customary, politely declined","confidence":"medium","tip":"Skip the tip and thank your server with a genuine \"gochisōsama deshita\" after the meal; to reward a tour guide, offer a small amount discreetly in an envelope rather than handing over cash directly.","explanation":"Tipping isn't part of Japanese service culture — excellent service is already the standard (omotenashi), so offering extra cash can come across as confusing or even imply staff aren't paid enough, and servers will often try to refuse or return it. It's less a deep insult than an unfamiliar, slightly awkward gesture in most everyday settings.","regional_note":"Exceptions exist: ryokan guests sometimes give staff a small 'kokorozuke' envelope for extra care, and some upscale hotels or international-style restaurants add a service charge to the bill instead of accepting tips.","also_check":[{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15–20% is expected and a core part of service workers' income."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"Service is included by law; rounding up is a small, optional gesture."},{"country":"China","verdict":"caution","note":"Traditionally not expected and sometimes refused, though changing in tourist hubs."}],"sources":[{"title":"Tipping in Japan","url":"https://www.japan.travel/en/plan/tipping-in-japan/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Why You Would Be Considered Rude For Tipping In This Popular Country","url":"https://matadornetwork.com/read/never-tip-japan/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Do you Tip in Japan? Japanese Tipping Etiquette","url":"https://www.insidejapantours.com/blog/2013/09/22/do-you-tip-in-japan-japanese-tipping-etiquette/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Keep feet tucked, not a taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"When seated on tatami, tuck your feet under you or sit cross-legged neatly rather than stretching your legs out toward other people or a room's tokonoma alcove.","explanation":"Japan doesn't share the Thai or Middle Eastern belief that feet are unclean or that showing soles is a grave insult. The real norm is about formal sitting posture: in seiza (formal kneeling) or sitting cross-legged on tatami, stretching your legs out carelessly toward other people, a shrine's altar, or the tokonoma alcove reads as sloppy or disrespectful rather than a deliberate insult.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"Feet are considered the lowest, dirtiest body part; pointing soles at someone or a Buddha image is a serious insult."},{"country":"Egypt","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the sole of your foot or shoe is a strong insult, often associated with contempt."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Showing the soles of your feet is generally neutral, though propping feet on furniture may be seen as informal in some settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Seiza","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seiza","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Tokonoma","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokonoma","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"No taboo, but mind chopsticks","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you're left-handed, it's fine to hold chopsticks in your left hand — just never stand them upright in a rice bowl or pass food directly between chopsticks, since both gestures echo funeral rituals.","explanation":"Japan has no toilet-related taboo against the left hand like South Asia or the Middle East — being left-handed at the table isn't considered unclean or offensive. The real etiquette concerns are chopstick-specific: use whichever hand is dominant, and avoid taboos like standing chopsticks upright in rice or passing food chopstick-to-chopstick, both of which mimic funeral rites.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is considered unclean from bathroom hygiene; always eat and pass food with the right hand."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Eating or passing food with the left hand is taboo; use the right hand, especially for shared dishes."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"No stigma against left-hand use; left-handed diners are accommodated without issue."}],"sources":[{"title":"A Basic Guide to Japanese Etiquette","url":"https://www.nippon.com/en/guide-to-japan/gu020010/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Japanese etiquette","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine for kids, too familiar for adults","confidence":"medium","tip":"Save head-pats for children you know well; with adults, a nod, bow, or verbal compliment shows warmth without the overly familiar physical contact.","explanation":"Japan doesn't treat the head as sacred the way Thai Buddhist culture does, so patting someone's head isn't a severe taboo. It's normal and affectionate to pat a child's head, but doing the same to an adult — especially a stranger, senior, or colleague — reads as condescending or overly familiar rather than friendly, since Japanese social interaction is shaped by age and status hierarchy.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the highest and most sacred body part; touching it, even a child's, is a serious breach of etiquette."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head is often affectionate, but touching an adult's head can be seen as disrespectful, especially with elders."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Patting someone on the head is generally seen as friendly or playful, though context and relationship matter."}],"sources":[{"title":"Japan Guide — Hierarchy and Respect in Japanese Society","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/japan-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Amae","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amae","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Decline gently, not bluntly","confidence":"medium","tip":"Decline politely with a phrase like \"mō kekkō desu\" (I'm fine, thank you) or mention you're full, and close the meal with \"gochisōsama deshita\" to show appreciation either way.","explanation":"Japanese hosts often expect a guest to show a little reserve (enryo) before accepting, and may offer food more than once, so an abrupt, flat refusal can feel blunt. Politely declining is still widely accepted, especially today — it's more about tone and phrasing than a hard rule against saying no.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food is often seen as rejecting hospitality; it's better to accept a small portion."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"caution","note":"Declining a host's food can seem standoffish, though most hosts respect dietary limits if explained warmly."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"It's generally acceptable to politely decline food if full or restricted, though some hosts may insist once."}],"sources":[{"title":"Japan Guide — Social Etiquette and Cultural Norms","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/japan-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Japanese etiquette","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Slurping shows appreciation","confidence":"medium","tip":"Feel free to slurp noodles audibly in a casual noodle shop — it's a compliment to the chef, not bad manners — but don't force it if it doesn't feel natural.","explanation":"Slurping noodles like ramen, soba, or udon is traditionally seen as a sign of enjoying the meal, and it also helps cool hot noodles as you eat. It's widely accepted in casual and mid-range noodle shops, though a modern sensitivity — half-jokingly called 'nuudoru harasumento' (noodle harassment) — has emerged around whether loud slurping bothers nearby diners, especially tourists.","regional_note":"The slurping norm applies mainly to noodle dishes, not dining generally, and very formal kaiseki meals tend to be quieter overall.","also_check":[{"country":"China","verdict":"caution","note":"Slurping noodles is generally acceptable in casual settings, but in formal dining it may be seen as noisy or impolite."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping is typically considered bad table manners and associated with lack of self-control."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta is strongly frowned upon; it is seen as uncivilized and disrespectful to the food."}],"sources":[{"title":"Soba","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soba","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Japanese cuisine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_cuisine","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Not a standard greeting","confidence":"medium","tip":"Default to a bow, or a soft handshake if one is offered first, and don't initiate a cheek kiss unless your host clearly does.","explanation":"Bowing — sometimes paired with a handshake in business settings — is the standard Japanese greeting, and cheek kissing isn't part of everyday social custom. It mainly appears in formal diplomatic contexts, like meetings between heads of state, so using it in an ordinary social setting can come across as oddly intimate or foreign.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"La bise (cheek kissing) is a standard greeting among friends and acquaintances, varying by region in number of kisses."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing is not a traditional greeting and can feel overly familiar; bowing or a handshake is preferred."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"A single cheek kiss is a common, friendly greeting, especially among women or in social settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Cheek kissing","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheek_kissing","kind":"reference"},{"title":"How to Shake Hands with the Japanese","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/articles/how-to-shake-hands-with-the-japanese/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Modest, well-wrapped gifts expected","confidence":"medium","tip":"Bring a modest, nicely wrapped gift (regional omiyage works well), present it with both hands, and don't be surprised if it's politely set aside unopened rather than opened in front of you.","explanation":"Gift-giving is a meaningful way to build relationships and show appreciation in Japan, whether as a business courtesy, a seasonal gift, or omiyage (a souvenir brought back for colleagues and family). Presentation matters more than price: gifts are modestly wrapped and usually given and received with both hands, and sets of four or nine items are avoided because those numbers sound like 'death' and 'suffering.'","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"China","verdict":"caution","note":"Gift-giving is common but avoid clocks, umbrellas, and sharp objects; white or black wrapping is taboo."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts are appreciated but should be modest and practical; overly expensive gifts can be seen as bribery."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"caution","note":"Gift-giving is polite but avoid alcohol unless you know the recipient's religion; use the right hand only."}],"sources":[{"title":"Mizuhiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mizuhiki","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Old Enough! (Hajimete no Otsukai)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Enough!","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Japanese etiquette","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Omiyage","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omiyage","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Japan Guide — Social Etiquette and Cultural Norms","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/japan-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"China":{"slug":"china","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Not customary, often declined","confidence":"medium","tip":"Skip tipping at everyday restaurants, but set aside a modest cash tip for a tour guide or driver at the end of a trip, since that's the one context where it's genuinely expected.","explanation":"Tipping is not part of mainland Chinese restaurant culture; menu prices are final and there is no built-in service charge being pointed to. Staff may refuse a tip outright or chase you down to return it, and some locals read an offered tip as implying they're underpaid, though this isn't universally seen as outright insulting. The real exceptions are tour guides and drivers, who commonly expect a tip, and a handful of international luxury hotels catering to Western guests.","regional_note":"Hong Kong and Macau, shaped by longer British and Portuguese influence, have normalized tipping (often ~10%) far more than mainland China.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is not practiced and can be seen as confusing or even insulting; excellent service is simply the norm."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% at restaurants is standard and service staff rely on it as part of their income."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"A service charge is legally included in the bill, but rounding up or leaving small change is a nice gesture."}],"sources":[{"title":"Chinese Culture - Etiquette (Cultural Atlas, SBS / Multicultural NSW)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/chinese-culture/chinese-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Tipping in China - Intrepid Travel","url":"https://www.intrepidtravel.com/ca/china/tipping-in-china","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Tipping in China - China Xian Tour","url":"https://www.chinaxiantour.com/travel-guide/tipping-in-china","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Widely positive, no offense","confidence":"high","tip":"Use a thumbs-up freely to compliment food, a performance, or someone's work; it reads as warm in both casual and semi-formal settings.","explanation":"In China, a thumbs-up is understood the same way as in most Western countries: praise, agreement, or 'well done.' It's used casually by ordinary people and even by officials as a sign of approval, and carries no negative or vulgar meaning.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"Thumbs-up is considered an obscene insult rather than a positive gesture."},{"country":"Greece","verdict":"caution","note":"The gesture has historically carried a vulgar connotation there, though global familiarity has softened this among younger people."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Thumbs-up is a common, everyday sign of approval with no negative connotation."}],"sources":[{"title":"Dos and Don'ts of Chinese Hand Gestures - The Chairman's Bao","url":"https://www.thechairmansbao.com/gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"What does a thumbs up in China mean? - Calendar UK","url":"https://www.calendar-uk.co.uk/frequently-asked-questions/what-does-a-thumbs-up-in-china-mean","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Seen as disrespectful","confidence":"medium","tip":"When sitting, especially with elders or hosts nearby, keep your feet flat on the floor or crossed at the ankle rather than propped up or pointed at anyone.","explanation":"Chinese etiquette guides consistently describe feet as the 'lowest' part of the body, so deliberately showing the soles of your feet or pointing your feet at someone is considered disrespectful. This is treated less severely than in Thailand, where it intersects with Buddhist sacred-space rules, but it's still something hosts and etiquette guides flag as impolite.","regional_note":"Younger, more Western-influenced urban Chinese tend to be more relaxed about this than older or more traditional households.","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing feet at people or at Buddha images is a serious insult, since the foot is the lowest and head the highest part of the body."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the soles of your feet is considered a clear sign of contempt."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Propping feet on furniture or toward others is seen as casual or rude in formal settings but isn't a major taboo."}],"sources":[{"title":"9 Chinese Hand Gestures You Can Learn Today - FluentU","url":"https://www.fluentu.com/blog/chinese/2017/12/04/chinese-gestures/","kind":"community"},{"title":"7 Gestures to Avoid in China - Listen & Learn USA","url":"https://www.listenandlearnusa.com/blog/7-gestures-to-avoid-in-china","kind":"community"},{"title":"Chinese Etiquette Guide (PDF) - University of Exeter","url":"https://exeter.ac.uk/media/universityofexeter/internationalexeter/pdfs/Chinese%20Etiquette%20Guide.pdf","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Fine, no real taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Use whichever hand feels natural for your chopsticks; if you're left-handed, just be mindful of your elbow room at a crowded table.","explanation":"China has no hygiene-based taboo on the left hand like South Asia or the Middle East. Chopsticks were traditionally held in the right hand mainly to avoid elbows and sticks colliding with neighbors at a shared table, not because the left hand was considered unclean, and left-handed chopstick use is increasingly common and accepted today.","regional_note":"In Xinjiang and other Muslim-majority areas of China, broader Islamic custom can make the left hand less preferred for eating, similar to Muslim practice elsewhere.","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is reserved for bathroom hygiene, so eating or passing food with it is considered unclean."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is reserved for unclean tasks; eating or passing food with it is considered offensive."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"There's no stigma on the left hand; chopstick etiquette instead focuses on rules like not pointing or stabbing food with chopsticks."}],"sources":[{"title":"Chopsticks - Ninchanese Blog","url":"https://ninchanese.com/blog/2013/01/16/chopsticks/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Chopstick etiquette article - italki","url":"https://www.italki.com/fr/article/129","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine for kids, odd for adults","confidence":"medium","tip":"Patting a friend's or stranger's child on the head as a friendly gesture is generally fine; avoid doing the same to adults you don't know well.","explanation":"China doesn't carry the strict Buddhist head-sacredness taboo found in Thailand or Laos; it's common, even affectionate, for an adult to pat a child's head. Touching an adult's or stranger's head, though, is still generally seen as an invasive overstep of personal space rather than a deep spiritual offense.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body in Buddhist belief, so touching anyone's head, including a child's, is a serious offense."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Touching an elder's or stranger's head is disrespectful, though patting a child's head is often acceptable among close family."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Patting a child's head or a close friend's head affectionately is generally seen as warm rather than offensive."}],"sources":[{"title":"9 Ways to Avoid Offending Anyone When Traveling in Asia - Afar Magazine","url":"https://www.afar.com/magazine/9-things-that-are-surprisingly-offensive-in-asia","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Culture question - touching a child's head (forum thread) - Chinese-Forums.com","url":"https://www.chinese-forums.com/forums/topic/7557-culture-question-touching-a-childs-head/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Soften gaze with seniors","confidence":"high","tip":"With a senior colleague, client, or elder, use soft, intermittent eye contact rather than a constant direct gaze to signal respect without seeming evasive.","explanation":"In Chinese business and social settings, sustained direct eye contact—especially toward someone older or more senior—can read as a challenge or confrontation rather than confidence. Chinese people often lower their eyes slightly as a sign of respect, favoring brief, intermittent eye contact over a fixed stare.","regional_note":"Younger professionals in cosmopolitan cities like Shanghai and Shenzhen, especially those used to Western business norms, tend to be more comfortable with sustained eye contact.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged eye contact can feel intimidating; briefer glances or looking near the neck area are more typical."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct eye contact is read as a sign of confidence, honesty, and engagement."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Direct eye contact between genders is generally avoided, while it's more acceptable, though not prolonged, between men."}],"sources":[{"title":"Chinese Culture - Etiquette (Cultural Atlas, SBS / Multicultural NSW)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/chinese-culture/chinese-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Averted Gaze Respect - HI-COM Asia (China business/legal translation)","url":"https://translate.hicom-asia.com/area/averted-gaze-respect/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected, hygiene-driven norm","confidence":"high","tip":"Take your shoes off at the entryway if you see a shoe rack or slippers laid out, and wear whatever indoor slippers your host offers.","explanation":"Removing shoes before entering a Chinese home is the norm nationwide, rooted in keeping floors clean, and hosts typically provide indoor slippers for guests. It isn't a strict rule everywhere, though—some rural households with hard or concrete flooring are more relaxed about it.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes is mandatory in homes and many traditional inns, with indoor slippers provided."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal is optional and varies by household, so it's best to follow the host's lead."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"Many households keep shoes on indoors, so removing them isn't automatically expected, though some hosts do prefer it."}],"sources":[{"title":"Shoes or no shoes inside, Chinese haven't always agreed - South China Morning Post","url":"https://www.scmp.com/magazines/post-magazine/short-reads/article/2187916/shoes-or-inside-chinese-havent-always-been","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Shoes Off - The World of Chinese","url":"https://www.theworldofchinese.com/post/shoes-off","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Open hand is more polite","confidence":"medium","tip":"When indicating a person or direction, use an open hand gesture instead of a single pointed finger.","explanation":"Pointing directly at a person with a single extended index finger can come across as aggressive or accusatory in China; the more polite convention is to gesture with an open hand, palm up, toward the person or object. It's a matter of perceived rudeness rather than a severe taboo, and younger, more casual settings are more forgiving of it.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Pointing with a finger is seen as impolite; gesturing with an open hand is preferred."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"caution","note":"Index-finger pointing is considered impolite; using the thumb to indicate direction is the common alternative."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing with the index finger is common and generally unremarkable in casual settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Dos and Don'ts of Chinese Hand Gestures - The Chairman's Bao","url":"https://www.thechairmansbao.com/gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Chinese Pointing Explained: Meaning, Culture, Usage - Prepedu","url":"https://prepedu.com/en/blog/chinese-pointing","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Discreet is safer, context-dependent","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep physical affection low-key in public—a quick hand-hold is fine in most cities, but save hugging or kissing for private settings, especially around older people.","explanation":"Overt PDA like passionate kissing or hugging is still generally frowned upon in China, especially by older generations, and can draw disapproving looks, though it's not illegal. Younger, urban couples increasingly hold hands or share a brief, discreet kiss in public, so tolerance varies a lot by generation and city.","regional_note":"Big cities like Shanghai and Beijing are visibly more tolerant of PDA among younger couples than smaller towns or rural areas.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Kissing and casual affection in public are widely accepted and unremarkable."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"rude","note":"Public affection between unmarried couples can draw legal consequences, not just social disapproval."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Hugging, hand-holding, and kissing a partner in public are common and socially accepted."}],"sources":[{"title":"Can you kiss in public in China? - La Cocina de Gisele","url":"https://lacocinadegisele.com/knowledgebase/can-you-kiss-in-public-in-china","kind":"community"},{"title":"Why Chinese Have Been Slow to Embrace Public Displays of Affection - Sixth Tone","url":"https://www.sixthtone.com/news/1000611/why-chinese-have-been-slow-to-embrace-","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"rude","label":"Punctuality signals respect","confidence":"high","tip":"Plan to arrive 10 minutes early, and if you're unavoidably delayed, call ahead with a clear apology and an updated arrival time.","explanation":"Arriving late to a business meeting in China is widely seen as disrespectful and can cost you 'face' (mianzi) with your counterparts, so punctuality is taken seriously, especially for a first meeting. That said, Chinese hosts themselves may ease into business with informal small talk or tea before getting started, so a slower start to the agenda isn't the same as a guest arriving late.","regional_note":"Younger, more informal startups in cities like Shenzhen may tolerate a few minutes' delay, but it's still safer to assume strict punctuality is expected.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is taken extremely seriously, and arriving even a couple of minutes late is seen as a real lapse."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"Running 15-30 minutes late to social events is common, but punctuality is still expected for business meetings."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is treated as a core sign of professionalism, and lateness is viewed negatively."}],"sources":[{"title":"Chinese Culture - Etiquette (Cultural Atlas, SBS / Multicultural NSW)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/chinese-culture/chinese-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"A Guide to Chinese Business Etiquette - CIBT Visas","url":"https://cibtvisas.com/blog/business-etiquette-china","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Expect a ritual back-and-forth","confidence":"high","tip":"Decline once or twice with a smile if you're not hungry, but expect your host to insist; if you truly don't want more, say so plainly and directly rather than repeating the polite refusal.","explanation":"In China, there's a real distinction between a 'ritual refusal' (politely declining once or twice out of modesty) and a genuine refusal. Hosts typically expect to insist a few times before taking no for an answer, so a single 'no thank you' is often read as politeness rather than a real decline.","regional_note":"In more rural or traditional households, the insisting can go on longer than in urban or younger families, who may accept a firmer 'no' more readily.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"An initial polite refusal is common, and leaving a little food on the plate is a typical way to signal you're satisfied."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"caution","note":"Declining a host's food too firmly can come across as rejecting their hospitality and generosity."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"An initial refusal is expected as polite modesty, and hosts typically insist before a guest accepts."}],"sources":[{"title":"Chinese Culture - Etiquette (Cultural Atlas, SBS / Multicultural NSW)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/chinese-culture/chinese-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Chinese Host and Guest Customs - China Highlights","url":"https://www.chinahighlights.com/travelguide/chinese-host-and-guest-customs.htm","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Harmless, but can mean 'three'","confidence":"medium","tip":"Use the OK sign for agreement or approval if you like, but don't be surprised if an older or rural person reads it as the number three rather than 'okay.'","explanation":"The OK hand sign isn't offensive in China, but it isn't purely an 'approval' gesture either: the same thumb-and-finger ring shape is also one of the ways to represent the number three in Chinese hand-counting gestures. In practice this means it's generally safe to use but can occasionally cause mild confusion rather than being read as a thumbs-up-style compliment.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture is a well-known vulgar insult, comparable to an obscene gesture rather than a sign of approval."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture can be read as a crude insult in some contexts, so it's best avoided."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"It can be understood to mean 'zero' or 'worthless' rather than approval, depending on context."}],"sources":[{"title":"Guide to Chinese Hand Gestures - Mandarin Blueprint","url":"https://www.mandarinblueprint.com/blog/guide-to-chinese-hand-gestures/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Use Your Body Gestures to Communicate in Chinese - ChineseClass101","url":"https://www.chineseclass101.com/blog/?p=2919","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Usually fine, context matters","confidence":"medium","tip":"Slurp freely at a casual noodle shop or home meal, but tone it down at a business dinner or upscale restaurant where a quieter style is more expected.","explanation":"Slurping noodles in China is traditionally read as enjoyment and a compliment to the cook, much like in Japan, and it genuinely does help cool hot noodle soup. But this is contested more than guides often admit: attitudes are shifting, particularly among younger, urban diners and in formal or high-end settings, where quieter eating is increasingly preferred.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is traditionally seen as a sign of enjoyment, though younger people increasingly see loud slurping as old-fashioned."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta loudly is considered poor table manners."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Audible slurping is generally seen as a minor breach of table manners rather than a serious offense."}],"sources":[{"title":"Why Asian Food Is Loud - Tatler Asia","url":"https://www.tatlerasia.com/dining/food/etiquette-manners-why-asian-food-is-loud","kind":"reference"},{"title":"The Role of Noises in Chinese Dining Etiquette - ali.mk","url":"https://ali.mk/posts/the-role-of-noises-in-chinese-dining-etiquette-a-cultural-insight/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Not customary, can feel awkward","confidence":"high","tip":"Greet with a handshake and a verbal 'ni hao' rather than attempting a cheek kiss, even in social settings.","explanation":"A handshake or a simple verbal greeting, not a cheek kiss, is the norm when meeting someone in China. Physical greetings like hugging or kissing are generally avoided, even among acquaintances, because they can feel overly intimate by local standards.","regional_note":"In expat-heavy circles of cities like Shanghai or Beijing, cheek kissing among close Western friends is sometimes tolerated, but it's still far from the norm.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"La bise (cheek kissing) is a standard greeting among friends and acquaintances, with the number of kisses varying by region."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Physical greetings like cheek kissing are unusual and can feel invasive; a bow is the expected greeting."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing, often once on each cheek, is a common greeting even among new acquaintances."}],"sources":[{"title":"Chinese Etiquette Essentials: 10 Tips to Good Manners in China - China Tours","url":"https://www.chinatours.com/chinese-etiquette-good-manners","kind":"reference"},{"title":"The Ultimate Guide to Chinese Etiquette: 25 Cultural Tips - LC Chinese School","url":"https://lcchineseschool.com/?p=12355","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Use palm-down, not one finger","confidence":"medium","tip":"To call someone over, wave with your palm facing down and fingers curling inward, rather than crooking a single raised index finger.","explanation":"Beckoning someone with a single upturned index finger is associated with calling an animal and is considered disrespectful in China. The accepted alternative is to extend the hand palm-down and curl the fingers toward yourself in a waving motion.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a single finger is considered very rude and can even be treated as a provocation."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Beckoning with the index finger is seen as impolite; a palm-down wave with all fingers is the accepted alternative."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Beckoning with the index finger is common and unremarkable in casual settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"9 Chinese Hand Gestures You Can Learn Today - FluentU","url":"https://www.fluentu.com/blog/chinese/2017/12/04/chinese-gestures/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Additional Etiquette Tip (study abroad blog) - Missouri State University","url":"https://blogs.missouristate.edu/studyaway-cpham/2014/04/20/additional-etiquette-tip/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Valued, but with real rules","confidence":"medium","tip":"Give something modest like fine tea wrapped in red or gold, present it with both hands, and don't be surprised if the recipient initially waves it off once or twice before accepting—that's a polite ritual on their end, not a cue for you to withdraw the gift.","explanation":"Gift-giving is a warmly regarded way to build relationships (guanxi) in China, but it comes with specific taboos: avoid clocks (the phrase for 'giving a clock' sounds like a funeral-related phrase), avoid sets of four (the word for 'four' sounds like 'death'), and skip white or black wrapping, which are associated with funerals, in favor of red or gold. In business and especially government contexts, keep gifts modest—since Xi Jinping's 2012 'eight-point' anti-corruption rules, giving lavish gifts or cash to officials carries real legal and career risk, not just a social misstep.","regional_note":"Hong Kong and Taiwan share the same clock and number-four taboos, since both stem from Mandarin/Cantonese homophones rather than being mainland-specific superstitions.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized, with sets of four or nine avoided and plain or subdued wrapping generally preferred over flashy colors."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Business gifts are often unnecessary or seen as slightly inappropriate; if given at all, something small and practical is best."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"polite","note":"Gifts are welcomed but should avoid alcohol or pork, and should be given with the right hand or both hands, not the left."}],"sources":[{"title":"Chinese Culture - Etiquette (Cultural Atlas, SBS / Multicultural NSW)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/chinese-culture/chinese-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Eight-point Regulation - background coverage - IDN Financials","url":"https://www.idnfinancials.com/cn/news/59912/john-ross-china-is-more-detailed-in-dealing-with-corruption-than-west","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Crack the Code: China's Unspoken Gift Etiquette Revealed - Classy Mandarin","url":"https://classymandarin.substack.com/p/the-unspoken-chinese-gift-etiqutte","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Thailand":{"slug":"thailand","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Optional, increasingly appreciated","confidence":"medium","tip":"Check the bill for an already-added service charge before tipping extra, and if there isn't one, leave loose change or round up in cash for good service.","explanation":"Tipping is not a traditional Thai custom and is never strictly expected, especially at street food stalls and local eateries where rounding up is enough. In tourist-oriented restaurants and upscale venues a service charge (often around 10%) is commonly already added to the bill, and where it isn't, leaving 5-10% for good service has become a common, welcomed gesture. Skipping a tip entirely is not seen as rude by most Thai staff.","regional_note":"Tipping is far more common in tourist hubs and hotels in Bangkok, Phuket, and Pattaya than at small local or rural eateries, where it can still seem unfamiliar.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Tipping is not customary and can confuse or embarrass staff, since good service is assumed rather than purchased."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% at restaurants is standard and expected; leaving nothing is seen as a serious insult."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"A service charge is included by law, so only a small amount of extra change for great service is needed."}],"sources":[{"title":"Etiquette 101: When and How Much to Tip in Thailand","url":"https://theculturetrip.com/articles/etiquette-101-when-and-how-much-to-tip-in-thailand","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Tipping in Thailand: Essential Guide to Tipping Culture","url":"https://radicalstorage.com/travel/tipping-in-thailand/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Tipping in Thailand","url":"https://www.thethailandlife.com/tipping-in-thailand","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Modern approval, old insult","confidence":"low","tip":"Use the thumbs-up casually with younger people, tourism staff, or close contacts, but rely on a smile or a wai instead in formal settings or with older, more traditional Thais.","explanation":"Traditionally in Thailand the thumbs-up was an obscene, derogatory gesture roughly equivalent to the middle finger, and it's still sometimes exchanged by children as a mild taunt. Younger, urbanized Thais and many in tourism and hospitality now use it the same way Westerners do, as casual approval or 'good job,' but the older insulting meaning hasn't fully disappeared. Sources genuinely disagree on how settled this shift is, so it's safest to treat the gesture as situational rather than a safe default.","regional_note":"The positive, Western-style meaning is most established among younger, urban Thais; it's more likely to be read as childish or confusing in traditional or rural settings.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"The thumbs-up is a standard, widely used positive approval gesture."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"A raised thumb alone is used to count 'one,' so holding one up to order a drink can cause mix-ups, though it isn't offensive."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"The thumbs-up is a classic, unambiguous sign of approval or agreement."}],"sources":[{"title":"Your Complete Guide To Thai Gestures","url":"https://theculturetrip.com/asia/thailand/articles/your-complete-guide-to-thai-gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Is thumbs up rude in Thailand?","url":"https://www.calendar-uk.co.uk/frequently-asked-questions/is-thumbs-up-rude-in-thailand","kind":"community"},{"title":"Module 2: Awareness of ASEAN Cultures","url":"https://www.stou.ac.th/schools/sla/modules/module2/presentation2.html","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Highly disrespectful gesture","confidence":"high","tip":"Sit with your feet tucked behind you or flat on the floor, and never use your foot to point at people or objects.","explanation":"In Thailand the feet are seen as the lowest and least clean part of the body, both literally and spiritually. Pointing your soles at a person, a Buddha image, or a portrait of the king is a serious sign of disrespect.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Putting your feet up is casual and carries no spiritual meaning."},{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Feet are also considered impure; touching someone with your foot warrants an apology."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Less charged, but pointing feet at someone is still seen as ill-mannered."}],"sources":[{"title":"Feet Low, Head High: A Guide to Thai Etiquettes","url":"https://thailandfoundation.or.th/?p=518467","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Thai Culture: Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/thai-culture/thai-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Must-Know Cultural Etiquette in Thailand: 15 Do's and Don'ts","url":"https://www.theinvisibletourist.com/etiquette-in-thailand/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Use your right hand","confidence":"medium","tip":"Use your right hand for anything eaten by hand or passed to someone, but don't worry about which hand holds your fork — spoon in the right, fork in the left, is standard Thai table manners.","explanation":"The left hand is traditionally considered unclean in Thailand, so food eaten directly by hand (like sticky rice) and anything passed or received, such as money, should use the right hand. This mainly matters when eating without utensils; at an ordinary meal Thais conventionally hold the fork in the left hand and the spoon in the right, so using your left hand to push food onto the spoon is completely normal.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Eating with the left hand is considered highly impolite, as it is reserved for personal hygiene."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is reserved for hygiene and should not be used for eating or passing food."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Hand-eating is rare and chopstick use makes which hand you favor largely irrelevant."}],"sources":[{"title":"Thai Culture: Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/thai-culture/thai-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Thai Table Manners","url":"https://mythailand.blog/2018/12/13/thai-table-manners/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Bizarre dining etiquette rules around the world","url":"https://www.foxnews.com/travel/bizarre-dining-etiquette-rules-around-the-world","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Head touching strongly taboo","confidence":"high","tip":"Keep your hands away from anyone's head and hair, no matter how friendly the gesture feels to you — use a light touch on the arm or shoulder instead if you need their attention.","explanation":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body in Thailand, believed to be where the spirit resides, so touching or patting anyone's head, including a child's, is seen as deeply disrespectful. This applies regardless of hierarchy or age; even masseuses typically ask permission before touching a client's head. Close family touching a young child's head is the main accepted exception.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Touching the head is usually avoided out of respect, especially for elders."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Touching someone's head without permission is seen as invasive, since personal space is highly valued."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"A pat on a child's head is often read as affectionate, though doing it to an adult can seem condescending."}],"sources":[{"title":"Feet Low, Head High: A Guide to Thai Etiquettes","url":"https://thailandfoundation.or.th/?p=518467","kind":"reference"},{"title":"An Introduction to Thai Taboos and Cultural No-Nos","url":"https://www.centarahotelsresorts.com/journal/an-introduction-to-thai-taboos-and-cultural-no-nos","kind":"community"},{"title":"Thai Culture: Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/thai-culture/thai-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Context-dependent, genuinely mixed","confidence":"low","tip":"Make eye contact to show you're engaged, but break it naturally every so often rather than holding a fixed stare, especially with someone senior to you.","explanation":"Guidance on eye contact in Thai business culture is genuinely inconsistent: some sources describe steady eye contact as important for building trust, while others warn that sustained, intense eye contact can come across as a challenge, especially toward someone senior. The safer practice described across sources is attentive but softened eye contact — glancing away periodically rather than holding a fixed stare — particularly with elders or higher-ranking colleagues.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged eye contact can feel intimidating, so brief, softer eye contact is more common."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Steady eye contact is read as confidence and honesty in business settings."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Eye contact between men is fine but should be brief and less direct with women."}],"sources":[{"title":"Thai Culture: Communication","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/thai-culture/thai-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Thailand Cultural Considerations","url":"https://rivermate.com/guides/thailand/cultural-considerations","kind":"community"},{"title":"Understanding Business Etiquette: Thailand","url":"https://www.aetnainternational.com/en/health-wellness/destination-guides/guide-to-life-in-thailand/understanding-business-etiquette.html","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected before entering homes","confidence":"high","tip":"Look for a pile of shoes at the entrance as your cue, and take your shoes off even if your host tells you not to bother.","explanation":"Removing shoes before entering a Thai home is a strongly observed custom, rooted in keeping outdoor dirt off indoor floors (where people traditionally sit) and the broader belief that feet and footwear are 'low' and unclean. It is considered disrespectful to keep shoes on indoors even if a host says it's fine. The same rule commonly applies to temples and many small shops, cafes, and guesthouses.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoe removal is mandatory in most homes and traditional inns, with indoor slippers often provided."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal is optional and varies household to household."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"polite","note":"Taking off shoes at the door is standard household etiquette, especially to keep floors clean."}],"sources":[{"title":"7 Customs in Thailand You Must Know Before Visiting","url":"https://samujana.com/7-customs-in-thailand-you-must-know-before-visiting/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Thai Culture","url":"https://en.psu.ac.th/international/arts-and-culture/thai-culture","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Thai Customs, Beliefs and Habits that Baffle Visitors to Thailand","url":"https://www.expique.com/article/customs-and-habits-that-baffle-first-time-visitors-to-thailand/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine for objects, not people","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you need to indicate a person, tilt your chin toward them instead of pointing; for objects, an open hand is more polite than a single finger.","explanation":"Pointing a single index finger directly at a person in Thailand is seen as rude and accusatory, so Thais more often indicate a person by tilting their chin in that direction. Pointing at inanimate objects or animals is generally tolerated, though using the whole hand rather than one finger is considered more polite either way.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is considered impolite; an open hand is used instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing is generally acceptable in casual settings, though it can seem blunt in formal ones."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is considered impolite; Indonesians point with the thumb instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Your Complete Guide To Thai Gestures","url":"https://theculturetrip.com/asia/thailand/articles/your-complete-guide-to-thai-gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in Thailand","url":"https://www.frommers.com/destinations/thailand/in-depth/etiquette/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Thai Culture: Communication","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/thai-culture/thai-culture-communication","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Hand-holding okay, more is not","confidence":"high","tip":"Holding hands is generally fine, but save kissing and prolonged hugging for private spaces, and avoid any physical affection at all on temple grounds.","explanation":"Public affection beyond simple hand-holding is traditionally frowned upon in Thailand; kissing and hugging in public, even between married couples, can draw stares or social disapproval. Attitudes are loosening somewhat, especially among younger couples in Bangkok, where hand-holding or an arm around a partner is now common, but overt kissing or prolonged hugging in public remains discouraged almost everywhere, and temple grounds are stricter still.","regional_note":"Younger couples in Bangkok and other urban centers are increasingly open about hand-holding, but kissing or hugging in public is still discouraged nationwide, and temples hold a stricter standard than everyday public spaces.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Public kissing and affection between couples is widely accepted and normal."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"rude","note":"Public displays of affection are illegal and can result in fines or arrest."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"PDA is generally avoided in public, though hand-holding is increasingly accepted among young couples."}],"sources":[{"title":"A Tom and Dee Scolded on the BTS – A Breaking Point of Thai Norms","url":"https://8limbsus.com/blog/tom-dee-scolded-bts-breaking-point-thai-sexuality-norms","kind":"community"},{"title":"Etiquette in Thailand","url":"https://www.frommers.com/destinations/thailand/in-depth/etiquette/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"PDA in Thai Culture: 4 Dos and Don'ts","url":"https://pinkpangea.com/?p=15376","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Expected, but often tolerated","confidence":"medium","tip":"Aim to arrive on time or a few minutes early yourself, but stay relaxed and don't show irritation if your Thai counterparts or the meeting itself runs late.","explanation":"Punctuality is formally valued in Thai business culture, and arriving on time is seen as a sign of respect, especially expected of foreign visitors. In practice, Thai meetings often start late, and Thais themselves may run late due to 'kreng jai' — a reluctance to cut off a prior conversation or inconvenience someone else — usually without serious offense or professional consequence. Showing visible frustration about a late start is considered worse manners than the lateness itself.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is critical, and even a one-minute delay can be seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"Arriving 15-30 minutes late to informal plans is common and tolerated, though punctuality is more expected for formal business meetings."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"caution","note":"Meetings may start late due to flexible local scheduling, but foreign visitors are still expected to arrive on time."}],"sources":[{"title":"Thai Culture: Business Culture","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/thai-culture/thai-culture-business-culture","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Business Etiquette in Thailand","url":"https://reiseliv.innovasjonnorge.no/artikkel/business-etiquette-in-thailand","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Punctuality in Thailand – Why are Thais Late?","url":"https://mysakonnakhon.com/punctuality-in-thailand-why-are-thais-late","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Decline once, then accept","confidence":"high","tip":"If you don't want more food, decline gently the first time it's offered and expect to be asked again — or simply say you're vegetarian or allergic if that's genuinely the reason.","explanation":"Thai hospitality customs expect a guest to politely decline a first offer of food before accepting on a second offer, reflecting a generous host and a gracious guest rather than outright refusal. A flat, immediate 'no' can come across as rejecting the host's generosity, so soft excuses are preferred over direct refusal. Genuine reasons like allergies, vegetarianism, or religious restrictions are a clear exception and are generally respected without offense.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"It's more polite to accept a small portion or leave a little food on the plate than to flatly refuse."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing a host's food is often taken as a personal rejection of their cooking and hospitality."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Refusing food is common for dietary or religious reasons but should come with a polite explanation and thanks."}],"sources":[{"title":"Thai Protocol","url":"https://www.unthsc.edu/about-us/wp-content/uploads/sites/17/Thaiprotocol.docx","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in Thailand","url":"https://www.shenmantra.com/articles/thai-massage/etiquette-in-thailand","kind":"community"},{"title":"Thai Food Etiquette – Dos and Don'ts","url":"https://www.thailand-property.com/blog/thai-food-etiquette-dos-and-donts","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Reads as 'that's okay'","confidence":"medium","tip":"The OK sign is safe for casual use, but a smile, nod, or small wai is the more natural choice in formal or respectful contexts.","explanation":"In everyday Thai use, forming a circle with the thumb and index finger is understood straightforwardly as 'this is okay' or approval, much like in the US, and it doesn't carry an offensive meaning the way it does in Brazil or Turkey. It isn't a traditional Thai gesture and is less common than a smile, a nod, or a wai, so it can feel slightly out of place in more formal or hierarchical interactions.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The circle made with thumb and finger is considered an obscene gesture there."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"The same gesture can be read there as a vulgar insult."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"The gesture commonly means 'OK' or can represent money, since the circle shape suggests a coin."}],"sources":[{"title":"Your Complete Guide To Thai Gestures","url":"https://theculturetrip.com/asia/thailand/articles/your-complete-guide-to-thai-gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Module 2: Awareness of ASEAN Cultures","url":"https://www.stou.ac.th/schools/sla/modules/module2/presentation2.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Communicate Like a Native Using Thai Hand Gestures and More","url":"https://www.thaipod101.com/blog/2019/08/16/thai-body-gestures/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Mild slurping is fine","confidence":"low","tip":"It's fine to slurp a little while eating noodle soup, but keep the volume down, especially in a sit-down restaurant rather than a street stall.","explanation":"Noodle soups in Thailand are eaten with a spoon and fork, and some slurping is normal and not considered rude, but Thai dining style generally favors a quieter, more restrained manner than in Japan or China, where loud slurping is treated as a compliment. Vigorous, noisy slurping is more likely to read as a lack of polish than a serious breach of manners, especially outside casual street-food settings.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Loud slurping is considered a compliment to the chef and part of properly enjoying the noodles."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping is generally acceptable and seen as a sign of enjoyment, especially in casual settings."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"caution","note":"Slurping is usually avoided in formal dining but tolerated in casual settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"How to Eat Noodles: The Slurp, the Chilli and the Chopsticks","url":"https://www.sbs.com.au/food/article/how-to-eat-noodles-the-slurp-the-chilli-and-the-chopsticks/apd208xp8","kind":"reference"},{"title":"What Not to Do When Eating Noodles","url":"https://jetstar.com/th/en/inspiration/articles/how-to-eat-noodle-soup-in-asia","kind":"community"},{"title":"Thai Table Manners: Great Info That Will Have You Eating Thai-Style","url":"https://culinarylore.com/food-culture:thai-table-manners-great-info/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Not customary; wai instead","confidence":"high","tip":"Greet with a wai (palms together, slight bow, with a smile) and let the other person set the tone if anything more familiar is appropriate.","explanation":"The standard Thai greeting is the wai — palms pressed together with a slight bow — not a cheek kiss, and public physical greetings like kissing are generally not part of Thai custom even among acquaintances. Initiating a cheek kiss, especially across genders or with someone older, can feel intrusive or cause mild discomfort, though foreign visitors are usually given latitude for not knowing the norm.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"La bise, cheek kissing, is a standard greeting among friends and acquaintances."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Physical contact in greetings is generally avoided in favor of bowing, so a cheek kiss would feel out of place."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing is a common social greeting, though the number of kisses can vary by region."}],"sources":[{"title":"Folk Gesture: ไหว้ or Wai","url":"https://folklore.usc.edu/?p=70849","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in Thailand","url":"https://www.frommers.com/destinations/thailand/in-depth/etiquette/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Thai Culture","url":"https://en.psu.ac.th/international/arts-and-culture/thai-culture","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Use palm-down wave instead","confidence":"high","tip":"To call a server or get someone's attention, wave with your palm facing down rather than curling an upturned finger toward yourself.","explanation":"Beckoning someone with a curled index finger, palm up, is considered rude and aggressive in Thailand — it's associated with calling animals and can come across as an aggressive challenge. The respectful way to call someone over is a palm-down wave or scooping motion with the fingers, which can look backwards to Western eyes but is the normal, polite way to summon a server or get someone's attention.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with an upturned, curled finger is seen as rude and animal-like; a gentle palm-down wave is used instead."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"This gesture is considered highly offensive and can be taken as a serious insult or challenge."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"The curled-finger beckon is common in casual settings but can seem bossy or condescending in formal ones."}],"sources":[{"title":"6 Little Differences in Thailand","url":"https://www.peacecorps.gov/connect/blog/6-little-differences-in-thailand/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Thai Protocol","url":"https://www.unthsc.edu/about-us/wp-content/uploads/sites/17/Thaiprotocol.docx","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Thai Gestures Body Language","url":"https://www.thaiholidayguide.com/thai-gestures-body-language/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcomed but not obligatory","confidence":"high","tip":"Bring a modest, shareable gift (like quality sweets or a small souvenir from your home country), wrap it in gold or yellow rather than black, and hand it over with your right hand and a wai.","explanation":"Gift giving in Thai business settings is appreciated and helps build rapport, though it isn't strictly obligatory, especially at a first or casual meeting. Gifts should be modest rather than extravagant (to avoid any appearance of a bribe), wrapped in auspicious gold or yellow paper rather than black, and should avoid sharp objects like knives or items like handkerchiefs believed to carry bad luck. Presenting a gift with the right hand (or both hands) along with a wai is the polite form, and the recipient typically won't open it in front of you.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift giving is highly ritualized: use both hands, avoid white wrapping paper, and never give four of something."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts are often unnecessary in business; if given, keep them small and practical."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts should avoid alcohol, pork, or images of living beings in line with Islamic values, and should be given with the right hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Business Etiquette in Thailand","url":"https://reiseliv.innovasjonnorge.no/artikkel/business-etiquette-in-thailand","kind":"reference"},{"title":"International Business Etiquette: Thailand","url":"https://cyborlink.com/besite/thailand.htm","kind":"community"},{"title":"6 Business Etiquette Tips for Businesswomen in Thailand","url":"https://zafigo.com/travel/6-business-etiquette-tips-for-businesswomen-in-thailand/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"India":{"slug":"india","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Discretionary, not a legal charge","confidence":"high","tip":"If a 'service charge' is already on your bill, you can ask to have it removed since it's optional by law — then leave roughly 5-10% in cash if the service was good.","explanation":"Tipping in India is appreciated but entirely discretionary. In July 2022 the Central Consumer Protection Authority (CCPA) explicitly barred restaurants from adding a 'service charge' automatically or by default, and in March 2025 the Delhi High Court upheld those guidelines, ruling that service charges and tips are voluntary payments that cannot be made mandatory. Many bills still show a suggested service charge line, but customers are legally entitled to have it removed and tip separately (or not) instead.","regional_note":"Tipping is more customary in upscale or tourist-heavy restaurants in big cities like Mumbai and Delhi than in small local eateries, where it is rare.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is considered insulting as it implies the staff needs extra money; excellent service is the norm."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% at restaurants is a strong social expectation since many service workers rely on tips for income."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"A service charge is included in the bill (service compris) by law, so extra tipping is optional, though rounding up is appreciated."}],"sources":[{"title":"Delhi HC rules restaurants can't impose mandatory service charge in bills","url":"https://newsonair.gov.in/delhi-hc-rules-restaurants-cant-impose-mandatory-service-charge-in-bills","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Service Charge Is Voluntary Payment By Consumers, Can't Be Made Mandatory On Food Bills: Delhi High Court","url":"https://www.livelaw.in/amp/high-court/delhi-high-court/delhi-high-court-restaurant-service-charge-on-food-not-mandatory-287836","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Positive, widely understood gesture","confidence":"medium","tip":"A thumbs-up to show approval or agreement reads as positive in India in both casual and most everyday situations.","explanation":"The thumbs-up is a well-established positive gesture in India, roughly equivalent to the Western 'okay' or 'good job' sign — it even features on India's one-rupee coin and gave its name to the popular Thums Up cola brand. It is used casually across ages and most everyday contexts without being seen as rude, unlike in a handful of other countries where the same gesture is an insult.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"The thumbs-up is an insult in Iran, roughly equivalent to an obscene gesture."},{"country":"Afghanistan","verdict":"rude","note":"Considered offensive in Afghanistan, similar to its negative meaning in Iran and parts of the Middle East."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"polite","note":"Generally positive, especially among younger people; older Thais may prefer a wai greeting instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Thumb signal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thumb_signal","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Thums Up","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thums_Up","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Feet are treated as impure","confidence":"medium","tip":"Sit with your feet tucked under you or flat on the floor; if you accidentally touch someone or something sacred with your foot, a quick touch of your hand to that person/object followed by touching your own chest is a commonly understood apology gesture.","explanation":"Feet are treated as the least pure part of the body in Hindu-influenced Indian culture, reflected in the widespread custom of charan sparsh — younger people touching an elder's or guru's feet to receive a blessing, which underscores how much spiritual weight is placed on feet. By the same logic, carelessly pointing the soles of your feet at a person, an idol, or religious objects, or touching someone with your foot, reads as disrespectful.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The feet are considered the lowest part of the body; pointing them at people or sacred objects is a major insult."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the soles of your feet is considered offensive, as feet are seen as unclean."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Generally neutral; putting feet up on furniture may be seen as too casual rather than offensive."}],"sources":[{"title":"Pranama","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pranama","kind":"community"},{"title":"Blessing","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blessing","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"rude","label":"Avoid the left hand","confidence":"high","tip":"Eat, pass dishes, and hand over money or gifts with your right hand; it's fine to use the left hand only for things like holding your own glass or a serving spoon.","explanation":"The left hand is traditionally associated with personal hygiene (bathroom use) in India, so eating, passing food, or handing over money, gifts, or other items with it is considered unclean and disrespectful. The right hand is used for eating, giving, and receiving; the left hand is reserved for secondary tasks, like holding your own drink or a serving utensil.","regional_note":"The taboo is strongest in traditional, religious, and rural settings, and somewhat relaxed — though still broadly observed — among younger people in big cities.","also_check":[{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"rude","note":"The same left-hand taboo applies strongly when eating, greeting, or giving/receiving items."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"No distinction is made — eating or writing with the left hand is completely normal."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"caution","note":"Eating is typically done with a spoon in the right hand and a fork in the left, used only to push food onto the spoon."}],"sources":[{"title":"India Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/india-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Hygiene in Islam","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hygiene_in_Islam","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Blessing from elders; risky otherwise","confidence":"medium","tip":"Only touch someone's head if you're in an elder or parental role blessing a child or much younger relative; otherwise avoid it, even with kids you don't know well.","explanation":"In Hindu-influenced Indian culture, touch customs around the head run in both directions: a younger person touching an elder's or guru's feet (charan sparsh) is a request for blessing, and an elder placing a hand on a younger person's head in return is understood as giving a blessing (ashirvad) — this is normal and welcome. A casual head-pat from a peer, stranger, or anyone outside that elder role is a different matter and can come across as condescending, especially toward an adult.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body; touching it, even a child's, is a significant taboo regardless of who does it."},{"country":"China","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head is often seen as affectionate, but doing the same to an adult can come across as patronizing."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Patting someone on the head is generally seen as playful or affectionate, especially with children or close friends."}],"sources":[{"title":"Pranama","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pranama","kind":"community"},{"title":"Blessing","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blessing","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Context and hierarchy dependent","confidence":"low","tip":"Keep eye contact natural and moderate; don't force intense or prolonged eye contact with someone senior to you, and take cues from how your counterpart engages.","explanation":"India is generally described as a high-context, relationship-oriented culture where communication relies heavily on tone and nonverbal cues, and more reserved eye contact toward elders or superiors can be a sign of deference in some settings. Norms vary widely by region, generation, and whether you're in a traditional or Westernized urban professional environment, so there isn't one fixed rule, and direct eye contact between peers is generally normal and unremarkable.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can feel intimidating; brief, softer eye contact is preferred, especially toward superiors."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct eye contact is generally expected as a sign of honesty and confidence, including in business."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Eye contact norms differ by gender; prolonged eye contact between unrelated men and women is often avoided."}],"sources":[{"title":"High-context and low-context cultures","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High-context_and_low-context_cultures","kind":"community"},{"title":"Eye contact","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_contact","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected sign of respect","confidence":"high","tip":"If you see a shoe rack, slippers left at the door, or your host removing their own shoes, follow suit without waiting to be asked.","explanation":"Removing your shoes before entering a home is widely expected in India and seen as a sign of respect and cleanliness, and the same norm applies at temples and other religious sites. Most households will have a shoe rack or a spot of shoes near the entrance as a visual cue.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes before entering a home is mandatory, and house slippers are often provided."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal isn't a universal norm; it depends on the household, so it's best to ask or observe."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoes are removed before entering homes and temples, similar to India."}],"sources":[{"title":"India Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/india-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Foot","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foot","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Use chin or open hand instead","confidence":"medium","tip":"Gesture with an open hand or a slight chin-tilt to indicate a direction or person, rather than pointing with one finger.","explanation":"Pointing at a person with a single index finger is traditionally considered rude in India; many people instead point with the chin, an open hand, or the whole hand. Pointing with two fingers is sometimes reserved specifically for addressing someone considered inferior, underscoring why single-finger pointing reads as commanding rather than neutral.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is considered impolite; gesturing with an open hand is preferred."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is seen as impolite; the thumb is commonly used instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing with the index finger is common and unremarkable in most casual contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"Pointing","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointing","kind":"community"},{"title":"Index finger","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Index_finger","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Legal for adults, but frowned upon","confidence":"medium","tip":"Save hugging, kissing, or prolonged physical affection for private settings; hand-holding is increasingly tolerated among younger couples in large cities but is still best used with discretion.","explanation":"India's Supreme Court has clarified that kissing or hugging between consenting adults in public is legal, but a broad obscenity provision in the law (penalizing 'obscene acts' that cause annoyance) has historically been invoked against public affection, most famously in a 2007 case against actors Richard Gere and Shilpa Shetty over a cheek kiss (later overturned on appeal). Culturally, overt affection still draws disapproval or stares in many settings, especially outside big cities.","regional_note":"Attitudes are more relaxed among younger, urban Indians in cities like Mumbai, Delhi, or Bengaluru than in smaller towns and more conservative regions.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Public affection, including kissing, is common and socially accepted."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Public displays of affection are strictly discouraged and can draw legal or religious-police attention."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Public affection is uncommon; even hand-holding can feel too intimate in formal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Public display of affection","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_display_of_affection","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Proxemics / Personal space","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_space","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Some flexibility, but be on time","confidence":"medium","tip":"Arrive on time yourself even if your Indian counterparts run a little late, and send a quick message if you expect to be delayed.","explanation":"Punctuality is valued in Indian business culture, but meetings don't always start exactly on time, and some scholars classify South Asian business culture as relatively polychronic (more flexible about schedules), though that broad regional label is itself debated. International visitors are still expected to arrive on schedule themselves as a sign of professionalism, and turning up late without notice to a meeting with senior leaders or external clients can read as disrespectful.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is critical; arriving even a few minutes late is considered disrespectful."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Arriving 15-30 minutes late to social and some business meetings is common and generally accepted."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is seen as a sign of professionalism; lateness is viewed as unreliable."}],"sources":[{"title":"India Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/india-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Polychronicity","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polychronicity","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Hosts insist; outright refusal is awkward","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you're full or can't eat something, accept a small token portion or explain briefly rather than flatly saying no — it preserves the spirit of hospitality without you overeating.","explanation":"Hospitality in India is often summed up by the principle Atithi Devo Bhava ('the guest is God'), and hosts take real pride in feeding guests well, commonly offering food more than once as an expression of care. Flatly refusing everything can feel like rejecting that hospitality, but a polite decline — especially one explained by being full or having dietary needs — is generally understood rather than treated as a serious insult.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"A modest, polite refusal is acceptable, but hosts may insist once or twice before taking no for an answer."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food from a host, especially a home-cooked meal, is often seen as rejecting their hospitality and can cause real offense."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Politely declining food, especially for dietary reasons or because you're full, is generally accepted without offense."}],"sources":[{"title":"Atithi Devo Bhava","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atithi_Devo_Bhava","kind":"community"},{"title":"Hospitality","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hospitality","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Understood as positive or neutral","confidence":"medium","tip":"Using the OK hand sign to mean 'fine' or 'got it' is generally safe in India; if you want to be extra clear, you can also just say 'theek hai' (okay) or nod.","explanation":"The OK hand sign (thumb and index finger forming a circle) doesn't carry a documented negative or vulgar meaning in India, unlike in parts of the Middle East, the Mediterranean, or Brazil. The same hand shape appears in Indian corporate logos (including Piramal Group, Bharat Petroleum, and Adlabs Studios) and echoes the vitarka mudra, a teaching/explanation gesture from Buddhist and Hindu iconography, both of which point to a neutral-to-positive reading rather than an offensive one.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"Considered a highly offensive, obscene gesture."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Commonly understood to mean 'okay' or 'money' in a neutral, positive way."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"Can be read as a vulgar insult in some contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"OK sign","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OK_sign","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Vitarka mudra","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitarka_mudra","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"More tolerated than in the West","confidence":"low","tip":"Don't worry about a little noise while eating soup, chai, or noodles in casual settings, but keep chewing quiet and avoid exaggerated slurping at upscale or formal business meals.","explanation":"Slurping isn't the dining faux pas in India that it is under Western fine-dining norms — one etiquette comparison groups India with Korea as places where it's 'acceptable or even expected' to slurp soup or noodles, unlike Europe and North America where it's considered impolite. That said, loud chewing or other mouth noises are still seen as poor manners, and in upscale restaurants or formal business meals it's safer to eat quietly and at a moderate pace.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is a sign of enjoyment and appreciation for the dish, and is considered good manners."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is generally acceptable and can signal that you're enjoying the meal."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping is considered impolite and disruptive in most dining settings, especially formal ones."}],"sources":[{"title":"Table manners","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_manners","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Not a traditional greeting","confidence":"medium","tip":"Default to a handshake or namaste, and wait for the other person to initiate anything more familiar like a cheek kiss — especially with elders or across genders.","explanation":"Cheek kissing is not a traditional Indian greeting. General etiquette research describes it as 'largely uncommon' and potentially offensive in cultures shaped by Hindu or Buddhist tradition, and one well-known case even brought legal trouble for a public cheek kiss in India. Standard greetings are a handshake (in professional settings) or namaste (palms together with a slight bow); cheek kissing stays rare and situational, mostly among close friends in Westernized urban circles.","regional_note":"Younger, Westernized urban circles may occasionally cheek-kiss close friends, but it remains uncommon nationally.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"La bise (cheek kissing) is a standard greeting among friends and acquaintances."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Physical greetings are generally avoided in favor of bowing; cheek kissing would feel unexpectedly intimate."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing is a common social greeting, even among people meeting for the first time in informal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Cheek kissing","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheek_kissing","kind":"reference"},{"title":"India Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/india-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Use an open hand instead","confidence":"low","tip":"To call someone over, extend your hand with the palm facing down and bring your fingers in together, rather than curling a single finger toward yourself.","explanation":"Beckoning with a single curled index finger (the Western 'come here' gesture) is generally less common in India and much of Asia; a more typical polite way to call someone over is an open hand, palm facing down, with the fingers scratching inward together. No strong India-specific evidence was found that single-finger beckoning is a severe insult there the way it is in some neighboring cultures, but it can still come across as abrupt or commanding rather than respectful.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"The polite beckoning form uses a palm facing the recipient at head height; reversing it is considered impolite."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a curled finger is considered highly offensive and has historically been associated with calling animals."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Less taboo than in parts of Asia, but curling a single finger to beckon can still feel abrupt; an open-hand wave is more neutral."}],"sources":[{"title":"List of gestures","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Appreciated with a few key rules","confidence":"medium","tip":"Offer and receive gifts with your right hand or both hands, and skip leather goods or beef-based items for Hindu recipients, or alcohol for Muslim recipients.","explanation":"Gift-giving is a normal and appreciated part of Indian social and business life — sweets, chocolates, or something from your home country are safe choices. Gifts should be given and received with the right hand or both hands, never the left hand alone, and since cows are sacred to Hindus, leather items and anything containing beef should be avoided; alcohol is best avoided for Muslim recipients since it's prohibited in Islam.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized; gifts are presented and received with both hands."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Business gifts are less expected; if given, something small and practical is preferred."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Avoid alcohol or pork-related gifts, and anything that could be seen as implying a bribe."}],"sources":[{"title":"India Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/india-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Alcohol and Islam","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcohol_and_Islam","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"France":{"slug":"france","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Appreciated, not required","confidence":"high","tip":"Pay the bill as printed, and if service was good, just round up to the nearest euro or leave a couple of coins in cash.","explanation":"Restaurant bills in France legally include a service charge (service compris), so waitstaff are already paid for service and tipping is not expected or required. Rounding up the bill or leaving a few euros in cash for good service is a nice, purely optional gesture, not an obligation calculated as a percentage.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is unnecessary and can even confuse or embarrass staff; excellent service is the cultural default, not something paid extra for."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% at restaurants is the customary, near-mandatory norm since many service workers rely on tips for income."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"caution","note":"A 'coperto' (cover charge) is often added to the bill and is separate from tipping; tipping itself is optional, with a few euros appreciated but not expected."}],"sources":[{"title":"France Travel Facts — CIA World Factbook","url":"https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/about/archives/2023/countries/france/travel-facts","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Tipping in France: What You Need to Know About Service Compris","url":"https://tasteoffrancemag.com/trending/tipping-in-france-what-you-need-to-know-about-service-compris/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Generally positive gesture","confidence":"high","tip":"Use it freely with friends, service staff, or colleagues to show approval; in formal business settings, pair it with (or replace it with) a verbal acknowledgment.","explanation":"In France, a thumbs-up is a well-understood, positive sign of approval or 'good job,' the same as in most of North America and Western Europe. It's fine in casual and most everyday contexts, though a verbal response is more natural in very formal settings.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Greece","verdict":"rude","note":"The thumbs-up is widely considered an offensive, dismissive insult, roughly equivalent to the middle finger."},{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"The thumbs-up is traditionally seen as a vulgar gesture, similar in effect to showing the middle finger."},{"country":"Nigeria","verdict":"caution","note":"In some parts of Nigeria and West Africa, a thumbs-up can be read as rude or dismissive rather than purely positive."}],"sources":[{"title":"French Culture — Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/french-culture/french-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"The Common Gesture You Should Never Make At A French Restaurant","url":"https://www.tastingtable.com/1953001/never-make-ok-gesture-french-restaurant","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Keep feet off furniture","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep your feet on the floor in social, dining, and public-transport settings, and don't put them up on furniture — but don't worry about which way your soles happen to point.","explanation":"France has no taboo about the soles of the feet specifically — that belief comes from Thai, South Asian, and Middle Eastern cultures, where the foot is considered unclean. In France it's simply a matter of general manners: propping your feet on a table, chair, or the seat across from you (e.g. on a train) is seen as inconsiderate and a little crude, not a deep insult.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The feet are considered the lowest, most unclean part of the body; pointing the soles at someone or touching them with a foot is a serious insult."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the soles of your feet to someone is considered highly disrespectful, as the foot is viewed as unclean."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Putting feet up casually (e.g. on a coffee table at home) is generally unremarkable, though it depends on the setting and whose furniture it is."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Most Interesting Etiquette Facts from Around the World","url":"https://luxebeatmag.com/interesting-etiquette-facts-around-world","kind":"community"},{"title":"French Culture — Etiquette (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/french-culture/french-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"No taboo, but mind utensils","confidence":"high","tip":"Keep your fork in your left hand and knife in your right throughout the meal — don't switch the fork to your right hand to take bites, as Americans often do.","explanation":"France has no cultural taboo around the left hand — that association comes from South Asian and Islamic hygiene customs, not French ones. In fact, Continental (French) dining etiquette keeps the fork in the left hand and the knife in the right for the whole meal, including bites, rather than switching the fork to the right hand after cutting as is common in the US.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is considered unclean and reserved for hygiene tasks; always eat and pass food with the right hand."},{"country":"Egypt","verdict":"rude","note":"As in much of the Arab world, the left hand is seen as unclean; eating or handing food with it is frowned upon."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Chopstick etiquette is the main concern, not hand choice; left-handed use isn't stigmatized."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Knife And Fork Rule To Keep In Mind When Dining In Europe","url":"https://www.foodrepublic.com/1284236/knife-fork-rule-dining-europe-utensils","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Why Europeans Hold Forks Differently Than Americans","url":"https://www.aol.com/why-europeans-hold-forks-differently-131000984.html","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Overly familiar, not taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Avoid touching an adult's head; a hand on the shoulder or arm is the more normal, lower-risk way to show warmth among people who already know each other.","explanation":"France doesn't treat the head as sacred the way some Southeast and South Asian cultures do, so there's no religious or spiritual taboo against touching it. Still, French physical contact is generally reserved for people you already know well, so patting an adult's head — especially a stranger's — would read as condescending or overly familiar, even though it's common and affectionate with young children.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body; touching anyone's head, adult or child, is a serious taboo."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head can be affectionate, but touching an adult's head is generally avoided out of respect, especially in Hindu contexts."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting someone's head can come across as playful or condescending depending on context; it's best reserved for close friends or children."}],"sources":[{"title":"French Culture — Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/french-culture/french-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"French people don't hug","url":"https://thegoodlifefrance.com/french-people-dont-hug/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Signals sincerity and confidence","confidence":"high","tip":"Maintain steady eye contact during greetings and key points of conversation, but don't make it an unbroken stare — brief natural breaks are normal.","explanation":"Direct eye contact is expected in France and reads as sincerity, confidence, and engagement, both socially and in business. Avoiding it can come across as evasive or disinterested; French communication norms treat steady eye contact as a basic sign of respect for the person speaking.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can feel intense or disrespectful; briefer glances, especially toward superiors, are more comfortable."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"caution","note":"Sustained eye contact with elders or superiors can be seen as challenging; a more deferential, softer gaze is preferred in hierarchical settings."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct eye contact is generally read as honesty and attentiveness, similar to France."}],"sources":[{"title":"French Culture — Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/french-culture/french-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Cyborlink — International Business Etiquette: France","url":"https://cyborlink.com/besite/france.htm","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Shoes-on is the default","confidence":"medium","tip":"Default to keeping your shoes on unless your host asks you to remove them or you notice other shoes already lined up at the door.","explanation":"Keeping shoes on indoors is actually the French norm — hosts rarely ask guests to remove them, especially in a home you don't know well, and doing so unprompted can feel presumptuous. That said, many French people remove their own shoes inside their own home, so if you see a pile of shoes by the door, follow suit, and it's always fine to offer.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes before stepping up from the genkan entryway is mandatory, not optional."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes before entering a home is standard, basic etiquette, much like in Japan."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Generally not expected unless the host asks or the home clearly has a shoes-off household (e.g. light carpets, shoes piled at the door)."}],"sources":[{"title":"Society & Social Issues in France — Visiting a Home (Five Colleges)","url":"https://www.langmedia.fivecolleges.edu/resources/france/society-social-issues/visiting-home","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Removal of footwear indoors — France","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Removal_of_footwear_indoors","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Less polite, not taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"When indicating someone or something, use an open hand or a slight nod rather than a single pointed finger.","explanation":"Pointing with a single finger isn't a serious taboo in France, but it's seen as a bit brusque or childish. French etiquette favors gesturing with an open hand, or a subtle nod or chin lift, to indicate people or objects, especially in polite or formal company.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing at a person with the index finger is considered quite rude; gesturing with an open hand is the polite alternative."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Pointing is acceptable casually but can read as confrontational or rude in formal settings."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is considered offensive; the thumb or an open hand is used instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"French Culture — Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/french-culture/french-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Must-Know French Gestures and Body Language for Travelers","url":"https://www.polyglottistlanguageacademy.com/language-culture-travelling-blog/2025/6/21/must-know-french-gestures-and-body-language-for-travelers","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Relatively relaxed about PDA","confidence":"medium","tip":"Normal affection (hand-holding, a kiss hello or goodbye) is fine almost anywhere in France; save more intense displays for private moments, especially in smaller towns.","explanation":"France is one of the more relaxed European countries about public affection — holding hands, hugging, and kissing between couples are common and unremarkable, especially in cities like Paris. More explicit or prolonged intimacy draws more notice outside big cities or among older, more conservative generations.","regional_note":"Expect more tolerance in Paris and other large cities than in smaller, more traditional towns.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Public displays of affection are discouraged even between couples, as they're seen as disruptive to social harmony."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"polite","note":"Like France, Italy is generally relaxed about public affection, with hugging and kissing between couples seen as a natural expression."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Public affection between unmarried couples is illegal and strictly enforced, with serious legal consequences."}],"sources":[{"title":"Public displays of affection — Expatica","url":"https://www.expatica.com/living/love/public-displays-of-affection-108636","kind":"reference"},{"title":"French Culture — Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/french-culture/french-culture-communication","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Slightly late is tolerated","confidence":"high","tip":"Aim to arrive on time or a few minutes early, and send a quick message if you expect to be more than 10 minutes late.","explanation":"On-time arrival is expected and valued in French business culture, but a short delay of 5-10 minutes is usually tolerated, especially if you let someone know. In Paris specifically, up to about 15 minutes is sometimes excused due to traffic ('le quart d'heure parisien'). Beyond that without explanation can look disorganized or disrespectful, particularly with senior contacts.","regional_note":"Punctuality norms loosen somewhat further south in France, and arriving around 15 minutes late to a dinner party or social gathering is generally seen as fine, unlike for a business meeting.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Arriving even a few minutes late is seen as disrespectful; punctuality is treated as essential."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is strictly observed in business; lateness is viewed as poor planning and a lack of respect."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Arriving 15-30 minutes late to a meeting is common and generally expected, especially outside very formal corporate settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"The 15-minute rule: is it OK to be late in France?","url":"https://www.thelocal.fr/20251003/the-15-minute-rule-is-it-ok-to-be-late-in-france","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Building business relationships in France — Aetna International","url":"https://aetnainternational.com/en/individuals/destination-guides/movers-guide-to-france/building-business-relationships.html","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Polite decline is fine","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you need to decline, say so politely with a short reason ('je suis allergique,' 'je n'ai plus faim') rather than refusing silently or abruptly.","explanation":"French hosts put real effort into a meal and appreciate guests tasting what's offered, so refusing outright without any explanation can seem impolite, especially at a family gathering. But a polite decline with a brief reason — an allergy, a dietary restriction, or simply being full — using phrases like 'non merci' or 'je n'ai plus faim,' is completely normal and accepted, particularly once you've already had a portion.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"An initial polite refusal is often expected before accepting, as hosts frequently insist; outright acceptance on the first offer can seem eager."},{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food offered by a host, especially in a family setting, can be seen as rejecting their hospitality."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Declining is generally fine if explained politely, though it can seem impolite in more formal or traditional family settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Society & Social Issues in France — Visiting a Home (Five Colleges)","url":"https://www.langmedia.fivecolleges.edu/resources/france/society-social-issues/visiting-home","kind":"reference"},{"title":"How to Survive Your First French Dinner Party","url":"https://www.polyglottistlanguageacademy.com/language-culture-travelling-blog/2025/7/28/how-to-survive-your-first-french-dinner-party","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Can mean 'zero' or 'worthless'","confidence":"medium","tip":"Play it safe with a thumbs-up or a verbal 'd'accord'/'parfait' instead of the OK circle, especially with older French people or in service settings like restaurants.","explanation":"Traditionally in France, the OK circle gesture (thumb and forefinger forming an 'O') represents the number zero and can imply something — or someone — is worthless, not the positive 'okay'/'perfect' meaning it carries in North America. That said, decades of American media exposure mean many French people, especially younger ones, also recognize the US meaning, so interpretation can depend on who you're gesturing to.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The OK sign is considered a vulgar, offensive gesture, roughly equivalent to an obscene insult."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture has historically been used as a derogatory, anti-gay insult and is widely considered offensive."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"The gesture commonly means 'money' or 'OK' and is generally understood positively in everyday use."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Common Gesture You Should Never Make At A French Restaurant","url":"https://www.tastingtable.com/1953001/never-make-ok-gesture-french-restaurant","kind":"reference"},{"title":"10 Common Gestures Easily Misunderstood Abroad — Matador Network","url":"https://matadornetwork.com/read/hand-signs-meaning/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"French Culture — Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/french-culture/french-culture-communication","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"rude","label":"Eat quietly, no slurping","confidence":"high","tip":"Twirl noodles discreetly onto your fork (using a spoon to help, if offered) and eat without audible suction, the same way you'd eat pasta.","explanation":"French table manners place strong emphasis on eating quietly and with restraint, so slurping is considered uncouth across casual bistros and formal restaurants alike. This applies to noodles as it does to soup or any dish — audible eating noises are seen as a lack of self-control at the table, not a compliment to the cook.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is a sign of enjoyment and also helps cool hot noodles while eating."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping is generally acceptable and can signal enjoyment of the meal, though norms vary by region and setting."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta is considered poor manners; it should be eaten quietly, twirled onto a fork."}],"sources":[{"title":"French dining etiquette — Novotel/Accor","url":"https://novotel.accor.com/a/en/great-escape-magazine/food-and-beverage/french-dining-etiquette.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"27 Do's and Don'ts at the French Table","url":"https://www.frenchtoday.com/?p=30690","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Standard greeting, count varies","confidence":"high","tip":"Start with the right cheek and let the other person set the pace and count — two kisses is the safest default if you're unsure.","explanation":"La bise (cheek-kissing) is the standard social greeting among friends, family, and acquaintances across France — it's not used for first business introductions, where a handshake is the norm instead. The tricky part is the number of kisses: most of the country does two, but some regions do one, three, or four, so it's best to follow the other person's lead.","regional_note":"Crowdsourced regional surveys (such as the 'Combien de bises' poll) map real variation: two kisses is standard across most of France, three is common in parts of the Occitanie/Auvergne area, four persists among older residents in parts of eastern Brittany, and Brittany's Finistère region is one of the few areas where a single kiss is the norm.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Physical greetings like cheek-kissing aren't customary; a bow is the norm, though this is a difference in custom rather than an offense."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek-kissing is also common as a greeting, though the number of kisses and which cheek to start with varies by region."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek-kissing is generally reserved for close friends and family; a handshake is the safer default for acquaintances and business contacts."}],"sources":[{"title":"La bise: Who to kiss in France, how many times and on which cheek — The Local","url":"https://www.thelocal.fr/20191029/la-bise-who-to-kiss-in-france-how-many-times-and-on-which-cheek","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Combien de bises (crowdsourced regional kiss-count survey)","url":"https://www.combiendebises.free.fr","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Seen as brusque, not a grave insult","confidence":"medium","tip":"To call someone over, use a palm-down hand wave or just say their name rather than curling a single finger toward yourself.","explanation":"Curling your index finger to beckon someone is seen in France as a bit brusque, reminiscent of summoning a waiter, child, or animal. It's considered impolite, especially toward someone you should treat with respect, but it isn't a severe social taboo. A palm-down wave or simply calling the person's name is the more common, polite alternative.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a curled index finger is considered quite rude and is associated with calling animals; a palm-down wave of all fingers is used instead."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"A curled-finger beckon is strongly associated with calling animals and is considered a serious insult toward a person."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Often fine among friends in casual settings, but can come across as condescending or aggressive in formal or professional contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"French Culture — Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/french-culture/french-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Must-Know French Gestures and Body Language for Travelers","url":"https://www.polyglottistlanguageacademy.com/language-culture-travelling-blog/2025/6/21/must-know-french-gestures-and-body-language-for-travelers","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Rare in business, handle with care","confidence":"medium","tip":"Save any gift for after a relationship is established rather than a first meeting, and lean toward books, music, or a quality item from your home region rather than wine.","explanation":"Business gift-giving isn't a strong French tradition — it's uncommon outside the December holiday season, and giving something at a first meeting can look like you're trying to curry favor. If you do give a gift (for example, after a deal closes), keep it modest and tasteful; books or music tied to your contact's interests are safer choices than wine, since the French take pride in their own wine and may not welcome a foreign bottle.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is an important business ritual; careful presentation and wrapping matter as much as the gift itself."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts in formal business settings can seem unnecessary or even raise suspicion; a small token after a successful deal is more acceptable."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is common and appreciated, but alcohol should be avoided and gifts should be given and received with the right hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Business Etiquette Around the World: France — On Call International","url":"https://blog.oncallinternational.com/?p=4259","kind":"reference"},{"title":"A Guide to Gift Giving and Etiquette in France","url":"https://www.annieandre.com/a-guide-to-gift-giving-and-etiquette-in-france/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Germany":{"slug":"germany","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Appreciated, not obligatory","confidence":"high","tip":"When paying in cash, round up or add about 10% and tell the server the total you want to pay while saying 'Stimmt so' ('keep the change').","explanation":"Tipping (Trinkgeld) in Germany is a polite way to reward good service but is never mandatory, since staff earn a regular wage and service is already built into menu prices. Most diners round the bill up or add roughly 5-10%, handing the cash directly to the server rather than leaving it on the table.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is considered unnecessary and can even seem insulting, since excellent service is already the baseline expectation."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is the norm and socially expected, since many servers rely on tips to make up a large share of their income."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"A service charge is included by law, but rounding up or leaving small change for good service is still a common courtesy."}],"sources":[{"title":"How to tip in Germany - allaboutberlin.com","url":"https://allaboutberlin.com/guides/tipping","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Tipping In Germany - smartergerman.com","url":"https://go.smartergerman.com/blog/tipping-in-germany/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Tipping in Berlin - berlinwalk.com","url":"https://www.berlinwalk.com/post/tipping-in-berlin","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Generally positive gesture","confidence":"high","tip":"Use it freely for casual agreement, but in very formal settings a nod or spoken confirmation comes across as more professional.","explanation":"A thumbs-up is widely understood in Germany as a friendly, informal signal of approval or agreement, just as in most Western countries, and it's used comfortably in everyday life and casual business settings. One quirk: Germans also raise the thumb to mean 'one' when counting on their fingers, so it can briefly read as a number rather than approval out of context.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture is considered a serious obscene insult, roughly equivalent to showing the middle finger."},{"country":"Greece","verdict":"caution","note":"Among older generations it can be read as a vulgar insult similar to 'up yours,' though younger Greeks mostly take it as 'OK' today."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Thumbs-up is a universally positive, casual gesture with no real risk of offense."}],"sources":[{"title":"8 Key German Gestures - FluentU","url":"https://www.fluentu.com/blog/german/german-gestures/","kind":"community"},{"title":"German Culture - Communication - Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/german-culture/german-culture-communication","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Impolite, not taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep both feet on the floor when sitting in company, especially in formal settings, and avoid resting your shoes on tables or chairs.","explanation":"Pointing the soles of your feet at someone or propping your feet on furniture reads as careless or disrespectful in Germany, more a matter of posture and consideration for shared spaces than any belief about feet being unclean. It is a far milder issue than in cultures where feet carry a strong symbolic taboo.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"Feet are considered the lowest and most impure part of the body, so pointing them at people or at Buddha images is a serious offense."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Feet on furniture is often fine casually at home among family, but is considered inconsiderate in someone else's home or in formal settings."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the soles of your feet is highly insulting, since feet are regarded as unclean and the gesture conveys contempt."}],"sources":[{"title":"Navigating German Etiquette: What Gestures Are Rude in Germany? - en.fmyly.com","url":"https://en.fmyly.com/article/what-gestures-are-rude-in-germany/","kind":"community"},{"title":"German Culture - Etiquette - Cultural Atlas","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/german-culture/german-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Left hand is fine","confidence":"high","tip":"Keep your fork in your left hand and knife in your right throughout the meal rather than switching hands American-style, and keep both hands visible above the table.","explanation":"There is no stigma around the left hand in German dining. German table manners follow the Continental style, where the fork stays in the left hand and the knife in the right for the whole meal, unlike the American habit of switching utensils between hands. The left-hand taboo found in parts of South Asia, the Middle East, and Africa simply doesn't apply in Germany.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is traditionally reserved for hygiene tasks, so eating or passing food with it is considered unclean."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is seen as impure, so using it to eat or share food, especially from a communal dish, is disrespectful."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"caution","note":"In Muslim-majority areas the left hand is considered unsuitable for eating, though attitudes are more relaxed in secular, urban settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Oh fork: The German dining etiquette Americans might struggle with - The Local","url":"https://www.thelocal.com/20191016/oh-fork-the-joys-and-shocks-of-german-dining-customs-as-an-american","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Germany Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/germany-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Overly familiar, not taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Stick to a handshake or, with people you know well, a brief touch on the arm or shoulder - skip patting an adult's head altogether.","explanation":"Germans keep a reserved physical distance and generally reserve touch for people they know well, so an unsolicited head-pat on an adult acquaintance or stranger comes across as patronizing or overly familiar rather than a strict cultural taboo. It's treated like other uninvited touching - awkward and presumptuous, not a serious insult.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body, so touching it, even on a child, is a serious breach of etiquette."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head is often affectionate, but doing so to an adult, especially an elder, can be seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Head-patting is generally reserved for children or close friends; doing it to an unfamiliar adult can seem patronizing."}],"sources":[{"title":"German Culture - Communication - Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/german-culture/german-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"8 Key German Gestures - FluentU","url":"https://www.fluentu.com/blog/german/german-gestures/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected and valued","confidence":"high","tip":"Hold steady eye contact when greeting, speaking, and especially the instant your glasses touch during a toast.","explanation":"Germans treat direct eye contact as a sign of honesty, confidence, and engagement, and it is expected both in everyday conversation and in business settings; looking away can read as evasive or disinterested. The custom is especially strict when clinking glasses for a toast ('Prost') - breaking eye contact at that moment is considered rude, and a well-known superstition warns it brings seven years of bad luck in your love life.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can feel intimidating or confrontational; briefer glances, especially toward superiors, are preferred."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"caution","note":"Sustained eye contact with elders or superiors can be seen as a challenge to hierarchy; a more lowered gaze is often expected."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct eye contact is similarly valued as a sign of honesty and engagement, though intensity expectations vary by region."}],"sources":[{"title":"German Culture - Communication - Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/german-culture/german-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Eye Contact Following a Toast in Germany - USC Digital Folklore Archive","url":"https://folklore.usc.edu/eye-contact-following-a-toast-in-germany/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Prost! Why do Germans make eye contact when they clink glasses? - The Local","url":"https://www.thelocal.de/20180404/why-do-germans-look-each-other-in-the-eye-when-they-clink-glasses","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Follow your host's lead","confidence":"medium","tip":"If unsure, ask your host 'Soll ich die Schuhe ausziehen?' ('Should I take my shoes off?') when you arrive, or bring a clean pair of socks just in case.","explanation":"Many Germans remove their shoes at home and offer guests 'Hausschuhe' (house slippers), particularly in households with carpets, wood floors, or young children, but it isn't a strict universal rule and some hosts keep shoes on for more formal visits. The safest approach is to watch what your host does, or simply ask.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes indoors is mandatory, with dedicated house slippers and often separate ones for the bathroom."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal varies widely by household and region; it's often optional unless the host asks."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoes are routinely removed at the door, with slippers commonly offered to guests."}],"sources":[{"title":"The GW Expat Blog - german-way.com","url":"https://german-way.com/?p=11437","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Why We Don't Wear Shoes in Our Home - ourgabledhome.com","url":"https://ourgabledhome.com/why-we-dont-wear-shoes-in-our-home/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Avoid pointing at people","confidence":"medium","tip":"When indicating a specific person, gesture with an open hand rather than a single pointed finger.","explanation":"Sources vary on how strongly it registers, but there is general agreement that jabbing an index finger at a specific person reads as accusatory or rude in Germany, similar to broader European norms; using an open hand to indicate someone is the safer choice. Pointing at objects or directions with the index finger is generally unremarkable.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger at a person is considered very impolite; an open hand or slight bow is used instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Pointing at people is common and rarely called out, but it's still seen as a bit brusque or impolite in more formal settings."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is considered offensive; people point with the thumb or an open hand instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"German Culture - Communication - Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/german-culture/german-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"German hand gestures explained - Lingoda","url":"https://www.lingoda.com/blog/en/german-gestures/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine in moderation","confidence":"high","tip":"Keep physical affection brief and low-key in public, and avoid it altogether at work or in business settings.","explanation":"Holding hands, hugging, or a brief kiss between couples is normal and accepted in German public life, but Germans are generally more reserved than Mediterranean cultures and view prolonged or intense displays as inappropriate, especially in professional settings. Workplaces in particular expect a clear line between affection and professional conduct.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Public displays of affection are discouraged and can embarrass onlookers in most everyday settings."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Kissing as a greeting and moderate affection between couples in public are common and socially accepted."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Public affection between unmarried couples is culturally and legally unacceptable."}],"sources":[{"title":"Public displays of affection - Expatica","url":"https://www.expatica.com/living/love/public-displays-of-affection-108636","kind":"reference"},{"title":"German Culture - Communication - Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/german-culture/german-culture-communication","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"rude","label":"Punctuality is paramount","confidence":"high","tip":"Aim to arrive 5-10 minutes early, and if you expect any delay, call or message ahead with an apology and your updated arrival time.","explanation":"In German business culture, punctuality is a core sign of respect and professionalism; arriving late to a meeting without warning is seen as discourteous and suggests poor planning or disregard for others' time. Arriving a few minutes early is well regarded, and meetings tend to start and end on schedule.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"Arriving 15-30 minutes late to a business meeting is often tolerated and sometimes expected, as relationship-building is valued alongside punctuality."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is taken extremely seriously; arriving late is a serious breach that can damage trust and business relationships."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Meetings may start later than scheduled due to hospitality norms and prayer times, but foreign visitors arriving on time is still seen as respectful."}],"sources":[{"title":"Germany Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/germany-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"German business culture and etiquette - WeAreDevelopers","url":"https://wearedevelopers.com/magazine/german-business-culture-and-etiquette","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"A clear 'no' is respected","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you don't want more food, say so plainly once - there's no need for an elaborate excuse, since a direct 'no' is taken seriously rather than as modesty.","explanation":"Germans are direct communicators, so a clear, polite decline - ideally with a short reason like being full - is generally respected and taken at face value; hosts typically won't keep pressing you to eat more, unlike in some hospitality-insistent cultures. A vague or hesitant 'no' offered just to seem polite can backfire, since it may be read as your real answer and nothing more will be offered.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"A polite refusal out of modesty is common, but hosts may still insist once or twice before accepting it."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing home-cooked food can be taken personally, since it's seen as rejecting the host's hospitality and effort."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Hosts often insist multiple times before accepting a refusal, though stated dietary restrictions are generally respected."}],"sources":[{"title":"German Culture - Etiquette - Cultural Atlas","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/german-culture/german-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Germany Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/germany-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Can read as a vulgar insult","confidence":"medium","tip":"Avoid the OK sign with older Germans or in formal settings; a thumbs-up or a verbal 'ja, passt' is a safer way to signal agreement.","explanation":"The thumb-and-index-finger circle can be read in Germany as a crude insult rather than approval - an older, still-recognized usage - though many younger Germans now understand it as the international 'OK' sign through social media and English-language influence. Because interpretation splits by generation and context, it shouldn't be assumed to read as simple agreement.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture is a well-known vulgar insult and should be avoided entirely."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"It is often read as a crude insult implying homosexuality, which carries strong offense in many social contexts."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Understood as 'OK' and historically also as a symbol for money; not considered offensive."}],"sources":[{"title":"German Culture - Communication - Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/german-culture/german-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Common hand gestures to avoid abroad - Babbel","url":"https://www.babbel.com/en/magazine/common-hand-gestures-to-avoid-abroad","kind":"community"},{"title":"8 Key German Gestures - FluentU","url":"https://www.fluentu.com/blog/german/german-gestures/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"rude","label":"Avoid loud slurping","confidence":"medium","tip":"Twirl noodles onto your fork and sip soup quietly from the spoon rather than straight from the bowl.","explanation":"German table manners favor quiet, controlled eating, so audibly slurping noodles or soup reads as a lapse in manners rather than the sign of enjoyment it can be in East Asian dining culture. It stands out most in formal settings or restaurants.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is normal and can signal enjoyment of the meal rather than poor manners."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is common and generally unremarkable, especially in casual settings."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Audibly slurping pasta is considered a clear breach of table manners."}],"sources":[{"title":"Study Abroad Etiquette: Germany - OnCall International","url":"https://blog.oncallinternational.com/?p=6143","kind":"community"},{"title":"Table manners around the world - Lingoda","url":"https://www.lingoda.com/blog/en/table-manners-across-the-world/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Handshake is the norm","confidence":"medium","tip":"Offer a handshake by default, and let the other person initiate a cheek kiss or hug if that's how they prefer to greet you.","explanation":"A firm handshake is the standard German greeting, used both at first meetings and in ongoing professional relationships. Cheek kissing does happen - usually one or two kisses - but mainly among close friends and family, or more commonly in the southwest near the French border; it isn't a default greeting, so it's best to follow the other person's lead.","regional_note":"In the southwest, near the French border, two-cheek-kiss greetings are somewhat more common than in the rest of Germany.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing ('la bise') is the standard greeting among friends and acquaintances, with the number of kisses varying by region."},{"country":"United Kingdom","verdict":"caution","note":"A single cheek kiss is common among friends, but a handshake is the safer choice for first meetings or formal settings."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Physical greetings like cheek kissing are unusual and can cause visible discomfort; a bow is the traditional and expected greeting."}],"sources":[{"title":"Germany Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/germany-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"German Culture - Greetings - Cultural Atlas","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/German-culture/German-culture-greetings","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Can seem brusque or rude","confidence":"medium","tip":"Wave with an open hand, palm toward the person, or just say their name, instead of curling a single finger to beckon them.","explanation":"Curling a single finger to call someone over can come across as brusque, commanding, or rude in Germany - more like summoning a pet than a person - but it isn't the severe taboo it is in parts of Asia. Germans more commonly wave with an open hand or simply call out to the person.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a single curled finger is considered very rude; an open hand with the palm down and fingers waved together is used instead."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a finger is seen as highly offensive and is even historically associated with calling animals; an open-hand, palm-down wave is the polite alternative."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Common among friends, but it can come across as rude or dismissive in formal settings; a verbal invitation is safer."}],"sources":[{"title":"Traditional Gestures Around The World That Mean The Opposite In The US - WorldAtlas","url":"https://www.worldatlas.com/articles/traditional-gestures-around-the-world-that-mean-the-opposite-in-the-us.html","kind":"community"},{"title":"Common hand gestures to avoid abroad - Babbel","url":"https://www.babbel.com/en/magazine/common-hand-gestures-to-avoid-abroad","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Welcome, but mind the rules","confidence":"high","tip":"If bringing flowers, pick an odd number, skip red roses, and remove the wrapping paper before handing them to your host.","explanation":"When invited to a German home, bringing a small gift such as an odd number of flowers (unwrapped before handing them over) or a bottle of wine is customary and appreciated - but avoid red roses, which signal romantic interest, and skip flowers like lilies or chrysanthemums that are associated with funerals. In business settings, gifts should stay modest and practical, since anything lavish can raise concerns about bribery under Germany's strict anti-corruption rules.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is a deeply ingrained business ritual where presentation and reciprocity matter, often exchanged at the first meeting."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts are welcome but should respect Islamic customs - avoid alcohol or pork products and offer with the right hand."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Business gifts are common but usually kept modest in value to stay within typical corporate anti-bribery policies."}],"sources":[{"title":"Germany Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/germany-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Flowers as a gift: do's and don'ts - Regions Florist","url":"https://www.regionsflorist.de/en/blog/flowers-as-a-gift-dos-and-donts","kind":"reference"},{"title":"German Gift Giving Etiquette: What You Need to Know - BM Magazine","url":"https://bmmagazine.co.uk/business/german-gift-giving-etiquette-what-you-need-to-know/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Italy":{"slug":"italy","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Optional, rounding up only","confidence":"medium","tip":"Check the bill for 'coperto' or 'servizio' lines, and if neither is shown, leave a few euros in cash for good service rather than a percentage tip.","explanation":"Tipping is not obligatory in Italian restaurants; the bill usually includes a small per-person 'coperto' (a cover charge for bread, table setting, and your seat) and sometimes a separate 'servizio' (an actual service charge), which already covers service when present. When no service charge is listed and you received good service, Italians typically just round up the bill or leave a couple of euros in cash rather than calculating a percentage.","regional_note":"Tourist-heavy cities like Rome, Venice, and Florence see more service charges and tipping than small towns.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is considered insulting; excellent service is the norm without extra payment."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is standard and expected for restaurant service staff."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"Service is included by law, but leaving small change for good service is appreciated."}],"sources":[{"title":"Explained: The etiquette around paying your restaurant bill in Italy","url":"https://www.thelocal.it/20240612/explained-the-etiquette-around-paying-your-restaurant-bill-in-italy","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Italian Tipping Etiquette: Why You Mostly Shouldn't, and When You Should","url":"https://migaku.com/blog/language-fun/italian-tipping-etiquette-why-you-mostly-shouldnt-and-when-you-should","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Positive and widely understood","confidence":"high","tip":"Use it freely in casual situations, but opt for a verbal 'perfetto' or 'va bene' in formal business settings.","explanation":"The thumbs-up is understood across Italy as a positive, informal sign of approval or agreement, much like in most Western countries. It's commonly used among friends and in casual settings, though it's best reserved for informal contexts rather than very formal business meetings.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Greece","verdict":"rude","note":"Thumbs-up is widely cited as an insult similar to a raised middle finger."},{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"Thumbs-up is considered an obscene gesture in Iran, similar to showing the middle finger."},{"country":"Nigeria","verdict":"rude","note":"Thumbs-up can be viewed as rude or insulting in parts of Nigeria."}],"sources":[{"title":"Thumb signal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thumb_signal","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Is thumbs up offensive in Italy?","url":"https://lacocinadegisele.com/knowledgebase/is-thumbs-up-offensive-in-italy","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Keep feet off furniture","confidence":"low","tip":"Keep your feet on the floor and avoid resting your shoes on chairs, tables, or sofas, especially in someone's home.","explanation":"There's no documented Italian superstition specifically about showing the soles of your feet (that taboo is strongest in Thailand and parts of the Middle East). The real sensitivity in Italy is more about hygiene: resting shoed feet on furniture, tables, or seats is seen as inconsiderate because street shoes are considered dirty.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the soles of your feet is extremely offensive, as the foot is considered the lowest and dirtiest part of the body."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Casual foot placement is generally acceptable in most social settings."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the soles of your feet or pointing them at someone is a grave insult."}],"sources":[{"title":"Unspoken Etiquette Rules To Know Before Striking Up A Conversation With Locals In Italy","url":"https://www.islands.com/1602684/tips-for-starting-conversations-with-locals-in-italy/","kind":"community"},{"title":"My Italian habits that foreigners just don't get","url":"https://www.thelocal.com/20150410/my-italian-habits-that-foreigners-just-dont-get","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"No left-hand taboo exists","confidence":"high","tip":"Eat Continental-style with the fork in your left hand and knife in your right — switching hands repeatedly looks more unusual than left-hand use itself.","explanation":"Italy has no taboo against the left hand; in fact, Continental European table manners keep the fork in the left hand (knife in the right) throughout the entire meal, unlike the American style of switching hands. The old Western stigma against left-handedness itself (once called 'the devil's hand') was about forcing children to write right-handed, not about food hygiene like the toilet-related taboo found in parts of South Asia or the Middle East.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Eating with the left hand is widely seen as rude; the left hand is reserved for bathroom hygiene."},{"country":"Morocco","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is considered unclean; eat with the right hand, especially from shared dishes."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"No stigma against left-handed eating; left-handedness is fully accepted at the table."}],"sources":[{"title":"Table manners","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_manners","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Italian Superstitions","url":"https://www.theintrepidguide.com/italian-superstitions/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Overly familiar, not rude","confidence":"medium","tip":"Save head-pats for children or very close family, and stick to a handshake or light arm touch with everyone else.","explanation":"Patting an adult's head in Italy isn't a strong taboo, but it can read as patronizing or overly familiar outside of close family or affectionate contexts with children. Italians are warm and tactile with people they know well, but unsolicited touching — especially to the head — can make someone uncomfortable if you're not close.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred body part in Thai culture, and touching it is a major taboo."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting an adult's head is generally seen as disrespectful, though it may be fine for close family or children."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"A light pat can be friendly among close acquaintances but may feel overly familiar otherwise."}],"sources":[{"title":"Italy","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Italy","kind":"community"},{"title":"Personal space","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_space","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Signals sincerity and confidence","confidence":"high","tip":"Hold a steady, friendly gaze during greetings and conversation, without staring fixedly.","explanation":"Direct eye contact is valued in Italy as a sign of sincerity, confidence, and engagement, especially during greetings and business meetings. It's standard practice during introductions and conversations, and avoiding it can come across as evasive or disinterested.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can be seen as intimidating; brief glances are preferred."},{"country":"Finland","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct eye contact is valued as a sign of honesty, typically less intense than in Italy."},{"country":"Nigeria","verdict":"caution","note":"Sustained eye contact with a superior may be seen as challenging; juniors often lower their gaze."}],"sources":[{"title":"Italy Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/italy-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Business Culture Around the World: Italy","url":"https://www.skyminder.com/blog/analysis-studies/business-culture-around-the-world-italy/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Not required, follow host","confidence":"medium","tip":"Glance at the entryway for a shoe rack, or ask 'Devo togliere le scarpe?' (should I take off my shoes?) when you arrive.","explanation":"There's no single nationwide rule — many Italians ask guests to remove shoes at the door to keep floors clean, since street shoes are seen as dirty, while others don't mind. It's safest to follow your host's lead rather than assuming either way.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes before entering the genkan (entryway) is mandatory and deeply ingrained."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal varies by household; it's often seen as informal or hygienic but not expected."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes is common and often expected in private homes."}],"sources":[{"title":"Unspoken Etiquette Rules To Know Before Striking Up A Conversation With Locals In Italy","url":"https://www.islands.com/1602684/tips-for-starting-conversations-with-locals-in-italy/","kind":"community"},{"title":"blogs.rollins.edu (Italy culture post)","url":"https://blogs.rollins.edu/letters/?p=20333","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Can seem brusque or rude","confidence":"medium","tip":"When indicating a person, use an open hand or a nod rather than a single pointed finger.","explanation":"Pointing directly at a person with your index finger can come across as brusque or accusatory in Italy, similar to the general Western norm, though it's far from a severe taboo. Italians are expressive with their hands and commonly gesture with an open palm or a nod instead when indicating people or directions.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with a finger is considered rude; an open hand is used instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Generally acceptable in casual contexts, though considered slightly informal."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is offensive; the thumb or an open hand is used instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Pointing","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointing","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Italy","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Italy","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Subtle affection is fine","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep it to hand-holding or a brief kiss in public, and save anything more intimate for private settings.","explanation":"Mild public affection — holding hands, a hug, or a quick kiss — is common and accepted in Italy, much like in most of Western Europe. More passionate or prolonged displays can draw disapproving looks in conservative settings or around family, more because Italians value composure in public than because of any strict rule against affection itself.","regional_note":"Older generations and smaller, more traditional towns in the south tend to be less comfortable with public affection than younger people in big northern cities.","also_check":[{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"rude","note":"Passionate public affection can still violate public decency laws and lead to fines, even after 2020 reforms loosened rules on cohabitation and alcohol."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek-kiss greetings and light PDA are common and socially accepted."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Public affection beyond hand-holding is uncommon and can draw stares, though it's seen as awkward rather than offensive."}],"sources":[{"title":"Public display of affection","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_display_of_affection","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Italy","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Italy","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Slight delay tolerated","confidence":"medium","tip":"Aim to arrive on time, but don't panic over a short, unannounced delay — a brief apology on arrival is usually enough.","explanation":"Italian business culture values relationships and flexibility, so being a few minutes late to a meeting is generally tolerated and not seen as a serious breach, especially once rapport is established. That said, punctuality is still respected, particularly in northern business centers, and habitual lateness without notice can hurt your credibility.","regional_note":"Flexibility around time tends to be more pronounced in the south than in business-focused northern cities like Milan.","also_check":[{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is a strict cultural norm; even a few minutes late is seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Arriving late is a serious breach of etiquette; being early is expected."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"A 15-30 minute delay is common and often expected in business meetings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Italy Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/italy-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Business Culture Around the World: Italy","url":"https://www.skyminder.com/blog/analysis-studies/business-culture-around-the-world-italy/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"rude","label":"Seen as rejecting hospitality","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you must decline, say something like 'Ho già mangiato, ma sembra delizioso' (I've already eaten, but it looks delicious) rather than just saying no.","explanation":"Offering food is a core expression of Italian hospitality, and declining it — especially an invitation to share a meal — can genuinely come across as rejecting someone's generosity. It's customary to at least accept a small portion or taste what's offered; if you must refuse, do it warmly and with a clear reason rather than a flat no.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"A polite refusal with a brief explanation is acceptable and won't be seen as rejecting hospitality outright."},{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food, especially in a family setting, is often seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"A gracious refusal with compliments is acceptable in formal settings, though still slightly impolite in family contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"Italy","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Italy","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Positive, means okay or perfect","confidence":"medium","tip":"Use the OK sign naturally to confirm agreement or approval — it won't cause offense the way it might in Turkey or Brazil.","explanation":"The OK hand sign is understood positively in Italy, meaning 'fine,' 'perfect,' or agreement, with no vulgar connotation of the kind found in some neighboring Mediterranean countries. It's used comfortably in casual, dining, and business contexts alike.","regional_note":"In Naples it also has an old folk meaning: an upturned circle once symbolized love or marriage, a downturned one justice, though this is largely historical trivia today.","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The OK sign is considered vulgar, resembling an obscene gesture."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"It can be read as a homophobic insult or vulgar reference."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"In the south it can mean 'zero' or 'worthless'; the positive 'OK' meaning is more common in the north."}],"sources":[{"title":"OK gesture","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OK_gesture","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Quiet eating is preferred","confidence":"low","tip":"Twirl pasta neatly on your fork and eat without excessive noise, especially in a restaurant or someone's home.","explanation":"Italy doesn't have a culture of celebratory noodle-slurping the way Japan or China does; quiet, unhurried eating is the general norm at the table, similar to most Western dining etiquette. Loud slurping of pasta would likely draw a glance in a formal setting, but it's a minor faux pas rather than a serious offense.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is a sign of appreciation and considered normal."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is common and shows enjoyment of the meal."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping and loud eating are generally considered impolite."}],"sources":[{"title":"Table manners","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_manners","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Italy","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Italy","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Standard greeting among friends","confidence":"medium","tip":"Let the other person lead and follow their direction — regional habits vary, and some areas or individuals reverse the order.","explanation":"Two light cheek kisses are a standard greeting among friends, family, and acquaintances in Italy, typically starting by leaning your head to the right so your cheek meets the other person's left cheek first, then switching sides. It's reserved for people you know, not strangers or formal business introductions, where a handshake is standard.","regional_note":"Three kisses sometimes occur in parts of the south, and cheek-kissing between male friends or relatives is common in southern and central Italy.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing isn't customary; a bow or handshake is standard, and unexpected contact can feel awkward."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing is common among friends and acquaintances, with the number of kisses varying by region."},{"country":"Finland","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing is rare; a handshake or nod is the standard greeting even at social gatherings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Italy (Culture and identity section)","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Italy","kind":"community"},{"title":"Cheek kissing","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheek_kissing","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Informal, not a grave insult","confidence":"low","tip":"With people you don't know well, say 'vieni qui' or wave with an open hand rather than curling a single finger.","explanation":"The palm-up, finger-curling beckon is common in Italy and the wider West; it can read as a bit abrupt or overly casual, especially toward a stranger, elder, or server, but there's no evidence it carries the severe 'calling an animal' insult it does in parts of Asia. It's best avoided with people you don't know well or in formal situations.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a curled finger is considered vulgar and animal-like; a gentle wave with fingers down is used instead."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"This gesture is used only for calling animals and can be seen as a serious insult."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Common in casual settings, but can seem condescending or aggressive in formal or professional contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"Beckoning sign","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beckoning_sign","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Italy","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Italy","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcome gesture, mind flowers","confidence":"medium","tip":"For flowers, choose a cheerful variety other than chrysanthemums and avoid bundles of 17.","explanation":"Gift-giving is a welcome, friendly gesture in Italy as long as it's modest rather than ostentatious — in business settings, an overly expensive gift can feel like a bribe, while quality wine, food, or elegant small items are well received. If giving flowers, avoid chrysanthemums, which are strongly associated with funerals and cemeteries, and skip the number 17, considered unlucky in Italy much like 13 elsewhere.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized; careful wrapping and presentation matter as much as the gift itself."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts are rarely given at initial business meetings; a small token is more appropriate once a relationship is established."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"polite","note":"Gifts are welcome, but avoid alcohol or pig-related items and present with the right hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Italy Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/italy-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Chrysanthemum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrysanthemum","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Italian Superstitions","url":"https://www.theintrepidguide.com/italian-superstitions/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"United Arab Emirates":{"slug":"united-arab-emirates","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Appreciated, not obligatory","confidence":"medium","tip":"Check the bill for an existing service charge, then leave about 10-15% in cash directly to staff if you want it to reach them.","explanation":"Tipping is not legally required in the UAE and many restaurant bills already include a service charge, but leaving roughly 10-15% in cash for good service is common and appreciated, especially in Dubai and Abu Dhabi's international dining scene. Because the service charge printed on a bill doesn't always reach the staff directly, many diners tip in cash even when one is listed.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Tipping is not part of local custom and can confuse or mildly embarrass staff, since excellent service is already the norm."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is expected and forms a real part of server income, not an optional extra."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Service is included by law, but rounding up or leaving small change for good service is still a polite gesture."}],"sources":[{"title":"Tipping in Dubai","url":"https://visitdubai.com/cs/articles/tipping-in-dubai","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Taxes, service charges and tipping","url":"https://visitabudhabi.ae/en/plan-your-trip/essential-info/taxes-service-charges-and-tipping","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Riskier than it looks","confidence":"low","tip":"In business settings or with older Emiratis, use a verbal thank-you or a nod instead of a thumbs-up.","explanation":"Western visitors often treat a thumbs-up as universally positive, but established UAE business-etiquette guidance specifically warns against it (grouped with crossing your legs) as a gesture that can cause offence. At the same time, decades of Western media exposure mean many Emiratis, especially younger ones, now read it as simply 'good' or 'OK', so the real-world reaction is genuinely inconsistent.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"The thumbs-up is widely considered an obscene insult, roughly equivalent to the middle finger elsewhere."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"polite","note":"The gesture generally reads as positive, though it carries no special emphasis the way a wai does."},{"country":"Greece","verdict":"caution","note":"Older Greeks in particular may read an abrupt thumbs-up as closer to a rude dismissal than a compliment."}],"sources":[{"title":"Culture and etiquette tips when doing business in the UAE","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/blog/culture-etiquette-tips-when-doing-business-in-the-uae","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in Dubai – Important Tips","url":"https://www.dhowcruiseindubai.com/blog/?p=170","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Feet should stay down","confidence":"high","tip":"Keep both feet flat on the floor or tucked beneath you, and avoid resting an ankle on a knee in a way that points your sole at anyone.","explanation":"Across the Gulf, the sole of the foot is viewed as the dirtiest part of the body because it touches the ground, so deliberately pointing it at someone is read as a strong insult. This is a regional cultural custom rather than an Islamic legal rule, but it is taken seriously in Emirati homes, majlis seating, and any formal gathering where people sit on low cushions or face each other directly.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The feet are considered the lowest and most impure part of the body, so pointing them at a person or a Buddha image is a serious taboo."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Deliberately showing the soles of your feet, especially on tatami mats or in temples, is seen as careless and disrespectful."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Showing the soles of your feet is generally neutral and only seems informal in unusually formal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"What behaviours are rude in the Middle East","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/blog/what-behaviours-are-rude-in-the-middle-east","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Ask Ali: Why it is rude to show the soles of your feet","url":"https://www.thenationalnews.com/arts-culture/ask-ali-why-it-is-rude-to-show-the-soles-of-your-feet-1.295633/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"rude","label":"Right hand for eating","confidence":"high","tip":"Eat, pass dishes, and accept food with your right hand, even if you're left-handed.","explanation":"The left hand is traditionally reserved for personal hygiene and the right hand for eating, greeting, and handling food — a distinction rooted in Islamic practice that is widely observed in the UAE, including by many non-Muslim residents in mixed settings. Eating or passing food with the left hand in front of others is considered impolite, though practical exceptions are made for people who cannot use their right hand.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is reserved for toilet hygiene, so eating or passing food with it is considered unclean."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"There is no stigma around which hand you use; chopstick technique matters far more than left- or right-handedness."},{"country":"United Kingdom","verdict":"polite","note":"Left-handed eating carries no cultural stigma at all."}],"sources":[{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Why Left-Handed Eating Is Frowned Upon In The Middle East","url":"https://www.foodrepublic.com/1573656/why-left-handed-eating-frowned-upon-middle-east","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine for kids, not adults","confidence":"medium","tip":"Reserve head-patting for children you know, and use a handshake or a warm word instead with adults.","explanation":"There is no basis for treating the head as 'the seat of the soul' in Islamic teaching — in fact, a well-known hadith encourages gently stroking an orphan's head as an act of kindness, so the gesture toward children is generally read as affectionate rather than offensive. Toward an adult, especially an elder or someone you don't know well, patting the head comes across as condescending or overly familiar rather than a religious violation.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body, so touching anyone's head, child or adult, is a serious breach of etiquette."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head is often affectionate, but touching an adult's head, especially by a stranger, reads as condescending."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Patting a child's head is usually read as friendly, though doing it to an adult can still seem patronizing."}],"sources":[{"title":"Hadith on Orphans: Be kind to orphans for a soft heart","url":"https://www.abuaminaelias.com/dailyhadithonline/2019/12/15/be-kind-to-orphans/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Sunnah.com – Kindness, Mercy and Compassion Towards Children","url":"https://sunnah.com/adab/19","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Confident, not with everyone","confidence":"medium","tip":"Hold steady eye contact with someone of your own gender, but keep it brief and polite when speaking with the opposite gender in a business setting.","explanation":"Among people of the same gender, steady eye contact in the UAE is read as sincere and engaged, consistent with the warm, direct greeting style described in Emirati hospitality customs. Between men and women who are not closely related, however, prolonged eye contact can be seen as overly forward, so a more measured gaze is the safer default in mixed-gender business settings.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Sustained eye contact can feel confrontational; brief, softer eye contact is the norm."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Steady eye contact is expected in business as a signal of confidence and honesty."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged eye contact with someone senior or older can be read as a challenge rather than respect."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greeting | Abu Dhabi Business Culture","url":"https://visitabudhabi.ae/en/abu-dhabi-convention-and-exhibition-bureau/business-culture/greeting","kind":"reference"},{"title":"A Handshake is Worth 1000 Words: Body Language in Dubai Business Culture","url":"https://www.arabicpod101.com/blog/2019/02/14/body-language-in-dubai-business-culture/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected in most homes","confidence":"medium","tip":"Watch what your host and other guests do at the entrance, and remove your shoes if you see a shoe rack or pile of shoes.","explanation":"Removing your shoes before entering a home is a widely observed sign of respect and cleanliness in the UAE, tied to Emirati hospitality customs and the practice of sitting and dining on floor seating. It is not a universal legal or religious requirement, so the safest approach is simply to follow your host's lead at the door.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoe removal before entering a home is mandatory and extends to many traditional inns and some restaurants."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal is optional and depends entirely on the individual household's preference."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes at the door is standard in most homes, and hosts often provide guest slippers."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greeting | Abu Dhabi Business Culture","url":"https://visitabudhabi.ae/en/abu-dhabi-convention-and-exhibition-bureau/business-culture/greeting","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Countries That Always Take Their Shoes Off Inside","url":"https://dailypassport.com/countries-always-take-shoes-off-inside/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Use your whole hand instead","confidence":"medium","tip":"Indicate a person or direction with your whole hand rather than a single pointed finger.","explanation":"Pointing directly at a person with a single index finger is widely described as impolite across Arab countries, including the UAE, because it can come across as accusatory. The more common and accepted alternative is to gesture with the whole open hand toward the person or direction.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with a single finger is considered impolite; gesturing with the whole hand is preferred."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Index-finger pointing is considered offensive; many Indonesians instead point with the thumb."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing with the index finger is common and mostly unremarkable in casual settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Common Gestures People Use in Arab Countries","url":"https://www.theculturetrip.com/asia/united-arab-emirates/articles/11-common-gestures-people-use-in-arab-countries","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Legal risk, not just taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Save kissing, hugging, or extended hand-holding for private settings, and don't assume tourist zones are exempt from the law.","explanation":"Kissing or hugging in public can be prosecuted in the UAE as an offence against public decency — a British couple was jailed in 2010 after being convicted of kissing in a Dubai restaurant — though enforcement is inconsistent and married couples holding hands is generally tolerated. Unmarried couples carry the most legal exposure, and norms are visibly more relaxed inside international hotels and licensed venues than in public spaces.","regional_note":"Sharjah enforces stricter public-decency and dress rules than Dubai, including a ban on alcohol, which makes PDA there a higher-risk choice.","also_check":[{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Public displays of affection are explicitly listed as a finable offence under the 2019 Public Decency Regulations."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek-kissing as a greeting and casual affection in public are widely accepted."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Public affection is uncommon and can draw uncomfortable looks, even among young couples."}],"sources":[{"title":"British kissing couple loses jail appeal in Dubai","url":"https://gulfnews.com/uae/crime/british-kissing-couple-loses-jail-appeal-in-dubai-1.607664","kind":"reference"},{"title":"What expect when moving from Dubai to Sharjah","url":"https://www.bayut.com/mybayut/what-expect-moving-from-dubai-to-sharjah","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Locals flexible, you shouldn't be","confidence":"medium","tip":"Arrive on time yourself even if you expect to wait, and don't show irritation if your host is delayed.","explanation":"Gulf business culture tends to be relaxed about strict scheduling, and it's common for Emirati counterparts to run a little late or extend small talk and coffee before getting to business. Foreign visitors, however, are still expected to arrive on time themselves as a sign of respect, since the relaxed local pace doesn't extend the same way to guests.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is taken very seriously, and arriving even a few minutes late is considered disrespectful."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Strict punctuality is the norm, and lateness is viewed as unprofessional."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Arriving 15-30 minutes late to a business meeting is common and generally unremarkable."}],"sources":[{"title":"Culture and etiquette tips when doing business in the UAE","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/blog/culture-etiquette-tips-when-doing-business-in-the-uae","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Six adjustments Western bankers need to make when working in the Gulf","url":"https://www.efinancialcareers.com/news/2010/07/six-adjustments-western-bankers-need-to-make-when-working-in-the-gulf","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"rude","label":"Accept at least a little","confidence":"high","tip":"Accept at least a small portion or a sip of whatever is offered before politely declining more.","explanation":"Hospitality is central to Emirati culture, and refusing food or drink offered by a host is considered impolite — business etiquette guidance for the UAE states outright that an offered drink should always be accepted. Declining outright, especially on a first offer, can come across as rejecting the host's generosity.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"A polite decline is acceptable if done with a brief apology, though hosts may still insist once or twice."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food from a host is seen as rejecting their hospitality and effort."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"An initial refusal is expected to be followed by insistence from the host; accepting eventually is the polite norm."}],"sources":[{"title":"Culture and etiquette tips when doing business in the UAE","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/blog/culture-etiquette-tips-when-doing-business-in-the-uae","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Greeting | Abu Dhabi Business Culture","url":"https://visitabudhabi.ae/en/abu-dhabi-convention-and-exhibition-bureau/business-culture/greeting","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Not safely 'OK' here","confidence":"low","tip":"Use a verbal 'great' or a simple nod of approval instead of the OK sign.","explanation":"The Western 'all good' meaning of the OK sign doesn't reliably carry over to the Gulf: general references on obscene gestures note it carries vulgar or offensive connotations in parts of the Middle East, and at least one UAE-focused etiquette source describes it locally as evoking the 'evil eye' rather than approval. Because interpretations vary by generation and audience, and solid country-specific evidence is thin, treat it as genuinely unpredictable rather than safe.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture is considered a serious obscene insult."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"It commonly signals 'money' or 'OK', though context still matters in very formal settings."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"It can mean 'zero' or 'worthless' in some contexts rather than straightforward approval."}],"sources":[{"title":"Obscene gesture","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obscene_gesture","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in Dubai – Important Tips","url":"https://www.dhowcruiseindubai.com/blog/?p=170","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"No specific rule, stay modest","confidence":"low","tip":"If you're eating noodles at a restaurant, favor quiet bites over loud slurping, since restrained table manners are generally valued.","explanation":"Noodles are not part of traditional Emirati cuisine, and no source documents a specific Gulf rule about slurping them. General Gulf dining etiquette favors restrained, quiet eating over loud table noises, so audible slurping in a restaurant would likely read as a minor lapse in manners rather than a serious offence.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is read as a compliment to the chef and is expected in ramen shops."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping is common and generally read as enjoying the food, though less so in very formal settings."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta is considered a clear breach of table manners."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greeting | Abu Dhabi Business Culture","url":"https://visitabudhabi.ae/en/abu-dhabi-convention-and-exhibition-bureau/business-culture/greeting","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Arab Table Manners: Business Lunch Etiquette in Arabic Countries","url":"https://www.arabicpod101.com/blog/2018/11/27/arab-table-manners/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Same gender yes, cross-gender no","confidence":"medium","tip":"With someone of the same gender who greets you warmly, mirror their lead; with the opposite gender, default to a verbal greeting and a nod unless they initiate physical contact.","explanation":"Warm greetings, including handshakes, embraces, and cheek-kissing among close friends or relatives, are common between people of the same gender in the UAE, reflecting Bedouin hospitality traditions. Greeting someone of the opposite gender this way is different: unless they are a close relative, it's best to wait and see whether they extend a hand or cheek first.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek-kissing (la bise) is a standard greeting between friends and acquaintances of any gender, with the number of kisses varying by region."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Physical greetings like cheek-kissing are not customary; a bow is the expected greeting instead."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek-kissing is common among the same gender, but cross-gender physical greetings in public are avoided."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greeting | Abu Dhabi Business Culture","url":"https://visitabudhabi.ae/en/abu-dhabi-convention-and-exhibition-bureau/business-culture/greeting","kind":"reference"},{"title":"A Handshake is Worth 1000 Words: Body Language in Dubai Business Culture","url":"https://www.arabicpod101.com/blog/2019/02/14/body-language-in-dubai-business-culture/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Seen as a gesture for animals","confidence":"high","tip":"To call someone over, turn your palm face-down and wave your fingers toward yourself rather than curling one finger upward.","explanation":"Beckoning with a single raised finger is seen across much of the Arab world, including the UAE, as a gesture used to call animals or servants, which makes it demeaning when directed at a person. The accepted alternative is to turn your palm face-down and make a gentle scooping motion with your fingers toward yourself.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"The same upright-finger beckon is used only for animals and is considered very rude directed at a person."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"A single raised finger to beckon is seen as crude; a palm-down wave is the polite alternative."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"The gesture isn't taboo but can come across as condescending in a formal setting."}],"sources":[{"title":"Traditional Gestures Around The World That Mean The Opposite In The US","url":"https://www.worldatlas.com/articles/traditional-gestures-around-the-world-that-mean-the-opposite-in-the-us.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcome, with a few rules","confidence":"medium","tip":"Offer the gift with your right hand or both hands, and don't be offended if it isn't opened in front of you.","explanation":"Gift-giving is welcomed in UAE business and social settings as long as it follows a few consistent rules: avoid alcohol, pork products, or imagery of dogs, and give with the right hand or both hands together rather than the left. Common, safe choices are dates, chocolates, or pastries, and gifts are typically opened in private rather than in front of the giver.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized; sets of four are avoided, and gifts are presented with both hands."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Business gifts are often unnecessary and should stay small and practical if given at all."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Similar rules around alcohol, pork, and hand use apply, but gift-giving is less common at a first meeting."}],"sources":[{"title":"Gift Giving Etiquette in the Middle East","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/blog/gift-giving-in-the-middle-east","kind":"reference"},{"title":"UAE Customs & Etiquette Sheet","url":"https://www.wtca.org/news/uae-customs-etiquette-sheet","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Turkey":{"slug":"turkey","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Appreciated, roughly 10%","confidence":"high","tip":"Leave around 10% in cash on the table or hand it to the server directly, and check the bill first for an already-included service charge.","explanation":"Tipping (bahşiş) is discretionary in Turkey but widely practiced to reward good service, with about 10% being typical at restaurants (more at upscale places). Some restaurants already add a service charge, in which case extra tipping isn't required, and cash is preferred since card tips don't always reach staff.","regional_note":"Tipping is lighter and more informal (rounding up the bill) outside tourist areas, while in hubs like Istanbul and Antalya closer to 10-15% is expected.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is considered insulting as it implies the staff needs extra money; excellent service is the standard."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is expected for service staff, as wages are often below minimum."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"A service charge is included by law, but leaving small change is polite; overtipping can seem showy."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkey — Wikivoyage travel guide (Tipping section)","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkey","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Do You Tip In Turkey? Yes, Here Is Your Tipping Etiquette Guide","url":"https://www.chasingthedonkey.com/tipping-in-turkey-how-to-tip-in-turkey/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Tipping in Turkey: Who, How Much and When (Complete Guide)","url":"https://myturkeyadventure.com/blog/tipping-in-turkey-complete-guide","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Generally positive, not taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Thumbs-up is safe to use in everyday and business settings in Turkey; it's understood the same positive way as in the West.","explanation":"Although some round-ups claim thumbs-up is an offensive 'phallic' gesture across a broadly defined 'Middle East,' Turkey-specific sources that do catalogue real taboo gestures (the OK sign, the fig gesture) never flag thumbs-up, and it's commonly used and read positively in Turkey, much like in the West.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Greece","verdict":"rude","note":"Thumbs-up is a severe insult, roughly equivalent to a raised middle finger."},{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"Considered an obscene gesture, similar to the middle finger in Western cultures."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Thumbs-up means 'good' and is read positively, though less used for 'okay' than in the West."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkish Culture: Communication","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/turkish-culture/turkish-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Not bad intentions, it's just etiquette in Turkey","url":"https://www.dailysabah.com/life/2015/01/07/not-bad-intentions-its-just-etiquette-in-turkey","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Gestures — Wikivoyage","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Disrespectful foot display","confidence":"high","tip":"Keep your feet flat on the floor or crossed at the ankle, and never point the sole of your foot or shoe toward another person.","explanation":"Showing the sole of your foot or shoe to someone in Turkey is a real insult, especially while seated in a home, restaurant, or coffee shop — it implies the other person is beneath you. Locals avoid pointing their feet at others or propping shoes up where soles are visible.","regional_note":"The taboo is strongest in traditional homes and religious settings; very casual gatherings among close friends may be more relaxed.","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The feet are the lowest and most impure part of the body; pointing them at someone or a Buddha image is a serious offense."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the sole of your foot is a grave insult implying the person is beneath you."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Putting feet up or showing soles is generally casual and acceptable informally, though it can seem rude in formal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Customs and etiquette in Turkey","url":"https://www.roughguides.com/destinations/europe/turkey/culture-etiquette/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkish cultural taboos","url":"https://www.crownrelo.com/article/turkish-cultural-taboos","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkey — Wikivoyage travel guide","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkey","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Real custom, weaker taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Use your right hand for shared dishes and bread to be safe, but don't worry about it if you're left-handed eating your own plate in casual, secular settings.","explanation":"Turkey's Directorate of Religious Affairs (Diyanet) has publicly discouraged eating with the left hand on religious grounds, and many Turks favor the right hand for bread and shared dishes. But a 2018 Diyanet statement calling left-handed eaters 'demons' sparked public mockery and criticism, showing the custom is more contested than the stronger taboo seen in Gulf countries, and secular/urban Turks follow it less strictly.","regional_note":"The custom is observed most strictly in religious and rural households and is often relaxed in secular, urban circles.","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is considered unclean and reserved for bathroom use; eat with the right hand."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"No strong taboo against the left hand; chopstick etiquette matters far more."},{"country":"Morocco","verdict":"rude","note":"Eating with the left hand is also considered unclean; use the right hand, especially from communal dishes."}],"sources":[{"title":"Demons use their left hand to eat: Turkey's top religious body","url":"https://www.hurriyetdailynews.com/demons-use-their-left-hand-to-eat-turkeys-top-religious-body-126760","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkey's religious chiefs brand left-handed eaters as demons","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2018/02/05/turkey-s-religious-chiefs-brand-left-handed-eaters-as-demons-","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkish Culture: Etiquette","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/turkish-culture/turkish-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkey: religious affairs officials serve as distraction tool","url":"https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2018/02/turkey-religious-affairs-officials-serve-as-distraction-tool.html","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Can feel condescending to adults","confidence":"medium","tip":"Save head-patting for children or close family, and greet adults with a handshake instead.","explanation":"Patting an adult on the head in Turkey isn't a strict sacred-body taboo like in Buddhist Southeast Asia — Turkey and the wider Middle East are actually a 'high-contact' culture where casual touch between acquaintances is common. The real issue is that patting an adult's head can feel condescending or overly familiar, especially from a stranger; general guidance is to avoid touching people without clear rapport, particularly women.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the highest and most sacred part of the body; touching it is a major taboo."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting the head is generally reserved for children or close family; doing so to an adult may seem patronizing."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Patting someone on the head is often seen as friendly, especially among friends or family."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkey — Wikivoyage travel guide (Respect section)","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkey","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Haptic communication","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haptic_communication","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Expected, but not prolonged","confidence":"medium","tip":"Offer natural, direct eye contact when greeting or talking with Turks, but don't force it with someone of the opposite gender who seems to be avoiding it.","explanation":"Direct eye contact is expected in Turkish greetings and conversation as a sign of sincerity and engagement, and Turks often hold eye contact during serious discussions. However, devout or conservative individuals may look away from someone of the opposite gender out of modesty, and women may avoid eye contact with unfamiliar men to discourage unwanted attention.","regional_note":"This modesty-based eye-contact avoidance is most common among devout or conservative Turks and far less of a factor in secular, urban, mixed-gender settings.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged eye contact can feel confrontational or intimidating; brief glances are preferred."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct, sustained eye contact is expected as a sign of honesty and engagement."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Eye contact between men is common, but between men and women it's typically brief or avoided out of modesty."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkish Culture: Communication","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/turkish-culture/turkish-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkey — Business Etiquette, Manners, and Culture","url":"https://www.cyborlink.com/besite/turkey.htm","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkish Cultural Gestures & Body Language","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/turkish-cultural-gestures-body-language-realistic","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Respectful indoor custom","confidence":"high","tip":"Take your shoes off at the door without being asked, and do the same before entering any mosque.","explanation":"Removing your shoes before entering a Turkish home is standard practice and a sign of respect and cleanliness — hosts often provide slippers. Mosques also require all visitors to remove their shoes at the entrance, where shelves or bags are typically provided.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes is mandatory, usually at a genkan entryway, with slippers provided for indoor use."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal isn't universal; it depends on the household, so it's best to follow the host's lead."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoes are removed before entering homes and many temples as a sign of respect and cleanliness."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkey — Wikivoyage travel guide (Respect section)","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkey","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Mosque manners — Customs and etiquette in Turkey","url":"https://www.roughguides.com/destinations/europe/turkey/culture-etiquette/mosque-manners/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Seen as rude and aggressive","confidence":"high","tip":"Gesture with an open hand (palm up or down) instead of your index finger when indicating a person or direction.","explanation":"Pointing directly at someone with your index finger is considered rude in Turkey — multiple Turkish-focused etiquette sources state plainly that 'it is rude to point at someone here,' often associating the gesture with accusation. Turks typically indicate people or directions with an open hand or a nod instead.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is very rude; use an open hand or a gentle wave instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing with the index finger is common and generally acceptable in casual contexts."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is impolite; use the thumb or a closed fist with thumb extended instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Not bad intentions, it's just etiquette in Turkey","url":"https://www.dailysabah.com/life/2015/01/07/not-bad-intentions-its-just-etiquette-in-turkey","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkish Culture: Communication","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/turkish-culture/turkish-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkish cultural taboos","url":"https://www.crownrelo.com/article/turkish-cultural-taboos","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Minimal PDA expected","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep public affection light (hand-holding is fine) and save more visible affection for private settings, especially outside major cities.","explanation":"Public displays of affection like kissing or prolonged hugging are tolerated in big cities and tourist resorts but can draw unwanted stares, and are more frowned upon in conservative or rural areas. Social attitudes toward public affection, especially among same-sex couples, remain notably more conservative outside secular, educated, urban circles.","regional_note":"Istanbul, Izmir, and tourist resorts are considerably more tolerant than rural or conservative inland regions.","also_check":[{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"rude","note":"PDA is strictly discouraged and can draw legal trouble, even for married couples."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek-kissing as a greeting and light PDA are common and socially accepted."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Public affection is seen as embarrassing and generally avoided, though attitudes are slowly shifting among younger people."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkey — Wikivoyage travel guide (Respect section)","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkey","kind":"reference"},{"title":"LGBT rights in Turkey","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LGBT_rights_in_Turkey","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Visitors expected to be punctual","confidence":"high","tip":"Plan to arrive on time or a few minutes early for business meetings, and message ahead if you'll be delayed.","explanation":"For business meetings in Turkey, visitors are expected to arrive on time even though their Turkish counterparts may themselves run a little late — arriving late as a guest can still read as a lack of respect. Social gatherings are far more flexible about timing.","regional_note":"Expectations are strictest in formal business settings in major cities; informal social get-togethers are much more relaxed about timing nationwide.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is critical; even a few minutes late can be seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Arriving 15-30 minutes late is common and often expected in business meetings."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is a sign of respect; lateness is generally unacceptable."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkey — Business Etiquette, Manners, and Culture","url":"https://www.cyborlink.com/besite/turkey.htm","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkey — Wikivoyage travel guide (Business etiquette)","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkey","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Refusing outright may offend","confidence":"high","tip":"Decline once with a polite excuse, then accept a small amount if your host insists again — or thank them warmly and firmly the second time if you truly don't want more.","explanation":"Turkish hospitality places real importance on offering food and drink, and hosts often extend offers more than once. It's customary for guests to initially decline and accept once the host insists again, letting the host show generosity and the guest show humility; flatly refusing without that back-and-forth, especially in a family home, can come across as rejecting the host's generosity.","regional_note":"This offer-and-insist ritual is strongest in home and family settings; casual restaurant service doesn't carry the same expectation.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Refusing food is fine with a polite excuse, but accepting a small amount first is often smoother."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food from a host, especially in a family setting, can feel like a personal rejection."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"A simple 'no, thank you' is generally accepted without offense."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkey — Wikivoyage travel guide (Respect section)","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkey","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkish Culture: Etiquette","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/turkish-culture/turkish-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Culture of Turkey","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Turkey","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Serious insult, avoid entirely","confidence":"high","tip":"Avoid the OK hand sign entirely in Turkey — nod, say 'tamam' (okay), or use a thumbs-up instead.","explanation":"The OK hand sign (thumb and index finger forming a circle) is not read as 'okay' in Turkey. Multiple independent sources describe it as a serious, vulgar gesture that functions as a homophobic insult. This is understood across generations, though younger, urban Turks are somewhat more likely to also recognize the Western 'OK' meaning.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"Considered a very offensive gesture, similar to an obscene insult."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"polite","note":"Commonly used to mean 'okay' or 'good,' though some younger people use it ironically."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Widely used to mean 'OK' or 'money,' with no negative connotations."}],"sources":[{"title":"Not bad intentions, it's just etiquette in Turkey","url":"https://www.dailysabah.com/life/2015/01/07/not-bad-intentions-its-just-etiquette-in-turkey","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkish Culture: Communication","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/turkish-culture/turkish-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Gestures — Wikivoyage","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkish cultural taboos","url":"https://www.crownrelo.com/article/turkish-cultural-taboos","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Likely seen as poor manners","confidence":"low","tip":"Eat soup or noodle dishes quietly with a spoon or fork; a little noise likely won't draw comment, but keep it minimal in formal settings.","explanation":"There's little direct, Turkey-specific guidance on slurping noodles or soup, since noodles aren't a dominant part of Turkish cuisine the way they are in East Asia. General Turkish etiquette guidance discourages audible eating and other mouth noises at the table, suggesting slurping would likely read as mildly poor manners rather than the sign of enjoyment it is in Japan — but this is inferred from broader table-manners norms rather than confirmed by a gesture-specific source.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is a sign of enjoyment and appreciation for the chef."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping is common and shows you're savoring the food, though it varies by region."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta is considered very bad table manners."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkey — Wikivoyage travel guide (Things to avoid)","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkey","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkey — Business Etiquette, Manners, and Culture","url":"https://www.cyborlink.com/besite/turkey.htm","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Common among friends","confidence":"medium","tip":"Let the other person initiate; mirror a cheek kiss if offered, and default to a handshake with new or more formal acquaintances.","explanation":"Cheek kissing is a common greeting in Turkey, especially between men in the same social circle (typically twice) and between closer acquaintances generally; between men and women it depends on familiarity and setting, with a handshake preferred in formal or conservative contexts. Hand-kissing — bringing an elder's hand to your forehead — is also a well-regarded way to greet older relatives.","regional_note":"Opposite-gender cheek kissing is more accepted in secular, urban circles and less common in conservative or rural settings.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing isn't customary; bowing is the standard greeting."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing (la bise) is common, though the number of kisses varies by region."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing between men is common, but between unrelated men and women it's generally avoided."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkey — Wikivoyage travel guide (Respect section)","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkey","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Gestures — Wikivoyage","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Considered disrespectful","confidence":"high","tip":"Use a palm-down scooping motion instead of a curling finger to call someone over.","explanation":"Beckoning someone by curling your index finger palm-up is considered rude in Turkey, since it's associated with calling animals rather than people. The respectful way to call someone over is to extend your hand palm-down and make a scooping or scratching motion toward yourself.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with the index finger is very rude and used only for animals or inferiors; use a full hand wave, palm down."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a finger is highly offensive; use a gentle hand motion with palm down instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Common among friends, but can seem aggressive or condescending in formal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkish Culture: Communication","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/turkish-culture/turkish-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkish Cultural Gestures & Body Language","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/turkish-cultural-gestures-body-language-realistic","kind":"community"},{"title":"7 gestures and phrases in Turkish culture","url":"https://cumhuriyet.com.tr/cumhuriyet-daily/galeri-7-gestures-and-phrases-in-turkish-culture-2105509","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Appreciated but modest","confidence":"high","tip":"Bring flowers, chocolates, or a small item from your home country when invited somewhere, and skip alcohol as a gift unless you already know your host drinks.","explanation":"Gift-giving is a well-regarded gesture of goodwill in Turkey, whether visiting someone's home (flowers, chocolates, or pastries are safe choices) or in business (something representative of your home country, good-quality sweets, etc.). Alcohol is best avoided as a gift unless you're confident your host drinks, since many Turks abstain for religious reasons.","regional_note":"Avoiding alcohol and pork-related gifts matters more in religious or conservative circles and is less of a concern in secular, urban settings.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized; avoid white or red wrapping (funeral colors) and present with both hands."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts can seem unnecessary or suspicious in formal business; if given, keep them small and practical."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts are appreciated but should align with Islamic values (no alcohol or pigskin); avoid giving directly to the opposite gender."}],"sources":[{"title":"Turkey — Wikivoyage travel guide (Respect section)","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Turkey","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Turkey — Business Etiquette, Manners, and Culture","url":"https://www.cyborlink.com/besite/turkey.htm","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Brazil":{"slug":"brazil","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Appreciated, rarely mandatory","confidence":"medium","tip":"Check the bill for a roughly 10% 'serviço' charge; if there isn't one, rounding up or leaving a little extra for good service is a nice gesture, not an obligation.","explanation":"Tipping is not a deeply ingrained expectation in Brazil the way it is in the US. Many restaurants print a voluntary ~10% service charge ('serviço') on the bill, which is customary to pay but not legally required, and diners who skip it are not considered rude. Outside full-service restaurants, small cash tips (a few reais, or coins left in a 'caixinha') are common for juice bars, parking attendants, or baggage handlers rather than a strict percentage rule.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is not practiced and can feel insulting, since good service is already expected without extra payment."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is standard and expected for restaurant service, since waitstaff rely on tips for income."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"A service charge is already included by law, so extra tipping is optional small change for exceptional service."}],"sources":[{"title":"List of tipping customs by country - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tipping_customs_by_country","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in Latin America - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_Latin_America","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Common positive gesture","confidence":"high","tip":"Use a thumbs-up freely to show approval or agreement — it is understood and welcomed in almost any context, from traffic to business meetings.","explanation":"Thumbs-up is one of the safest, most widely used positive gestures in Brazil, signaling approval or that everything is fine, in both casual and business settings. It has also become the preferred way to say 'okay' precisely because the circular OK hand sign is considered vulgar in Brazil.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Greece","verdict":"rude","note":"The thumbs-up can be read as an insult comparable to the middle finger."},{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture is widely seen as obscene, roughly equivalent to 'up yours'."},{"country":"Afghanistan","verdict":"caution","note":"The gesture can be seen as rude or offensive by some, particularly older generations, so a nod or verbal thanks is safer."}],"sources":[{"title":"Thumb signal - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thumb_signal","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Brazilian Culture - Communication, The Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/brazilian-culture/brazilian-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Portuguese cultural gestures & body language, Kaiwa","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/portuguese-cultural-gestures-body-language-realistic","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Informal, not taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Don't worry about where your soles point; just keep your feet off furniture and tables in formal settings as ordinary good manners.","explanation":"Brazil has no cultural taboo around feet being 'unclean,' unlike Thailand or parts of the Middle East. Propping your feet up, sitting cross-legged, or letting your shoe sole show is simply informal body language — fine among friends, but better avoided in a business meeting or someone's home out of general politeness rather than any specific foot-related offense.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The feet are considered the lowest, most impure part of the body; pointing them at people or sacred objects is a serious offense."},{"country":"Egypt","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the sole of your foot or shoe is a real insult, associated with dirt and disrespect."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Putting feet on furniture is generally just seen as informal or impolite in formal settings, not deeply offensive."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazil - Countries and Their Cultures (everyculture.com)","url":"https://www.everyculture.com/Bo-Co/Brazil.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Brazilian Culture - Etiquette, The Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/brazilian-culture/brazilian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Not a cultural issue here","confidence":"medium","tip":"Eat however feels natural — using your left hand for utensils or food is not considered rude or unhygienic in Brazil.","explanation":"Brazil has no religious or cultural rule against eating or handling food with the left hand, unlike India or parts of the Middle East where the left hand is reserved for hygiene. Brazilian table manners, rooted in European/Portuguese tradition, focus on general politeness (napkins, not reaching across the table) rather than which hand touches food.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is considered unclean and reserved for bathroom hygiene; food is eaten with the right hand."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Eating with the left hand is avoided under Islamic custom; the right hand is used for food."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Which hand holds chopsticks is a non-issue; left-handed eating draws no attention."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazil - Countries and Their Cultures (everyculture.com)","url":"https://www.everyculture.com/Bo-Co/Brazil.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in Latin America - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_Latin_America","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Can seem condescending","confidence":"medium","tip":"Save head-pats for children or close friends; greet adults with a handshake, cheek kiss, or a pat on the shoulder/back instead.","explanation":"Brazil has no special taboo around the head the way Thailand does. Patting an adult you don't know well on the head can still come across as patronizing or overly familiar, much as it would in most Western cultures, but it is not treated as a deep cultural offense. It's fine with children or very close friends.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body, and touching it is a serious taboo."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head is often affectionate, but doing so to an adult can read as patronizing."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting an adult's head is normally reserved for close friends or family; in professional settings it can seem condescending."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazil - Countries and Their Cultures (everyculture.com)","url":"https://www.everyculture.com/Bo-Co/Brazil.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Brazilian Culture - Etiquette, The Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/brazilian-culture/brazilian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected in business","confidence":"high","tip":"Maintain comfortable, steady eye contact in both business and social conversations — avoiding it can come across as uninterested or untrustworthy.","explanation":"Direct eye contact is valued in Brazil as a sign of honesty, confidence, and engagement, especially in business meetings and negotiations, where looking away can read as evasive or disinterested. Brazilians commonly combine steady eye contact with closer physical proximity and expressive body language compared to North American norms.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can feel intimidating; brief glances are often preferred, especially toward superiors."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct eye contact is expected in business, though typically less intense and held for shorter durations than in Brazil."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Eye contact between men is common, but between unrelated men and women it should stay brief and respectful."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazil Business Culture Guide, Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/brazil-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Portuguese cultural gestures & body language, Kaiwa","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/portuguese-cultural-gestures-body-language-realistic","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Often expected, not rude","confidence":"medium","tip":"When visiting a Brazilian home, offer to remove your shoes at the door or ask your host what they prefer — it's a common, appreciated courtesy, never an insult to offer.","explanation":"Contrary to the idea that Brazilians always keep their shoes on indoors, many households — most clearly documented in the Northeast — expect guests to remove their shoes before entering, and doing so is seen as considerate. Practice varies by family and region, so the safest approach is to watch for a shoe pile by the door or simply follow your host's lead.","regional_note":"Shoe removal is most explicitly expected in northeastern Brazil, though many urban households nationwide also prefer it, especially with light-colored floors.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes is mandatory before entering a home, usually at a dedicated genkan entryway."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal varies widely by household; it's polite to ask or follow the host's cue."},{"country":"Sweden","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes indoors is standard practice, especially in winter or when visiting a private home."}],"sources":[{"title":"Etiquette in Latin America - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_Latin_America","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Brazilian Culture - Etiquette, The Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/brazilian-culture/brazilian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Rude when aimed at people","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you need to indicate a person, use an open hand rather than a single pointed finger — pointing at objects draws much less attention than pointing at people.","explanation":"Pointing directly at a person with your index finger, especially a stranger in public, is seen as rude and confrontational in Brazil. Brazilians often gesture with an open hand instead, or point using their lips or chin toward a person or object, particularly when their hands are full.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with a single finger is considered impolite; an open hand gesture is preferred."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing with the index finger is common and generally acceptable, though seen as less polite in formal settings."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is considered offensive; the thumb or an open hand is used instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Etiquette in Latin America - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_Latin_America","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Portuguese cultural gestures & body language, Kaiwa","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/portuguese-cultural-gestures-body-language-realistic","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Warm, openly affectionate culture","confidence":"medium","tip":"Don't be surprised by close physical contact when greeting — reciprocate warmly with a cheek kiss or hug rather than stepping back, which can come across as cold.","explanation":"Brazil is a tactile, affectionate culture — hugging, cheek-kissing, hand-holding, and standing close while talking are all normal in social settings, reflecting a much smaller personal-space bubble than in the US or Northern Europe. Brazilians also generally hold relaxed attitudes toward the body (seen in beachwear and Carnival costumes), so moderate PDA attracts little attention; very formal or professional settings call for more restraint.","regional_note":"Cheek-kiss greetings vary by region: one kiss is typical in São Paulo, two in Rio de Janeiro, and three or more in Bahia and other parts of the Northeast.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Public affection is highly restrained; even hand-holding can feel out of place in formal settings."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"rude","note":"Public affection between unmarried couples is culturally taboo and can carry legal risk."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek-kissing (la bise) is a standard greeting, and moderate PDA is widely accepted socially."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazil - Countries and Their Cultures (everyculture.com)","url":"https://www.everyculture.com/Bo-Co/Brazil.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Brazilian kissing culture, The Discoveries Of","url":"https://www.thediscoveriesof.com/brazilian-kissing-culture/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Flexible socially, less so at work","confidence":"medium","tip":"For business, aim to arrive on time or within a few minutes; for a purely social invitation to a home, treat the stated time as a loose guideline rather than a deadline.","explanation":"Brazil is often described as running on 'hora brasileira' (Brazilian time) — for a social gathering or a visit to someone's home, arriving 15-30 minutes after the stated time is normal and not considered rude, and arriving exactly on time can even seem premature. Business meetings are different: Brazilians typically schedule in advance and increasingly expect punctuality, especially with senior executives, international partners, or in more formal industries.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is strictly observed; even a few minutes late is considered disrespectful."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Being late is seen as unprofessional and reflects poorly on planning."},{"country":"Mexico","verdict":"polite","note":"Arriving somewhat late to social gatherings is common and generally not seen as rude."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazilian Culture - Etiquette, The Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/brazilian-culture/brazilian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Polychronicity - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polychronicity","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Brazil Business Culture Guide, Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/brazil-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Can seem impolite, but excuses work","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you don't want more food, decline warmly with a compliment about the meal rather than a flat 'no' — most hosts will accept a polite explanation.","explanation":"Brazilian hospitality is warm, and hosts often encourage guests to eat more, so an outright refusal can feel like brushing off their generosity. However, Brazil does not carry the strong food-refusal taboo found in some South Asian cultures — politely declining with a smile, a compliment on the food, or a stated reason (fullness, dietary restriction) is generally accepted without real offense.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food, especially in a family setting, is considered quite disrespectful as it implies rejecting the host's hospitality."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"A polite refusal with a stated reason is accepted; blunt refusal without explanation can feel awkward."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"Declining food is more acceptable with a polite excuse and a compliment; hosts often insist you try a little regardless."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazil - Countries and Their Cultures (everyculture.com)","url":"https://www.everyculture.com/Bo-Co/Brazil.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Brazilian Culture - Etiquette, The Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/brazilian-culture/brazilian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Considered a vulgar insult","confidence":"medium","tip":"Avoid making the circular OK sign in Brazil — use a thumbs-up instead to signal approval or that something is fine.","explanation":"The circular 'OK' hand sign is a well-documented obscene gesture in Brazil, historically equivalent to the middle finger — most famously when it caused a diplomatic incident after then-Vice President Richard Nixon flashed it to a crowd in Rio de Janeiro in 1958 and was met with boos. Awareness of the American 'okay' meaning has spread, especially among younger, internationally-connected Brazilians, so the gesture is somewhat less universally shocking today, but it still carries the vulgar meaning for most locals and is best avoided.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"The sign is read as a vulgar insult, historically implying homosexuality as a slur."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"polite","note":"Commonly used to mean 'okay' or 'good,' though it can look dismissive if used in place of a verbal answer."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Often understood as 'okay' or 'money,' with no offensive meaning."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazilian Culture - Communication, The Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/brazilian-culture/brazilian-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in Latin America - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_Latin_America","kind":"reference"},{"title":"List of gestures - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Portuguese cultural gestures & body language, Kaiwa","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/portuguese-cultural-gestures-body-language-realistic","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Quiet eating is the norm","confidence":"medium","tip":"When eating noodle dishes (increasingly common via Japanese-Brazilian and Italian-Brazilian food culture), eat quietly as you would any other dish — slurping isn't expected or appreciated.","explanation":"Noodles are not a traditional staple of Brazilian cuisine, but general table manners there follow Western/European conventions favoring quiet, closed-mouth eating. Audible slurping — of noodles, soup, or coffee — is typically seen as a minor lapse in manners rather than a serious offense, especially outside formal settings.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is a sign of enjoyment and appreciation for the dish."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping is common and signals you're savoring the meal, though very loud slurping can seem crude in formal settings."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta is considered quite uncivilized; quiet, deliberate eating is the norm."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazil - Countries and Their Cultures (everyculture.com)","url":"https://www.everyculture.com/Bo-Co/Brazil.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in Latin America - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_Latin_America","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Common and expected","confidence":"high","tip":"Lean in starting with the right cheek and make a light kissing sound without pressing your lips to the skin, and follow the local kiss count rather than assuming it's always just one.","explanation":"Cheek-kissing (beijo no rosto) is the standard social greeting in Brazil, typically between women, and between men and women, starting on the right cheek. It signals warmth and is expected even among new acquaintances in social settings; declining it outright can come across as cold, though a handshake is the norm in formal business contexts.","regional_note":"The number of kisses varies by region: one is typical in São Paulo, two in Rio de Janeiro, and three or more in Bahia and other parts of the Northeast.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Cheek kissing is not customary; a bow or handshake is the standard greeting."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing (la bise) is common, though the number of kisses varies by region (typically two to four)."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Kissing between unrelated men and women is not done; follow local gender norms closely."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazil - Countries and Their Cultures (everyculture.com)","url":"https://www.everyculture.com/Bo-Co/Brazil.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Brazilian Culture - Greetings, The Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/brazilian-culture/brazilian-culture-greetings","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Brazilian kissing culture, The Discoveries Of","url":"https://www.thediscoveriesof.com/brazilian-kissing-culture/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Can seem brusque, not severe","confidence":"medium","tip":"To call someone over politely, wave with your whole hand rather than crooking a single finger, especially with someone you don't know well.","explanation":"Beckoning with a single crooked index finger can come across as curt or overly commanding in Brazil, particularly toward strangers or in professional settings — Brazilians more commonly wave someone over with the whole hand. That said, it is not treated as the severe insult it is in places like the Philippines; even calling a waiter over with a raised hand is completely normal and expected in Brazilian restaurants.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with the index finger is seen as rude, closer to calling an animal; a palm-down hand wave is used instead."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a finger is considered highly offensive and can be seen as a challenge; a gentle palm-down wave is used instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Common in casual settings, but can seem dismissive or condescending in formal or professional contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazilian Culture - Etiquette, The Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/brazilian-culture/brazilian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"List of gestures - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcome, with a few taboos","confidence":"medium","tip":"Choose a modest, thoughtful gift, avoid purple wrapping paper and sharp objects, and be mindful that opposite-gender gifts outside of birthdays can send an unintended romantic signal.","explanation":"Gift-giving is a warm, appreciated gesture in Brazil, both socially and in business, though business gifts should stay modest to avoid any appearance of bribery. A few specific things to avoid: wrapping a gift in purple (associated with mourning), giving anything sharp like knives (seen as symbolically 'cutting' the relationship), and giving a gift to someone of the opposite gender outside of a birthday, which can be misread as a romantic gesture rather than simple courtesy.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized; avoid white flowers or sets of four, and present gifts with both hands."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts are often unnecessary in business contexts; if given, keep them small and practical."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"polite","note":"Gifts are welcomed, but avoid alcohol or pigskin products, and use the right hand to give and receive."}],"sources":[{"title":"Brazilian Culture - Etiquette, The Cultural Atlas","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/brazilian-culture/brazilian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in Latin America - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_Latin_America","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"United States":{"slug":"united-states","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected, tied to wage law","confidence":"high","tip":"Tip 15-20% of the pre-tax total for sit-down service, and if service was genuinely poor, speak to a manager rather than leaving no tip.","explanation":"In the US, tipping 15-20% of the pre-tax bill at a sit-down restaurant is a deeply embedded social norm, not a bonus: federal law lets employers pay tipped workers a direct cash wage of just $2.13/hour (versus the $7.25 federal minimum), with tips expected to make up the difference. Skipping or significantly under-tipping for normal service is widely seen as a breach of etiquette, since servers' income structurally depends on tips.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is not practiced and can be seen as insulting; excellent service is already the baseline expectation."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"A service charge is included by law, but rounding up or leaving small change for good service is appreciated."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"caution","note":"Tipping is not customary and can confuse or embarrass staff; upscale restaurants may add a service charge instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Fact Sheet #15: Tipped Employees Under the FLSA","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/15-tipped-employees-flsa","kind":"reference"},{"title":"The Finer Points of Tipping - Emily Post Institute","url":"https://emilypost.com/advice/finer-points-of-tipping","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Commisceo Global USA Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/usa-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Clear, positive, everyday gesture","confidence":"high","tip":"Use it freely in casual and most professional settings; it is rarely if ever misread negatively in the US.","explanation":"In the US, the thumbs-up is a universally understood positive gesture meaning approval, agreement, or 'all good,' used casually among friends, coworkers, and strangers alike (e.g., signaling OK to a driver). It carries no negative connotation in any American context and is just as common online as in person.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"Traditionally read as a vulgar insult roughly equivalent to an obscene gesture, not as approval."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Can be used to mean the number 'one' when counting, so context determines whether it reads as approval or a numeral."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"polite","note":"Understood the same way as in the US, as a casual sign of approval, with no negative connotation."}],"sources":[{"title":"Thumb signal - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thumb_signal","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Hand gesture - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hand_gesture","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Informal, not a real taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep your feet on the floor in formal settings or around people you don't know well; it's fine to relax more with close friends or family.","explanation":"In the US, propping your feet up or letting the soles of your shoes face someone reads as very casual or overly relaxed rather than disrespectful - it's common to see people, including public figures, put their feet up to seem approachable. The main objection most Americans would have is practical (dirty shoes on furniture), not a belief that feet are symbolically unclean.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"Feet are considered the lowest, dirtiest part of the body; pointing soles at a person or a Buddha image is a serious insult."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the soles of your feet or resting them on furniture is considered a genuine insult, not just casual."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Pointing your feet at someone, especially an elder or in a formal setting, is seen as poor manners, though a milder breach than in Thailand."}],"sources":[{"title":"12 American Habits That Come Off as Rude Overseas","url":"https://www.aol.com/articles/12-american-habits-come-off-163616069.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Foot - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foot","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"No hand-use rule at all","confidence":"high","tip":"Use whichever hand is comfortable; American dining etiquette cares more about handling cutlery correctly (e.g., the 'zig-zag' fork-switching style) than about left-versus-right hand symbolism.","explanation":"There is no cultural rule in the US about which hand you eat with - handedness is purely personal preference, not a matter of hygiene or etiquette, unlike in parts of South Asia, the Middle East, and Africa where the left hand is reserved for hygiene and the right for eating. American table-manners guidance focuses on fork/knife handling, not which hand touches food.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Eating with the left hand is considered unclean, as it is traditionally reserved for bathroom hygiene."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is reserved for personal hygiene; food should be eaten and passed with the right hand."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Not strictly rude, but using the left hand for shared/communal dishes can look awkward since the right hand is customary there."}],"sources":[{"title":"Commisceo Global USA Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/usa-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Left-handedness - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Left-handedness","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Okay for kids, not adults","confidence":"medium","tip":"Stick to a handshake, high-five, or a pat on the shoulder with adults; save head-pats for children or people you know very well.","explanation":"In the US, patting a child on the head is a common, affectionate gesture, but doing the same to an adult you're not close to can come across as condescending or overly familiar - Americans generally keep greetings to handshake-level contact and reserve head-touching for close relationships. It isn't taboo or deeply offensive, just socially awkward outside family or close-friend contexts.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body; touching anyone's head, including children's, is a serious offense."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head can be affectionate, but doing so to an adult, especially an elder, is seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Public physical contact is minimal; patting someone's head is seen as overly familiar rather than a serious offense."}],"sources":[{"title":"12 American Habits That Come Off as Rude Overseas","url":"https://www.aol.com/articles/12-american-habits-come-off-163616069.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Personal space - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_space","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Signals confidence and honesty","confidence":"high","tip":"Maintain natural eye contact through most of a conversation, breaking it periodically rather than staring fixedly.","explanation":"In the US, making direct eye contact is expected and valued in both business and everyday social settings - it signals confidence, honesty, and engagement, and its absence can be misread as disinterest or evasiveness. This contrasts with many Asian and some Middle Eastern cultures, where sustained eye contact with elders or superiors can be read as a challenge.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can feel confrontational; briefer glances are more typical, especially with someone senior."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Eye contact between men is normal, but between men and women it's kept brief and more reserved in conservative settings."},{"country":"Nigeria","verdict":"caution","note":"Sustained eye contact with elders or superiors can be seen as a challenge; younger or junior people often lower their gaze out of respect."}],"sources":[{"title":"Commisceo Global USA Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/usa-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"USAHello - Be Polite in the US","url":"https://usahello.org/life-in-usa/culture/be-polite/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Host's preference, not a rule","confidence":"medium","tip":"Look for a shoe pile or rack by the door, or simply ask your host, 'Should I take my shoes off?' when you arrive.","explanation":"Unlike in many Asian households, there's no national expectation to remove your shoes when entering a US home - it comes down to the individual host. Many Americans do ask guests to take shoes off, for cleanliness or to protect floors and carpets, especially in bad weather, but plenty of households don't mind either way.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes before entering a home is mandatory, with slippers typically provided for indoor use."},{"country":"Canada","verdict":"polite","note":"Very common to remove shoes indoors, especially in winter when floors and carpets need protecting from snow and salt."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"In northeastern Brazil especially, guests are expected to remove their shoes before entering a home; keeping them on can be seen as rude."}],"sources":[{"title":"Etiquette in Brazil - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_Brazil","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in the United States - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_the_United_States","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Normal, but not at faces","confidence":"medium","tip":"Point freely for directions or objects; when gesturing toward a person, especially up close, an open hand feels less confrontational than a pointed finger.","explanation":"Pointing with your index finger to indicate a direction, object, or person across a room is normal and unremarkable in the US, unlike in many Asian cultures where it's considered rude. The main exception is pointing directly at someone's face up close, which can come across as aggressive or accusatory.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger at a person is considered rude; gesturing with an open hand, palm up, is preferred."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is considered impolite; people often point with the thumb instead."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Pointing is acceptable but can come across as blunt; many Germans still consider it a bit impolite toward people."}],"sources":[{"title":"Pointing - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointing","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Casual affection widely accepted","confidence":"high","tip":"Light, brief affection - hand-holding, hugging, a quick kiss - is fine in almost any public setting; save more intimate moments for somewhere private.","explanation":"Mainstream American social norms are quite relaxed about PDA - holding hands, hugging, and a brief kiss between partners are considered normal and accepted in most public settings. The Emily Post Institute's own modern guidance states plainly that 'there's nothing wrong with kissing and hugging in public'; more prolonged or intimate contact is where people start to feel it imposes on bystanders.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Overt affection like hugging or kissing in public is still more reserved than in the US, especially around older generations, though hand-holding among younger couples is increasingly common."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing (la bise) and light affection between couples are common and accepted in social settings."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Public affection between unmarried couples is culturally and legally taboo."}],"sources":[{"title":"Etiquette Throwback 1922: Emily Post on PDA - Emily Post Institute","url":"https://emilypost.substack.com/p/etiquette-throwback-1922-emily-post-7e4","kind":"reference"},{"title":"USAHello - Be Polite in the US","url":"https://usahello.org/life-in-usa/culture/be-polite/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Punctuality expected, minor exceptions","confidence":"high","tip":"Aim to arrive a few minutes early, and if you're running late, message the organizer as soon as you know, with an updated arrival time.","explanation":"In US business culture, punctuality signals respect and professionalism, and arriving late to a meeting without notice is widely seen as unprofessional and inconsiderate of others' time. A few minutes' grace is usually tolerated if you communicate proactively, but showing up late repeatedly or without warning damages your credibility.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is taken very seriously; arriving even a minute late is seen as disrespectful and poorly organized."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"In cities like Sao Paulo and Rio, meetings often start 10-15 minutes late by custom, though visitors should still aim to arrive on time."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Traffic and flexible scheduling mean meetings may start late, but arriving on time is still appreciated and expected of visitors."}],"sources":[{"title":"Commisceo Global USA Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/usa-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"USAHello - Be Polite in the US","url":"https://usahello.org/life-in-usa/culture/be-polite/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine if polite about it","confidence":"medium","tip":"Decline with a brief reason and a compliment, e.g., 'No thank you, I'm stuffed, but that looks amazing' - it shows appreciation without obligating you to eat.","explanation":"In the US, you can decline food a host offers without major offense, especially citing fullness, dietary restrictions, or allergies - a polite 'no thank you' is generally respected. In some households, especially in the South, a host may insist once or twice out of hospitality, but a warm, clear decline is still accepted.","regional_note":"Southern US hosts are more likely to press food on guests out of a strong regional hospitality tradition centered on generously feeding visitors, so a gentle 'maybe later' can smooth things over.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food outright can seem impolite; accepting at least a small amount, even if you don't finish it, is the safer approach."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Declining food, especially in a family setting, may prompt repeated insistence, since offering food is closely tied to hospitality."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"It's fine to decline if you explain politely, but refusing a dish someone cooked themselves can be taken as a slight to their effort."}],"sources":[{"title":"USAHello - Be Polite in the US","url":"https://usahello.org/life-in-usa/culture/be-polite/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Southern hospitality - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_hospitality","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Positive, rarely misread","confidence":"high","tip":"Use it normally; context is what matters, and an isolated hateful usage is the rare exception, not the rule.","explanation":"In everyday American use, the OK sign (thumb and index finger forming a circle) means approval or 'all good' and has carried that meaning for roughly two centuries. Since 2017 the same shape has also been co-opted, ironically and then sincerely, by a small extremist fringe online, but the Anti-Defamation League notes it 'in most contexts is entirely innocuous' - most Americans still read it as the ordinary positive gesture.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"Considered a vulgar insult, roughly equivalent to giving someone the middle finger."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"Can carry an offensive connotation there and is best avoided."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Commonly understood to mean 'OK' or 'money,' and generally reads as a positive gesture."}],"sources":[{"title":"OK gesture - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OK_gesture","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Hand gesture - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hand_gesture","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"rude","label":"Loud eating is poor manners","confidence":"high","tip":"Twirl noodles onto a fork or spoon and eat quietly; the rule relaxes a bit at a ramen or pho shop among people who expect slurping, but quiet eating is the safe default elsewhere.","explanation":"American table manners strongly favor quiet eating - chewing with your mouth closed and avoiding noise from food or cutlery is a basic expectation taught from childhood. Slurping noodles or soup loudly is seen as careless or poor manners, the opposite of the social approval the same sound gets in Japan or China.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is seen as a compliment to the chef and is expected at ramen and soba shops."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is common and signals enjoyment, though it's toned down in more formal dining."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Pasta is eaten quietly, twirled onto a fork without noise; slurping is seen as uncivilized."}],"sources":[{"title":"Table manners - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_manners","kind":"reference"},{"title":"USAHello - Be Polite in the US","url":"https://usahello.org/life-in-usa/culture/be-polite/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Not the default greeting","confidence":"medium","tip":"Don't initiate a cheek kiss with someone you're meeting for the first time or in a business setting; offer a handshake and let the other person lead if they want to cheek-kiss instead.","explanation":"Cheek kissing isn't the standard American greeting - a handshake or hug is far more typical, even among acquaintances. Where it does happen, it's usually between people who already know each other well, or in cities with strong European or Latin American immigrant influence, where a single cheek kiss has become more common socially.","regional_note":"Miami is a notable exception, where a single cheek kiss is a much more common social greeting than in most of the US, reflecting the city's large Latin American and European immigrant communities.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing (la bise) is a standard greeting among friends and acquaintances, with the number of kisses varying by region."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Bowing is the standard greeting, and cheek kissing is unusual enough that it can feel out of place rather than warmly received."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing is a common greeting, often one kiss per cheek, even among people meeting for the first time socially."}],"sources":[{"title":"Cheek kissing - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheek_kissing","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine casually, risky with staff","confidence":"medium","tip":"With friends, it's fine; to get a server's or stranger's attention, make eye contact, raise a hand, or say 'excuse me' instead.","explanation":"Curling your index finger to wave someone over is a normal, widely understood 'come here' gesture among friends and family in the US. It reads as demanding or disrespectful in more formal situations, though, particularly if used on a waiter, employee, or stranger, since it can feel like the gesture people use to call an animal.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with the index finger is associated with calling animals and is considered rude when directed at a person; a palm-down wave is used instead."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a curled finger toward a person is considered disrespectful and historically associated with calling dogs; a palm-down hand wave is preferred."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Not as loaded as in some cultures, but still seen as abrupt; a verbal request is preferred in formal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Hand gesture - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hand_gesture","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcome but kept modest","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep business gifts modest and check your recipient's company policy if you're unsure; for social occasions, a thoughtful, personal gift matters more than an expensive one.","explanation":"In US business settings, a modest, thoughtful gift is a nice gesture but isn't expected, and many companies cap gift value or discourage gifts from vendors to avoid any appearance of a bribe. Socially, gifts are customary for occasions like birthdays or holidays but aren't strictly obligatory - and directly asking a host about monetary gifts is considered poor form.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is a deeply ingrained ritual with strong expectations around presentation and reciprocity, often given at first meetings."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Business gifts are less common and should stay small and practical to avoid any appearance of bribery."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Gifts are commonly expected in business and social settings, with red envelopes of money customary for holidays and weddings; clocks and sharp objects are avoided as gifts."}],"sources":[{"title":"Commisceo Global USA Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/usa-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in the United States - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_the_United_States","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"United Kingdom":{"slug":"united-kingdom","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Optional, check the bill first","confidence":"high","tip":"Check the bill for an already-added service charge before tipping extra; if there isn't one, 10-12.5% for good service is standard practice.","explanation":"Tipping at UK restaurants is appreciated but discretionary, not obligatory. Many bills already include a 10-12.5% discretionary service charge, which can be removed if service was poor. Since 1 October 2024, the Employment (Allocation of Tips) Act 2023 requires employers to pass all tips and service charges to staff, so anything you leave goes directly to workers rather than the business.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping can be seen as insulting or confusing; excellent service is already the norm and included in the price."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is expected and forms a major part of server income; not tipping is considered very rude."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Service is legally included in the bill ('service compris'), but rounding up or leaving small change for great service is a nice gesture."}],"sources":[{"title":"Employment (Allocation of Tips) Act 2023 - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Employment_(Allocation_of_Tips)_Act_2023","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Gratuity - Wikipedia (UK tronc system)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gratuity","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Positive, casual approval sign","confidence":"high","tip":"Use the thumbs-up freely in casual and most professional settings in the UK; it is almost never read as rude.","explanation":"The thumbs-up is understood across the UK as a casual, positive signal of approval, agreement, or encouragement, used in everyday conversation and most professional contexts without causing offense. Its positive use in English-speaking countries is long-established, dating at least to the First World War.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Greece","verdict":"caution","note":"Historically read by some older Greeks as a vulgar gesture rather than approval, though the positive Western meaning is now widely understood, especially among younger people."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Often used to mean the number 'one' when counting on fingers, which can cause brief confusion rather than offense."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Can be seen as impolite by some in more conservative or older circles, though it is now widely understood and used positively, especially among younger people."}],"sources":[{"title":"Thumb signal - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thumb_signal","kind":"reference"},{"title":"List of gestures - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Feet on furniture looks careless","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep your feet and shoes off seats, tables, and other furniture out of consideration for cleanliness, not because of any specific taboo about the soles themselves.","explanation":"The UK has no specific cultural taboo against showing the soles of your feet or pointing them at someone, unlike the strong body-part taboos found in Thailand or parts of the Middle East and South Asia. The actual British faux pas is more mundane: putting your feet up on a chair, sofa, table, or train seat is seen as inconsiderate, careless of shared furniture, or unhygienic, rather than as a grave insult to another person.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"Feet are considered the lowest and most unclean part of the body; pointing the soles at a person or a Buddha image is genuinely offensive."},{"country":"Egypt","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the sole of your foot or shoe to someone is a real insult in much of the Arab world."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Similar to the UK, feet on furniture is seen as careless or rude in formal settings rather than taboo."}],"sources":[{"title":"Commisceo Global - United Kingdom Country Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/uk-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"No stigma on either hand","confidence":"high","tip":"Hold your fork in your left hand and knife in your right when cutting; either keep the fork in your left hand to eat or switch it to your right - both styles are normal in the UK.","explanation":"There is no cultural taboo in the UK against eating or handling food with the left hand. British table manners focus on using the knife and fork correctly: fork in the left hand and knife in the right while cutting, then either keeping the fork in the left hand ('Continental' style) or switching it to the right hand to eat ('American' style) - both are acceptable, and the choice of hand carries no stigma.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is traditionally considered unclean for eating; the right hand is used for handling and eating food."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Eating or passing food with the left hand is discouraged; the right hand is used for eating and greeting."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"There is no hand taboo; chopsticks are used with either hand and left-handedness carries no stigma."}],"sources":[{"title":"Table manners - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_manners","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Commisceo Global - United Kingdom Country Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/uk-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Patronizing, not taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Stick to a handshake, a nod, or a light touch on the arm or shoulder with people you know well; avoid patting an adult's head even as a joke.","explanation":"The UK has no sacred or spiritual taboo around the head, unlike cultures such as Thailand. Patting an adult on the head isn't forbidden, but it reads as patronizing or overly familiar - similar to how you'd treat a child or a pet - so most adults would find it condescending or odd outside of close family relationships.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered sacred; touching anyone's head, including a child's, is a serious breach of etiquette."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head affectionately is common, but doing so to an adult is seen as disrespectful and intrusive."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Public physical contact is minimal in general, so head-patting between adults is seen as overly familiar."}],"sources":[{"title":"List of gestures - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Personal space - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_space","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Confidence signal, don't stare","confidence":"high","tip":"Hold steady, natural eye contact when speaking or listening, glancing away periodically rather than staring fixedly.","explanation":"In the UK, especially in business and professional settings, moderate direct eye contact is expected and reads as honest, confident, and engaged; avoiding it can come across as evasive. Constant, unbroken staring can feel intense or confrontational, so natural breaks in gaze during conversation are normal.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact, especially with someone senior, can be seen as disrespectful; brief glances are preferred."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Direct eye contact between men can signal sincerity, but is often reduced between genders out of modesty."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Similarly values direct eye contact as a sign of confidence and honesty, closely matching UK norms."}],"sources":[{"title":"Commisceo Global - United Kingdom Country Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/uk-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Eye contact - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_contact","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Depends on the household","confidence":"medium","tip":"If unsure, ask 'Should I take my shoes off?' when you arrive, or simply follow what the host and other guests do.","explanation":"There's no single UK-wide rule on shoes indoors - it depends on the household, and the split is more generational than universal. A 2025 survey found 78% of 18-34 year-olds have a 'shoes-off' rule at home, compared with only 51% of those over 55, so younger and more recently-renovated homes increasingly expect shoes off, while others genuinely don't mind. Removing your shoes unprompted is never wrong, but insisting on it when a host waves it off can feel oddly formal.","regional_note":"Shoe removal is somewhat less common in rural homes, partly because cold stone floors and frequent trips between the house and garden make it less practical.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes at the entrance is a strict, near-universal rule, and indoor slippers are usually provided."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"polite","note":"Most Germans remove shoes on entering a home, and hosts often offer house slippers."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Similarly variable by household and region - common in many homes, especially colder areas, but not universal."}],"sources":[{"title":"Shoes in the house - Wikipedia (citing The Daily Telegraph, 2025)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoes_in_the_house","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Commisceo Global - United Kingdom Country Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/uk-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine for objects, not people","confidence":"medium","tip":"Point at things freely, but gesture toward a person with an open hand or a nod rather than a jabbing index finger.","explanation":"Pointing at objects or directions with your index finger is unremarkable in the UK, but pointing your finger directly at a person - especially while making a point in an argument - can come across as aggressive or accusatory. Many Britons will use an open hand or a nod instead when gesturing toward someone, particularly during a disagreement.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing at a person with the index finger is considered impolite; an open hand is used instead."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is considered offensive; the thumb is often used instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Similar to the UK, pointing at objects is fine but pointing at a person can seem confrontational."}],"sources":[{"title":"Pointing - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointing","kind":"reference"},{"title":"List of gestures - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Keep it brief and low-key","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep physical affection brief and low-key in public - holding hands or a quick kiss is fine, but save anything more passionate for private.","explanation":"Public displays of affection such as holding hands or a quick kiss are generally accepted across Western Europe, including the UK, and most Britons won't react to mild affection. However, British social norms favor reserve and understatement, so prolonged kissing or very visible affection in public can make bystanders uncomfortable.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek-kissing as a greeting and moderate PDA are culturally normal and common in public."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Public affection between unmarried couples is against the law and can lead to arrest."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"PDA is uncommon and even hand-holding can draw attention in many social contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"Public display of affection - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_display_of_affection","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Cheek kissing - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheek_kissing","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"rude","label":"Punctuality signals respect","confidence":"high","tip":"Aim to arrive on time or a few minutes early for a business meeting, and message or call ahead the moment you know you'll be late.","explanation":"Punctuality is taken seriously in UK business culture and is seen as a sign of professionalism and respect for other people's time. Turning up late to a meeting without warning comes across as rude and can suggest poor planning; a few minutes' delay is usually forgiven if you apologise and give advance notice, but persistent lateness damages your professional reputation.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is taken even more seriously than in the UK; arriving even a minute late is considered disrespectful."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"Arriving 15-30 minutes late to a business meeting is often tolerated and can be normal, though this varies by company and region."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Meetings may start later than scheduled due to a more flexible cultural approach to time, though punctuality from visitors is still appreciated."}],"sources":[{"title":"Commisceo Global - United Kingdom Country Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/uk-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Punctuality - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punctuality","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"polite","label":"A simple no thank you is fine","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you don't want something, a simple 'No, thank you, I'm fine' is enough - you don't need to refuse multiple times before accepting.","explanation":"Unlike cultures with a ritualized 'refuse twice, accept on the third offer' pattern, British hosts generally take a polite 'no, thank you' at face value - there's no expectation that you must perform modesty by declining before accepting. A host may offer again anyway out of general friendliness, but you're not being rude by sticking to your first answer.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"China","verdict":"caution","note":"A polite show of reluctance before accepting is common, and refusing the last piece of food on a shared plate can seem impolite."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Hosts often insist multiple times out of hospitality, and accepting at least a small amount is appreciated, especially in family settings."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"A brief initial refusal is a common politeness ritual, but guests are expected to accept when the host insists."}],"sources":[{"title":"Table manners - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_manners","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Hospitality - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hospitality","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Means 'fine' or 'agreed'","confidence":"high","tip":"Use the OK sign freely to confirm agreement or that something is fine; it is not read as rude or vulgar in the UK.","explanation":"In the UK, the OK hand sign (thumb and index finger forming a circle) is understood as a positive signal meaning 'okay,' 'fine,' or 'agreed' in everyday and professional contexts. It carries no offensive meaning in British culture, unlike in some other countries where the same shape is read very differently.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"Considered a vulgar, obscene gesture, roughly equivalent to showing the middle finger."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"Can be read as an offensive gesture with a vulgar sexual connotation rather than meaning 'okay.'"},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"In northern France it generally means 'okay,' but in southern France it can mean 'zero' or 'worthless,' so context matters."}],"sources":[{"title":"OK gesture - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OK_gesture","kind":"reference"},{"title":"List of gestures - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"rude","label":"Quiet eating expected","confidence":"high","tip":"Twirl noodles discreetly around your fork or spoon and eat quietly, as you would with any other dish in the UK.","explanation":"Eating quietly is a core part of British table manners, and slurping noodles or soup is generally seen as impolite and a bit uncouth. This isn't specific to noodles - Western table manners broadly discourage making noise with food or cutlery - so the norm applies in both casual and formal UK dining settings.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles like ramen or soba is a sign of enjoyment and is not considered rude."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is common and seen as showing appreciation, though it may be toned down in very formal settings."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta is considered poor manners; pasta is twirled onto a fork and eaten quietly."}],"sources":[{"title":"Table manners - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_manners","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Reserved for family and friends","confidence":"high","tip":"Default to a handshake or a friendly 'hello' with people you don't know well, and save the cheek kiss for friends and family.","explanation":"Cheek kissing is not the default British greeting - a handshake or simple verbal hello is far more common, especially with strangers or in professional settings. Where cheek kissing is used, a single kiss on one cheek is typical (some go for two), and it's generally reserved for family, close friends, or partners rather than new acquaintances.","regional_note":"Cheek kissing is somewhat more associated with middle- and upper-class social circles in the UK, reflecting historical French cultural influence, rather than being an evenly common national habit.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"'La bise' - one or more cheek kisses - is a standard everyday greeting among friends and acquaintances, with the exact number varying by region."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Physical greetings like kissing are avoided; a bow is the standard and expected greeting."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"One or two cheek kisses are common even among new acquaintances in social settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Cheek kissing - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheek_kissing","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Commisceo Global - United Kingdom Country Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/uk-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Comes across as bossy","confidence":"medium","tip":"To call someone over politely, use an open hand with the palm up, a small wave, or simply say their name.","explanation":"There's no legal or sacred taboo around this gesture in the UK, but beckoning someone with a crooked index finger (palm up) is widely seen as brusque or condescending when used on an adult, since the same gesture is typically used for calling children, pets, or in similarly Western Anglo cultures. It can come across as rude, especially toward a stranger or in a professional setting, more because it feels bossy than because it's a cultural taboo.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Considered very rude and associated with calling animals; the polite version waves the fingers with the palm facing down."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Seen as highly offensive when directed at a person and is reserved for calling animals; using it on someone can even cause real trouble."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Less severe than in some cultures, but still seen as impolite in formal settings, where a hand wave or verbal call is preferred."}],"sources":[{"title":"Beckoning sign - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beckoning_sign","kind":"reference"},{"title":"List of gestures - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Keep it modest, skip lilies","confidence":"medium","tip":"If invited to a colleague's home, bring a bottle of wine, a box of chocolates, or a seasonal bouquet - skip lilies and keep any gift modest rather than lavish.","explanation":"Gift-giving isn't a standard part of UK business culture, and an expensive or elaborate gift can come across as inappropriate or even like an attempted bribe; a modest gesture like good chocolates or a bottle of wine is more common than a formal gift exchange. If you're invited to someone's home, bringing wine, flowers, or chocolates for the host is a thoughtful touch - many people avoid all-white or lily arrangements due to their strong association with funerals, and very romantic gestures like red roses, when the occasion is simply a dinner invitation.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is a highly ritualized and expected part of business culture; careful presentation and wrapping matter a great deal."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts can seem unnecessary or even slightly suspicious in formal business settings until a relationship is well established."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is common and appreciated, but should respect Islamic customs - avoid alcohol and pork, and give with the right hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Commisceo Global - United Kingdom Country Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/uk-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Chrysanthemum - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrysanthemum","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Spain":{"slug":"spain","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Appreciated, never required","confidence":"high","tip":"Round up to the next few euros or leave about 5-10% for great service; a tip isn't expected for quick coffees or bar drinks.","explanation":"Tipping in Spain is a personal reward for good service, not a social obligation — menu prices already include service, and waitstaff don't depend on tips as part of their wage. Most locals round up the bill or leave roughly 5-10% only when service was notably good, and it's more common in sit-down restaurants than bars or cafés. Skipping a tip entirely is not considered rude.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping can confuse or even offend staff, since excellent service is already the norm and isn't paid for separately."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% at restaurants is the norm and servers rely on it as part of their income."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"A service charge is built into the bill by law, but rounding up or leaving small change for good service is appreciated."}],"sources":[{"title":"What are the rules on tipping in Spain?","url":"https://www.thelocal.es/20200109/dining-in-spain-how-much-should-you-tip","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Spanish Culture — Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/spanish-culture/spanish-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"To tip or not to tip? The ins and outs of tipping in Spain","url":"https://www.fascinatingspain.com/articulo/spanish-culture/tips-tipping-in-spain/20230412101036070482.html","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Safe, positive gesture","confidence":"high","tip":"Use it freely to signal approval or that everything's fine; it's one of the safest hand gestures to use in Spain.","explanation":"The thumbs-up is understood in Spain much as across most of Western Europe and Latin America: approval, agreement, or 'all good.' It's used casually among friends, service staff, and even in business settings without negative connotation, unlike in Greece or parts of the Middle East where it can be read as vulgar.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Greece","verdict":"rude","note":"Historically read as an insulting gesture roughly equivalent to showing a middle finger, though younger Greeks increasingly use it to mean 'OK'."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"In some traditional Gulf Arab contexts it can be seen as a crude or insulting gesture, so it's safer to avoid it in formal or conservative settings."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"polite","note":"Germans commonly use the raised thumb to mean the number 'one' when counting, in addition to its approval meaning."}],"sources":[{"title":"Understanding European Gestures","url":"https://www.ricksteves.com/travel-tips/sightseeing/european-gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Offensive International Travel Hand Gestures","url":"https://www.globalrescue.com/es/common/blog/detail/offensive-international-travel-hand-gestures/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Not taboo, but keep it tidy","confidence":"medium","tip":"Don't worry about which way your feet point, but keep your shoes or slippers on and your feet off the furniture when visiting someone's home.","explanation":"Spain has no cultural taboo against showing the soles of your feet the way Thailand or Gulf countries do — there's no belief that feet are spiritually 'low' or impure. That said, Spaniards are particular about feet generally: going barefoot indoors, even in someone's own home, is seen as a bit odd, and propping feet up on furniture in someone's house still comes across as overly casual.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"Feet are considered the lowest, most impure part of the body, so pointing soles at a person, a monk, or a Buddha image is genuinely offensive."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"There's no strong taboo, but propping feet on furniture or a table in someone's home can read as disrespectful or overly casual."},{"country":"Egypt","verdict":"rude","note":"Feet are considered unclean, so showing the soles of your shoes to someone, especially elders, is seen as insulting."}],"sources":[{"title":"13 things foreigners do that make Spaniards feel really awkward","url":"https://www.thelocal.es/20170407/things-foreigners-do-that-make-spaniards-feel-awkward","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Syracuse University Madrid homestay FAQ","url":"https://suabroad.syr.edu/madrid/wp-json/wp/v2/pages/71","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"No taboo, use either hand","confidence":"high","tip":"Eat normally with either hand; just keep both hands visible on the table rather than in your lap, which is the real Spanish table-manners rule to know.","explanation":"Unlike in parts of the Middle East, Africa, and South Asia where the left hand is reserved for hygiene and seen as unclean, Spain has no such taboo. Spanish table manners follow the continental European style — fork in the left hand, knife in the right, both hands visible on the table — but switching hands or using your left hand for bread or finger food causes no offense.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is traditionally reserved for hygiene, so eating or passing food with it is considered unclean."},{"country":"Egypt","verdict":"rude","note":"As in much of the Arab world, the left hand is used for washing, so eating or offering food with it is frowned upon."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Islamic etiquette calls for eating and passing dishes with the right hand only."}],"sources":[{"title":"Spanish table manners: do you know the rules?","url":"https://notesfromspain.com/2007/03/29/spanish-table-manners-do-you-know-the-rules/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"GW Blog Abroad — Spain","url":"https://blogs.gwu.edu/gwblogabroad/?p=5479","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine for kids, not for adults","confidence":"medium","tip":"Stick to a touch on the arm or shoulder to show warmth; save head pats for children.","explanation":"Patting a child or a very close family member's head is normal and affectionate in Spain, but doing it to an adult — even a friend — can come across as condescending, since it treats them like a child or pet. Spaniards are generally tactile and comfortable with closer personal space than Northern Europeans, but that familiarity is expressed through touches on the arm, shoulder, or back, not the head.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body, so touching anyone's head, including children, is a serious breach of etiquette."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head is common, but doing it to an adult, especially an elder or stranger, can be seen as patronizing."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"As in Spain, head-patting is normal for children but can feel condescending or overly familiar if done to an adult."}],"sources":[{"title":"Spanish Etiquette: The Ultimate Guide for Expats","url":"https://www.uniplaces.com/city-explorer/spanish-etiquette-the-ultimate-guide-for-expats-in-2025/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected, especially in business","confidence":"high","tip":"Hold eye contact when greeting and during conversation, but soften it rather than staring fixedly, which can come across as confrontational.","explanation":"Direct eye contact is valued in Spain as a sign of honesty, confidence, and engagement, both socially and in business meetings. Avoiding it or checking your phone while someone is speaking is seen as rude or disinterested, while steady (not aggressive) eye contact during a handshake or negotiation signals sincerity.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can feel intimidating or disrespectful; brief eye contact with glances away is more typical."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"caution","note":"Sustained eye contact with elders or superiors can be read as defiant in a culture that values hierarchical respect."},{"country":"Netherlands","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct eye contact is expected and tied to the Dutch value of blunt, honest communication, much like in Spain."}],"sources":[{"title":"Commisceo Global — Spain Business Etiquette Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/spain-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Spanish Etiquette: The Ultimate Guide for Expats","url":"https://www.uniplaces.com/city-explorer/spanish-etiquette-the-ultimate-guide-for-expats-in-2025/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Not expected, follow the host","confidence":"medium","tip":"Watch what your host does — if they're in slippers and offer you a pair, take the hint; otherwise it's fine to keep your shoes on.","explanation":"Removing shoes when entering someone's home isn't a strict guest custom in Spain the way it is in Japan or Korea — most Spanish homes have tile or hardwood floors, and families typically switch into slippers at home for comfort rather than out of a rule for visitors. As a guest, you're not expected to take your shoes off unless your host does, or asks you to.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes before stepping past the genkan (entryway) is mandatory and deeply ingrained."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Norms vary by household; some families expect shoes off, others don't mind, so it's best to follow the host's lead."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"polite","note":"As in Japan, removing shoes before entering a home is a firmly established, non-negotiable custom."}],"sources":[{"title":"Why Some Countries Take Off Their Shoes Indoors","url":"https://www.remitly.com/blog/lifestyle-culture/why-some-countries-take-off-their-shoes-indoors/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Syracuse University Madrid homestay FAQ","url":"https://suabroad.syr.edu/madrid/wp-json/wp/v2/pages/71","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Common but can seem blunt","confidence":"low","tip":"When indicating a person, use an open hand rather than a single finger, especially with someone you don't know well.","explanation":"Pointing directly at a person with your index finger is widely used in Spain but can come across as blunt or aggressive, especially when aimed at someone during a disagreement — soft etiquette advice favors gesturing with an open hand instead. Unlike in East Asia, there's no strong, well-documented taboo against it, so it's more a matter of manners than a hard rule.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with a single finger is considered impolite; gesturing with an open hand is the norm."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is seen as offensive; people often point with the thumb instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing with the index finger is common and unremarkable in everyday casual conversation."}],"sources":[{"title":"Insider's Guide to Understanding Spanish Hand Gestures","url":"https://worldstrides.com/blog/2012/02/insiders-guide-understanding-spanish-hand-gestures/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Offensive International Travel Hand Gestures","url":"https://www.globalrescue.com/es/common/blog/detail/offensive-international-travel-hand-gestures/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Common and widely accepted","confidence":"high","tip":"Don't hold back on hand-holding, hugging, or a kiss hello/goodbye in public — it's completely normal, just default to a handshake in business settings.","explanation":"Spain is a famously tactile, affectionate culture — couples kissing, hugging, and holding hands in public draws little to no attention, and this applies across age groups and to same-sex couples as well. Compared to the more reserved norms in the US or parts of Northern Europe, Spaniards are generally relaxed about public affection, though it's dialed back in formal settings like a business meeting.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Public affection beyond handholding is rare and can draw disapproving looks, even between married couples."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek-kiss greetings are standard and couples kissing or embracing in public is common and unremarkable."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Public displays of affection between unrelated men and women are against the law and can lead to real legal consequences."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Economics and Social Politics of Spanish PDA","url":"https://berkleycenter.georgetown.edu/posts/the-economics-and-social-politics-of-spanish-pda","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Barcelona's Burning Passion","url":"https://thecictravels.cic.sc.edu/barcelonas-burning-passion/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Be on time for business","confidence":"medium","tip":"Arrive on time (or a few minutes early) for a business meeting, but don't be surprised or offended if your Spanish counterpart shows up a little late — that flexibility doesn't extend to you as the visitor.","explanation":"For a BUSINESS meeting, punctuality is expected and genuinely valued in Spain — guides consistently advise arriving on time or a few minutes early, even though Spanish colleagues themselves sometimes run five or ten minutes behind. Arriving deliberately late sends the wrong message about professionalism. The real flexibility in Spanish time culture shows up socially: dinners, parties, and casual get-togethers routinely start later than planned, and nobody minds.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is strictly observed; arriving even five minutes late to a meeting is seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Arriving late to any meeting is a serious breach of etiquette; arriving early is the norm."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Similar to Spain's social norms, a 10-15 minute delay is common and unremarkable, especially outside formal business settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"A short guide to business etiquette in Spain","url":"https://www.lingoda.com/blog/en/business-etiquette-spain/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Business etiquette in Spain and other Spanish-speaking countries","url":"https://www.spanishexpress.co.uk/business-etiquette-in-spain-and-other-spanish-speaking-countries/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Spanish Culture — Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/spanish-culture/spanish-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Declining politely is fine","confidence":"medium","tip":"It's fine to decline or say you don't care for something; if your host insists, a warm 'de verdad, gracias' (really, thank you) usually settles it.","explanation":"A host offering food repeatedly is a sign of Spanish hospitality, and an initial polite decline is a normal part of the back-and-forth rather than a serious offense. Unlike in cultures where refusal is taken as a rejection of hospitality, Spaniards generally don't mind if you say you're full or that you don't like a particular dish — being upfront about it is actually valued over pretending to enjoy something you don't.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"A polite, simple decline is generally accepted and not seen as rejecting the host's hospitality."},{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food is often seen as rejecting hospitality outright; hosts will typically insist multiple times before accepting a no."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"Declining food can seem impolite unless paired with a gracious excuse and a compliment to the cook."}],"sources":[{"title":"Syracuse University Madrid homestay FAQ","url":"https://suabroad.syr.edu/madrid/wp-json/wp/v2/pages/71","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Spanish Culture — Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/spanish-culture/spanish-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Generally fine, minor dispute","confidence":"medium","tip":"Feel free to use the OK sign to mean 'all good' or 'perfect' — it's not read as vulgar in Spain the way it can be in Brazil or Turkey.","explanation":"The OK sign (thumb and forefinger circle) is understood in Spain the same way as in most of Western Europe — meaning 'fine', 'good', or 'perfect' — and does not carry the vulgar meaning it has in Brazil, Turkey, or parts of the Middle East. A handful of travel-advice lists lump Spain in with Latin America on this gesture, but more detailed sources specifically note Spain (and Portugal) as an exception where it isn't considered offensive.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The same gesture is a well-known vulgar insult, famously causing embarrassment for foreign visitors who used it unknowingly."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"It can be read as a crude insult referencing a derogatory stereotype, so it's best avoided there."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Commonly understood to mean 'OK' or sometimes 'money,' and used positively in everyday situations."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikivoyage — Gestures","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Gestures","kind":"community"},{"title":"Offensive International Travel Hand Gestures","url":"https://www.globalrescue.com/es/common/blog/detail/offensive-international-travel-hand-gestures/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Not complimentary, but not scandalous","confidence":"medium","tip":"Wind noodles onto your fork and eat quietly; a little noise at a casual dinner won't scandalize anyone, but there's no upside to slurping either.","explanation":"Spanish meals are lively and conversational — loud talk across the table is completely normal — but that's different from making noise while eating. General table manners in Spain (closed-mouth chewing, proper manners expected at the family table) suggest audible slurping would be seen as a minor lapse rather than fine the way it can be in Japan or China. It's unlikely to cause real offense, especially outside a formal setting, but it's not a complimentary gesture either.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles, especially ramen or soba, is seen as a compliment to the chef and even said to enhance the flavor."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles or soup is common and generally read as enjoying the meal rather than being impolite."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Audibly slurping pasta is considered poor table manners and is something Italians generally avoid."}],"sources":[{"title":"Spanish table manners: do you know the rules?","url":"https://notesfromspain.com/2007/03/29/spanish-table-manners-do-you-know-the-rules/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Syracuse University Madrid homestay FAQ","url":"https://suabroad.syr.edu/madrid/wp-json/wp/v2/pages/71","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Standard between friends","confidence":"high","tip":"Lean in and touch cheeks on both sides with a light kissing sound — no actual lip contact — and default to a handshake in a first business meeting or with men you don't know well.","explanation":"Two light cheek-to-cheek kisses are the standard greeting in Spain between women, and between men and women, once you're past a first, formal introduction. In business or when meeting someone for the first time, a handshake is the norm, and it tends to stay that way for men meeting men, though colleagues often shift to cheek kisses once a working relationship becomes personal.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Cheek kissing isn't customary; a bow or handshake is the standard greeting instead."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"La bise is standard, though the number of kisses (typically two to four) varies by region."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing between men who are close friends is common, but any greeting involving touch between unrelated men and women in public is generally avoided."}],"sources":[{"title":"Getting the kisses wrong","url":"https://www.thelocal.es/20210329/the-most-common-mistakes-foreigners-make-when-greeting-people-in-spain","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Spanish Social Etiquette","url":"https://www.expatica.com/es/living/integration/spanish-humor-108526","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Better to use an open hand","confidence":"low","tip":"Beckon with your palm up and fingers (or whole hand) curling toward you, rather than a single crooked index finger.","explanation":"The conventional way to beckon someone in Spain is palm-up with a scooping wave of the fingers or whole hand, not a single curled index finger. Using the curled-finger version isn't a dramatic insult the way it is in places like the Philippines, but it can read as brusque or over-familiar, particularly toward someone older or a stranger, so the open-hand version is the safer, more natural choice.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a single finger is considered impolite and is associated with calling animals; a palm-down wave is used instead."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"This gesture is strongly associated with calling dogs and is considered a serious insult when directed at a person."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"It's less charged than in some other cultures, but can still come across as overly familiar or dismissive rather than a polite way to call someone over."}],"sources":[{"title":"Insider's Guide to Understanding Spanish Hand Gestures","url":"https://worldstrides.com/blog/2012/02/insiders-guide-understanding-spanish-hand-gestures/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Offensive International Travel Hand Gestures","url":"https://www.globalrescue.com/es/common/blog/detail/offensive-international-travel-hand-gestures/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcome, keep it modest","confidence":"medium","tip":"For a dinner invitation, bring a good bottle of wine, chocolates, or a small bouquet — avoid chrysanthemums, white lilies, dahlias, red roses, and bouquets of exactly 13 flowers, since these carry funeral or superstitious associations.","explanation":"Gift-giving is appreciated in Spain in both business and social settings, but the emphasis is on quality and thoughtfulness over expense — an overly lavish business gift can look like you're trying to influence someone rather than build a relationship. If you're invited to someone's home for dinner, bring wine, chocolates, or flowers; just steer clear of flowers tied to funerals or superstition.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized, with careful wrapping and presenting gifts with both hands as part of the custom."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Business gifts are often seen as unnecessary or even suspicious; if given at all, keep them small and practical."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"polite","note":"Gifts are welcome but should avoid alcohol and pork products in keeping with Islamic customs, and are best offered with the right hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Commisceo Global — Spain Business Etiquette Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/spain-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Don't Make These 18 Gift Giving Mistakes Abroad","url":"https://www.globelink.co.uk/articles/travel-information/dont-make-these-18-gift-giving-mistakes-abroad.html","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Russia":{"slug":"russia","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcomed but not mandatory","confidence":"medium","tip":"Leave about 10% in cash directly with the server, since card terminals often can't add a tip and you should check whether a service charge is already included.","explanation":"Tipping in Russian restaurants is appreciated but remains voluntary rather than a strict social rule, with roughly 5-10% being a common urban norm for good service. Some restaurants already add a service charge to the bill, and many locals only tip extra when the service was genuinely excellent.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is considered unnecessary or even insulting since good service is expected without extra payment."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is the strong social norm and expected for sit-down restaurant service."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"A service charge is usually included by law, so tipping is optional and small change is the norm rather than a large extra tip."}],"sources":[{"title":"Moscow Lifehack: What's the Deal With Tipping","url":"https://www.themoscowtimes.com/archive/moscow-lifehack-whats-the-deal-with-tipping","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Global Tipping Guide - Russia","url":"https://www.arabianbusiness.com/lists/global-tipping-guide-how-gulf-states-compare-512703-htmlitemid512689","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Normal, friendly approval sign","confidence":"medium","tip":"Use a thumbs-up freely in casual situations; it will be read as approval, not as an insult.","explanation":"In Russia the thumbs-up is used much like in most of Europe and North America, as a casual, friendly sign of approval or 'good job.' A number of generic travel listicles claim it's offensive in Russia by lumping it in with countries like Iran, but Russia-focused sources describe it as an everyday positive gesture with no offensive meaning.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"The thumbs-up is a well-documented obscene insult, equivalent to the middle finger."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Thumbs-up can carry a crude meaning in some contexts and is best avoided with strangers."},{"country":"Greece","verdict":"caution","note":"Some Greeks, especially older generations, associate the gesture with an old sailors' insult, though it's increasingly read as positive among younger people."}],"sources":[{"title":"Russian cultural gestures and body language, realistically explained","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/russian-cultural-gestures-body-language-realistic","kind":"community"},{"title":"15 Insulting Gestures in Different Cultures","url":"https://www.lingoda.com/blog/en/15-insulting-gestures-in-different-cultures/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Mildly careless, not taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep your feet on the floor and off furniture or seats, especially if they'd be pointed toward another person.","explanation":"Showing the soles of your feet in Russia isn't treated as a serious insult the way it is in Thailand — it reads more as careless or inconsiderate, since feet are associated with street dirt. Propping your feet on furniture, armrests, or an opposite seat on public transport is the main thing locals object to.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The foot is the lowest part of the body, and pointing the sole at someone is a grave insult."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Showing the soles of your feet is generally not offensive, though it can read as very casual."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the sole of your foot to someone is considered disrespectful, as the foot is seen as unclean."}],"sources":[{"title":"Russian Etiquette 3D","url":"https://blogs.transparent.com/russian/russian-etiquette-3d/","kind":"community"},{"title":"5 Things NOT to do in Russia","url":"https://coffeeandcaminos.com/travel/guide-to-russian-etiquette/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"No taboo, Continental style","confidence":"high","tip":"Hold your fork in your left hand and your knife in your right in the Continental style, rather than switching the fork to your right hand between bites as in American-style dining.","explanation":"Russia follows Continental European table manners, where the fork stays in the left hand and the knife in the right throughout the meal — there is no cultural rule against using the left hand at the table. This differs from South Asian or Middle Eastern customs, where the left hand is considered unclean and avoided for eating.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Eating with the left hand is considered impolite since the left hand is reserved for hygiene tasks."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is considered unclean and should not be used for eating or passing food."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Food and drink should be handled only with the right hand, as the left is reserved for personal hygiene."}],"sources":[{"title":"9 Countries With Dining Etiquette Rules That May Surprise You","url":"https://www.wasserstrom.com/blog/2021/06/28/9-countries-with-dining-etiquette-rules-that-may-surprise-you/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Social etiquette","url":"https://expatica.com/ru/living/integration/russian-etiquette-106460","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Informal, can seem patronizing","confidence":"low","tip":"Avoid patting an adult's head unless you're close friends or family; a handshake or a pat on the shoulder is a safer default.","explanation":"Unlike Buddhist cultures such as Thailand, where the head is considered spiritually sacred, Russian culture has no documented religious or folk taboo against touching the head. That said, patting an adult on the head is still generally seen as overly familiar or condescending outside of close relationships, much as it would be in most Western countries.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body, and touching it is a major taboo."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Touching an adult's head is generally avoided due to spiritual beliefs about the head, though patting a child's head is common."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting someone on the head can read as condescending outside of close friendships or family."}],"sources":[{"title":"Russian Culture: Do's and Don'ts","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/russian-culture/russian-culture-do-s-and-don-ts","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Non-verbal confusion #2: Once more unto the breach","url":"https://trans-siberian.co.uk/blog/russian-gestures/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Signals honesty and respect","confidence":"medium","tip":"Make steady eye contact during greetings and toasts; it's expected there and signals honesty rather than confrontation.","explanation":"Maintaining direct eye contact in Russia — during a handshake, in conversation, and especially when clinking glasses for a toast — is read as a sign of sincerity, and looking away can come across as evasive. This applies in both everyday social situations and business settings.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can feel intimidating, especially with superiors; brief glances are more common."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct eye contact is expected in business as a sign of confidence and honesty."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Eye contact between unrelated men and women is typically kept brief out of modesty."}],"sources":[{"title":"Social etiquette","url":"https://expatica.com/ru/living/integration/russian-etiquette-106460","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Non-verbal confusion #2: Once more unto the breach","url":"https://trans-siberian.co.uk/blog/russian-gestures/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Standard, expected guest practice","confidence":"medium","tip":"Remove your shoes just inside the door unless your host specifically tells you to keep them on, and accept any slippers offered.","explanation":"Taking your shoes off when entering a Russian home is standard practice, largely to keep floors clean of mud, snow, and street grime, especially during winter. Hosts commonly offer guests slippers (tapochki) to wear instead, and it's polite to accept them.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoe removal is mandatory and ritualized, with separate slippers often used just for the bathroom."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal varies by household; many hosts don't expect it unless asked."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe-removal customs vary by household and aren't as consistently expected as in Russia or Japan."}],"sources":[{"title":"Etiquette in Russia","url":"https://www.frommers.com/destinations/russia/in-depth/etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"What to Expect When Visiting a Russian Home: Hospitality Dos and Don'ts","url":"https://www.polyglottistlanguageacademy.com/language-culture-travelling-blog/2025/4/13/what-to-expect-when-visiting-a-russian-home-hospitality-dos-and-donts","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Can come across as aggressive","confidence":"medium","tip":"Gesture with an open hand or nod your head toward someone instead of pointing your index finger at them.","explanation":"Pointing directly at a person with your index finger is generally seen as impolite and overly aggressive in Russia, similar to many European cultures, since it can feel accusatory rather than neutral. It's more acceptable to point at objects or directions than directly at a person.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger at a person is considered very impolite; an open hand is used instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing with the index finger is common and generally acceptable in casual settings."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is considered impolite; the thumb is used instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Non-verbal confusion #2: Once more unto the breach","url":"https://trans-siberian.co.uk/blog/russian-gestures/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Six Surprising International Etiquette Rules","url":"https://exhibitcitynews.com/six-surprising-intl-etiquette-rules/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Moderate affection generally tolerated","confidence":"medium","tip":"Casual affection like hand-holding is generally fine in cities, but same-sex couples should be aware Russian law specifically targets public same-sex affection with fines or worse.","explanation":"Hand-holding or a quick kiss between partners is commonly seen in Russian cities, and surveys suggest Russians have grown more tolerant of public affection over time. However, Russia's 'gay propaganda' laws impose real legal penalties specifically on public displays of affection between same-sex couples, so the risk is significantly higher for LGBTQ+ travelers.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"rude","note":"Public displays of affection can lead to legal penalties, even between married couples."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing as a greeting and casual PDA like hand-holding are widely accepted and common."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"PDA is generally avoided in public; even hand-holding can feel immodest in more formal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Kissing Games in Moscow","url":"https://www.rferl.org/a/kissing_games_in_moscow/24274661.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Russia is actually considering jailing gays who kiss in public","url":"https://thepinknews.com/2016/01/18/russia-is-actually-considering-jailing-gays-who-kiss-in-public","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"rude","label":"Disrespectful, especially in business","confidence":"medium","tip":"Arrive on time or a few minutes early for business meetings regardless of whether your Russian counterparts do the same.","explanation":"For business meetings, foreign visitors are expected to arrive on time even though a Russian counterpart may themselves show up late without apologizing — your own punctuality still signals seriousness and respect. In casual social settings, a short delay of around 10-15 minutes is usually tolerated without much concern.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"Arriving 10-15 minutes late is often acceptable for business meetings, especially in less formal settings."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Arriving even a minute late is considered disrespectful; punctuality is paramount."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Meetings may start later than scheduled, but foreign visitors are still expected to arrive on time."}],"sources":[{"title":"International Business Etiquette: Russia","url":"https://www.cyborlink.com/besite/russia.htm","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Doing business in Russia","url":"https://entrepreneurhandbook.co.uk/doing-business-in-russia/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"rude","label":"Insulting to host's hospitality","confidence":"medium","tip":"Accept at least a small portion of what's offered, or if you truly can't, decline warmly and explain why rather than simply saying no.","explanation":"Russian hospitality places strong importance on offering food and drink, and turning down a toast or a drink in particular can be seen as rejecting the host's friendship and goodwill. Refusing food outright, especially in a home setting, can come across as distrustful or ungrateful even if that's not the intent.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Politely refusing food with a clear excuse is generally acceptable, though accepting is still appreciated."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food from a host, especially in a family setting, can be seen as an insult to their cooking and hospitality."},{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food is considered impolite and can hurt the host's feelings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Social etiquette","url":"https://expatica.com/ru/living/integration/russian-etiquette-106460","kind":"reference"},{"title":"9 Countries With Dining Etiquette Rules That May Surprise You","url":"https://www.wasserstrom.com/blog/2021/06/28/9-countries-with-dining-etiquette-rules-that-may-surprise-you/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Evidence weak, stay cautious","confidence":"low","tip":"When in doubt, say 'хорошо' (khorosho, 'good') or give a thumbs-up instead of the OK sign.","explanation":"Evidence on the OK hand sign in Russia is thin and inconsistent: a few general 'gestures that offend around the world' round-ups list it as vulgar there, but there's no strong, Russia-specific documentation confirming it's a widely recognized insult. Because the evidence is weak in either direction, it's safer to avoid relying on the gesture than to assume it's completely neutral.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The OK sign is a well-documented, highly offensive gesture there."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture can carry a vulgar, derogatory connotation in some contexts."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Widely understood as 'OK' or to represent money, with no negative connotation."}],"sources":[{"title":"17 Gestures That Can Cause Offense Around the World","url":"https://www.shermanstravel.com/advice/18-gestures-that-can-cause-offense-around-the-world","kind":"reference"},{"title":"10 Common Gestures Easily Misunderstood Abroad","url":"https://matadornetwork.com/abroad/10-common-gestures-easily-misunderstood-abroad/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Not customary, keep it quiet","confidence":"low","tip":"Eat soup and noodle dishes quietly with a spoon or fork rather than slurping, in line with general European-style table manners.","explanation":"Russian table manners follow a quieter, Continental European style where eating audibly isn't generally appreciated, but noodle- or soup-slurping specifically isn't documented as a distinct, well-known Russian rule, since it isn't a noodle-centric food culture the way East Asia is. It's best understood as part of a general 'eat quietly' expectation rather than a specific taboo about noodles.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is a sign of appreciation and enjoyment, especially for ramen or soba."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping is often acceptable and can signal that the food is delicious, though norms vary by region."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta is considered poor table manners."}],"sources":[{"title":"9 Countries With Dining Etiquette Rules That May Surprise You","url":"https://www.wasserstrom.com/blog/2021/06/28/9-countries-with-dining-etiquette-rules-that-may-surprise-you/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Entertaining Etiquette: The Dining Traditions of the World","url":"https://www.sheknows.com/entertainment/articles/1074432/entertaining-etiquette-the-dining-traditions-of-the-world/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine for friends, not strangers","confidence":"medium","tip":"Default to a handshake when meeting someone for the first time, and only exchange cheek kisses if the other person initiates or you already know them well.","explanation":"Among people who already know each other — friends, family, and close colleagues — a cheek kiss, often paired with a hug, is a warm and normal Russian greeting, especially between women or when greeting a woman. For a first meeting or in formal settings, a handshake is expected instead, and initiating a cheek kiss there could come across as overly familiar.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing isn't a traditional greeting and can feel like an unexpected invasion of personal space; a bow is standard instead."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing (la bise) is a standard, expected greeting, though the number of kisses varies by region."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing is common between close male friends but avoided between unrelated men and women in public."}],"sources":[{"title":"Etiquette in Russia","url":"https://www.frommers.com/destinations/russia/in-depth/etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Social etiquette","url":"https://expatica.com/ru/living/integration/russian-etiquette-106460","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Reads as condescending, commanding","confidence":"low","tip":"To call someone over, use a palm-down hand with fingers waving inward rather than crooking a single finger.","explanation":"Curling your index finger to call someone over — the common Western 'come here' gesture — can come across as condescending or overly commanding in Russia, similar to summoning a child or a pet. Russians themselves more typically beckon with the palm facing down and fingers waving inward, rather than curling one finger upward.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with the index finger is considered very rude and used only for animals."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"This gesture can be seen as a serious insult or challenge."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Common in casual settings but can seem condescending in formal or professional contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"Non-verbal confusion #2: Once more unto the breach","url":"https://trans-siberian.co.uk/blog/russian-gestures/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Non-verbal confusion #3: International Incidents","url":"https://www.trans-siberian.co.uk/blog/russian-people-gestures/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcome, but mind the details","confidence":"medium","tip":"If giving flowers, pick an odd number and avoid yellow blooms, and skip knives or other sharp objects as gifts since they're said to symbolically 'cut' a relationship.","explanation":"Bringing flowers, chocolates, or a nice bottle when visiting a Russian home is a well-established, appreciated custom. The best-documented rule is about numbers, not color: give an odd number of flowers for happy occasions, since even numbers are specifically associated with funerals and graves; separately, yellow flowers carry their own distinct negative association with breakups or separation.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized; certain numbers and wrapping colors are avoided."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Business gifts are typically modest and practical rather than lavish."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"caution","note":"Avoid alcohol or pork-related items, and give with the right hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Social etiquette","url":"https://expatica.com/ru/living/integration/russian-etiquette-106460","kind":"reference"},{"title":"What's With All the Flowers in Russia?","url":"https://lidenz.com/flowers-in-russia/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Indonesia":{"slug":"indonesia","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Appreciated but optional","confidence":"medium","tip":"Check the bill for an already-included service charge; if there isn't one, rounding up or leaving a modest amount in cash for good service is a nice, optional gesture.","explanation":"Tipping is not obligatory in Indonesia, and many restaurants already add a 5-10% service charge to the bill. Leaving small change or rounding up is a welcomed, polite gesture for good service, especially in tourist-oriented restaurants in places like Jakarta or Bali. Small local warungs (eateries) generally neither expect nor require a tip.","regional_note":"Tipping is more common and expected in tourist hubs like Bali and Jakarta than at small local warungs or in rural areas.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping can be seen as insulting; excellent service is the baseline, not something extra to pay for."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"15-20% is the standard expected tip for sit-down restaurant service."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Service is included by law, but leaving small extra change for good service is a nice touch."}],"sources":[{"title":"Tipping in Indonesia","url":"https://www.rukita.co/stories/tipping-in-indonesia","kind":"community"},{"title":"Tipping in Bali: When and How Much to Tip","url":"https://www.baliholidaysecrets.com/tipping-in-bali/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Positive sign, widely accepted","confidence":"high","tip":"Use a thumbs-up freely for approval, and if you need to indicate a person or direction, point with your thumb rather than your index finger, as many Indonesians do.","explanation":"The thumbs-up is understood across Indonesia as a positive signal for 'good' or 'okay,' and no source found describes it as offensive in Java, Sumatra, or anywhere else in the country. It is a separate gesture from the well-documented Indonesian custom of pointing with the thumb (instead of the index finger) as a politeness norm. Both uses of the thumb are fine for visitors.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"Thumbs-up is considered an obscene insult, similar to the middle finger in the West."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Thumbs-up reads as a friendly 'good' or 'okay' signal."},{"country":"Greece","verdict":"caution","note":"Older or more traditional Greeks may still see it as rude, though it's increasingly read as positive among younger people."}],"sources":[{"title":"A Thumb Points the Way in Java","url":"https://www.csmonitor.com/1990/0412/dindo4.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Indonesian Culture - Gestures (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/indonesian-culture/indonesian-culture-gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Keep feet pointed away","confidence":"medium","tip":"Sit with your feet flat on the floor or with knees stacked rather than ankle-over-knee, and never rest your feet on tables or chairs.","explanation":"Indonesians traditionally regard the feet as the lowest and least respectful part of the body, so showing the soles of your feet to someone, resting your feet on furniture, or pointing your feet at a person is seen as disrespectful. The custom is rooted in broader Islamic and Southeast Asian etiquette around keeping feet out of view in social settings. It's treated as a genuine breach of manners rather than a minor slip.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The feet are the lowest body part; pointing the soles at someone or touching them with a foot is deeply offensive."},{"country":"Egypt","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the soles of your feet is a serious insult, as feet are seen as unclean."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Pointing feet at someone or at religious objects is considered poor manners, though less severe than in Southeast Asia."}],"sources":[{"title":"Indonesian Culture - Gestures (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/indonesian-culture/indonesian-culture-gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Indonesian Etiquette: How You Can Avoid Causing Offense","url":"https://www.worldnomads.com/explore/southeast-asia/indonesia/indonesia-etiquette-how-you-can-avoid-causing-offence","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"rude","label":"Always use your right hand","confidence":"high","tip":"Eat, pass dishes, and hand over money or gifts with your right hand; if you must use your left hand, a quick 'maaf, tangan kiri' (sorry, left hand) smooths it over.","explanation":"The left hand is traditionally considered unclean in Indonesia because it's associated with personal hygiene, so it should be avoided for eating, passing food, and giving or receiving items. This norm is especially strong given Indonesia's Muslim-majority population, where similar left-hand taboos are widespread. Visitors eating with their hands (common at many local meals) should use the right hand only.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is reserved for bathroom hygiene; always eat and pass items with the right hand."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is strictly avoided for eating and greeting; the right hand is used for all such interactions."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"There's no left-hand taboo, but chopstick etiquette and other table manners matter more."}],"sources":[{"title":"Why visitors to Indonesia should avoid using their left hand","url":"https://www.scmp.com/lifestyle/article/3035767/why-visitors-indonesia-should-avoid-using-their-left-hand-it-may-cause","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Indonesian Culture - Gestures (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/indonesian-culture/indonesian-culture-gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Never touch the head","confidence":"high","tip":"Avoid touching anyone's head, even affectionately with children; use a smile, a nod, or a light touch on the shoulder instead.","explanation":"The head is widely considered the most sacred part of the body in Indonesia, believed by many to be where the spirit resides, so touching someone else's head — including a child's — is seen as disrespectful. This view is reinforced by Islamic tradition in the country's Muslim-majority areas. Even a well-meaning pat can cause real offense.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the body's highest and most sacred point; touching it is a major faux pas."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head is sometimes tolerated, but touching an adult's head is seen as condescending."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Patting a child's head is often read as affectionate or encouraging."}],"sources":[{"title":"Etiquette in Indonesia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_Indonesia","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Indonesian Culture - Gestures (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/indonesian-culture/indonesian-culture-gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Keep it brief, not intense","confidence":"medium","tip":"With elders or senior colleagues, hold eye contact in short stretches and look away occasionally rather than maintaining a constant, steady gaze.","explanation":"In Indonesian business and social settings, brief or intermittent eye contact is generally preferred over sustained staring, especially with elders or superiors, where prolonged eye contact can come across as confrontational. Glancing down occasionally while listening is read as humility and respect rather than disinterest. Casual eye contact among peers is generally unremarkable.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged eye contact can read as confrontational; brief glances are more comfortable."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Steady eye contact is read as a sign of confidence and honesty."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Eye contact between men is normal, but between genders it is usually kept brief and minimal."}],"sources":[{"title":"How To Follow Business Etiquette in Indonesia","url":"https://clickup.com/p/business-etiquette/indonesia","kind":"community"},{"title":"Understanding Indonesian Business Culture","url":"https://emerhub.com/indonesia/understanding-indonesian-business-culture/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected before entering homes","confidence":"high","tip":"Remove your shoes at the door even if your host waves it off as unnecessary, and look for a pile of shoes at the entrance as your cue.","explanation":"Removing shoes before entering an Indonesian home is a widely observed sign of respect and cleanliness. Even if a host says it isn't necessary, that's often just politeness, and guests are still expected to take their shoes off. The custom extends to many mosques and temples, which require bare or socked feet to keep the space clean.","regional_note":"The custom also applies at many mosques and temples, which require visitors to remove shoes to keep the place of worship clean.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoe removal indoors is near-universal, often with dedicated indoor slippers."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal is optional and varies by household; many hosts don't expect it."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoes come off before entering homes and temples, though not usually in shops or offices."}],"sources":[{"title":"Basic Communications in Indonesia (Five Colleges)","url":"https://langmedia.fivecolleges.edu/resources/indonesia/basic-communications/greetings-and-partings","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Indonesian Culture - Etiquette (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/indonesian-culture/indonesian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Point with your thumb instead","confidence":"high","tip":"Point with your thumb or an open hand instead of your index finger, especially around elders or in more formal settings.","explanation":"Pointing at a person or object with the index finger is considered impolite and overly blunt in Indonesia, particularly in traditional Javanese custom. The widely used polite alternative is to point with the thumb, making a relaxed fist and extending the thumb toward the direction or object, or to gesture with an open hand. This matters most when addressing elders or people of higher status.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Index-finger pointing is impolite; an open hand or a nod is used instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Index-finger pointing is common and casual, though it can read as blunt in formal settings."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is impolite; people often use their chin or an open hand instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"A Thumb Points the Way in Java","url":"https://www.csmonitor.com/1990/0412/dindo4.html","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Indonesian Culture - Gestures (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/indonesian-culture/indonesian-culture-gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Keep it modest in public","confidence":"medium","tip":"Stick to handshakes, smiles, or at most brief hand-holding in public, and save anything more affectionate for private settings — and be extra cautious in Aceh.","explanation":"Indonesia is a conservative, Muslim-majority country where public affection beyond hand-holding is generally frowned upon and norms vary sharply by region. In Aceh, the only province enforcing Islamic sharia law, public affection between unmarried couples can be punished with public caning under the local criminal code. Separately, Indonesia's new national Criminal Code (KUHP), in force from January 2, 2026, criminalizes extramarital sex and cohabitation nationwide, though prosecution requires a formal complaint from a spouse, parent, or child — officials have said it is not intended to target tourists.","regional_note":"In Aceh, public affection between unmarried couples can be punished with public caning (up to around 100 strokes under the Qanun Jinayat) because the province enforces its own sharia-based criminal code.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Casual PDA, including kissing in public, is common and socially accepted."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Public affection between unrelated men and women is prohibited and can carry legal penalties."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"PDA is generally avoided in public; even hand-holding is often reserved for more private moments."}],"sources":[{"title":"Indonesia: Unmarried couples caned for public affection","url":"https://www.amnesty.org.uk/knowledge-hub/all-resources/indonesia-unmarried-couples-caned-public-affection-act-utmost-cruelty/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"New Criminal Code will come into effect on January 2, 2026","url":"https://www.idnfinancials.com/cn/news/60036/new-criminal-code-will-come-into-effect-on-january-2-2026","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Indonesia Criminal Code 2026: Sex Outside Marriage and What Tourists Need to Know","url":"https://eturbonews.com/indonesia-criminal-code-2026-sex-outside-marriage-and-what-tourists-need-to-know/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Punctuality valued, some flexibility","confidence":"medium","tip":"Arrive on time for formal or government meetings, and if you're running late, send a brief message ahead rather than showing up without warning.","explanation":"Formal meetings, especially with government officials, senior executives, or foreign partners, call for punctuality in Indonesia, while a more relaxed attitude toward time — often nicknamed 'jam karet' (rubber time) — is common in informal or social settings. Hierarchy also matters: being late to meet someone senior is viewed less favorably than minor lateness among peers. It's safest to aim for on-time arrival and give notice if delayed.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is strictly observed; arriving late is a serious breach of etiquette."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is highly valued; lateness is seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"Lateness of 15-30 minutes is common socially but less acceptable for formal business meetings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Indonesian Work Culture: A Practical Guide for Foreign Businesses and Founders","url":"https://www.zenind.com/help/post/indonesian-work-culture-a-practical-guide-for-foreign-businesses-and-founders","kind":"community"},{"title":"Indonesia Cultural Protocol: These Etiquette Rules Will Help Advance Your Business","url":"https://silkroadtoday.com/business/indonesia-cultural-protocol-etiquette-business","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Decline gently, not bluntly","confidence":"low","tip":"If you're full, accept a small token amount or decline warmly with thanks rather than a flat, blunt no.","explanation":"Indonesian social etiquette places a high value on indirectness and avoiding anything that could embarrass either party, so an abrupt, blunt refusal of food from a host can come across as impolite. Accepting a small portion, or declining warmly with thanks rather than a flat no, is the safer approach. Dedicated sourcing on food-refusal customs specifically was thin this session, so this is treated as an application of a broader, better-documented etiquette principle rather than a hard rule.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"A polite refusal with a proper excuse is acceptable, though accepting is still often preferred."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"caution","note":"Refusing food from a host, especially family, can feel like rejecting their hospitality."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Politely declining food, including for dietary reasons, is generally fine and not taken personally."}],"sources":[{"title":"Etiquette in Indonesia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_Indonesia","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Not the standard greeting","confidence":"medium","tip":"Offer a handshake with a slight nod as your default greeting, and let the other person initiate anything more familiar like a cheek kiss.","explanation":"The standard Indonesian greeting is a handshake, often with a slight nod, rather than a cheek kiss. Physical contact between genders is generally more reserved, consistent with the country's broader modesty norms, so cheek-kissing can come across as unexpectedly intimate, especially between people who aren't close or aren't the same gender. It's safest to follow the other person's lead rather than initiate a cheek kiss.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Kissing both cheeks is a standard, expected greeting among friends and acquaintances."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Bowing is the standard greeting; a cheek kiss would be unusual rather than a deliberate insult."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing is common between same-gender friends but avoided between men and women in public."}],"sources":[{"title":"Etiquette in Indonesia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etiquette_in_Indonesia","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Use a downward hand wave","confidence":"medium","tip":"Beckon with your whole hand, palm facing down, in a scratching or waving motion toward yourself, rather than curling a single finger.","explanation":"Indonesians typically beckon with a downward motion of the hand or fingers, rather than curling a single raised index finger toward themselves as is common in Western countries. A single-finger beckon can come across as impolite or overly commanding, similar to norms in several neighboring Southeast Asian countries. Using the whole hand, palm down, is the safer, more familiar gesture.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Finger-beckoning is considered rude and used mainly for animals; the whole hand, palm down, is used instead."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Finger-beckoning is considered highly offensive; a palm-down hand gesture is used instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Finger-beckoning is common and generally acceptable in casual settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Indonesian Culture - Gestures (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/indonesian-culture/indonesian-culture-gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Modest gifts, right hand","confidence":"medium","tip":"Offer and receive gifts with your right hand or both hands, keep them modest, and avoid alcohol or pork products for Muslim recipients.","explanation":"Gift-giving is a welcomed, relationship-building gesture in Indonesia, and gifts should be given and received with the right hand or both hands, since the left hand is considered unclean for offering items. Given Indonesia's large Muslim-majority population, it's also safest to avoid alcohol and pork products as gifts and to keep gifts modest rather than lavish. The halal-gift guidance is a reasonable extension of well-documented Islamic-influenced norms, though a source addressing gift-giving in Indonesia specifically wasn't found this session.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized; use both hands and avoid sets of four items (an unlucky number)."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts in formal business settings can seem inappropriate unless a relationship is already established."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"polite","note":"Gifts are appreciated but should be halal, avoiding alcohol or pork, and given with the right hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Why visitors to Indonesia should avoid using their left hand","url":"https://www.scmp.com/lifestyle/article/3035767/why-visitors-indonesia-should-avoid-using-their-left-hand-it-may-cause","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Indonesian Culture - Gestures (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/indonesian-culture/indonesian-culture-gestures","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Philippines":{"slug":"philippines","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Appreciated, often included already","confidence":"high","tip":"Check the bill for an existing 'service charge' line before tipping extra; if there isn't one, leave roughly 10% in cash directly with the server.","explanation":"Many larger Philippine restaurants already add a mandatory service charge (commonly 10%) under Republic Act 11360 (2019), which effectively functions as the tip, so no extra payment is expected on top of it. If no service charge appears on the bill, leaving about 10% in cash is a welcome but optional gesture of appreciation.","regional_note":"In tourist hubs like Boracay or Cebu, extra tipping on top of the service charge is more common than in small provincial eateries, where it is rare and sometimes refused.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is considered unnecessary and can embarrass staff, who may refuse the money since service is already included."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is standard and expected for sit-down restaurant service."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Service charge is included by law, but leaving small change for good service is a nice gesture."}],"sources":[{"title":"Tip or not to tip","url":"https://www.philstar.com/business/2024/06/19/2363792/tip-or-not-tip","kind":"reference"},{"title":"How much do Pinoys tip in restaurants?","url":"https://tribune.net.ph/2023/05/26/how-much-do-pinoys-tip-in-restaurants","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Casual, friendly sign of approval","confidence":"medium","tip":"Use it freely in casual settings, but back it up with words in formal business or official contexts.","explanation":"In the Philippines, a thumbs-up is understood much like in the US and most of the West — a casual, friendly sign of approval or 'okay.' It's common in informal interactions among friends, colleagues, and on social media, though it isn't a substitute for a verbal response in formal or solemn settings.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"The thumbs-up is considered an obscene insult, roughly equivalent to the middle finger elsewhere."},{"country":"Greece","verdict":"rude","note":"It can be read as a vulgar 'up yours'-style gesture rather than approval."},{"country":"Nigeria","verdict":"caution","note":"Generally positive, but using the left hand for it (or any gesture) is considered impolite."}],"sources":[{"title":"6 hand gestures in different cultures (& what they mean)","url":"https://blog.busuu.com/what-hand-gestures-mean-in-different-countries/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Hand Gestures 101: What They Mean and How to Use Them Effectively","url":"https://thespeakerlab.com/blog/hand-gesture-meanings/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Minor faux pas, not a real taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Don't worry about foot position in most everyday settings, but avoid propping your feet up on furniture or a table when visiting someone's home, especially around elders.","explanation":"Some travel guides warn against showing the soles of your feet in the Philippines by analogy with Thai, Middle Eastern, or South Asian customs, but there is little solid evidence Filipinos treat this as the serious taboo it is in Thailand or Egypt. At most it reads as a bit careless or too casual, similar to putting your feet on furniture in Western etiquette.","regional_note":"In the predominantly Muslim provinces of Mindanao, foot-related modesty norms closer to Islamic etiquette may be observed more strictly.","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"Feet are considered the lowest part of the body; pointing them at people or Buddha images is a serious offense."},{"country":"Egypt","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the sole of your shoe or foot is a grave insult, often linked with contempt."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Not a major issue, but feet on furniture or pointing soles at someone can read as overly casual in formal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Customs, Greetings and Etiquette: Getting It Right in the Philippines","url":"https://www.worktheworld.com.au/blog/customs-greetings-and-etiquette-getting-it-right-philippines","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Crossing Muddy Waters","url":"https://retiringtothephilippines.com/crossing-muddy-waters/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Contested, not a strict taboo","confidence":"low","tip":"At a kamayan-style meal, watch what your host does and mirror it; day-to-day, eating with a fork and spoon in either hand is completely normal and won't be noticed.","explanation":"Sources genuinely disagree here. Wikipedia's description of kamayan (traditional hand-eating) says the left hand is used to gather food from a shared dish while the right hand brings it to the mouth — not a strict 'left hand is unclean' rule. Other travel guides and forum posts claim Filipinos avoid the left hand entirely for eating and for passing objects, closer to South Asian or Gulf customs. In daily life, most Filipinos eat with a spoon and fork, so the question rarely even comes up.","regional_note":"In Muslim-majority communities in Mindanao, the stronger Islamic convention of reserving the left hand for hygiene and the right for eating is more likely to be observed.","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Eating or passing food with the left hand is considered unclean and is avoided in traditional dining."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Left-hand use is unremarkable as long as chopsticks are handled properly; there is no stigma."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is reserved strictly for hygiene; eating and passing food must be done with the right hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Filipino cuisine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filipino_cuisine","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Kamayan","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamayan","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine for kids, risky with adults","confidence":"low","tip":"Head-patting is generally fine with children you know, but avoid it with adults, especially someone older or more senior than you — a smile, nod, or handshake is safer.","explanation":"No Filipino-specific source found supports the idea that the head is sacred or houses the soul — that belief is well documented in Thai and Lao Buddhist culture, not Filipino culture. Patting a child's head in the Philippines is commonly an affectionate or encouraging gesture. Doing the same to an adult or stranger, though, can come across as condescending or overly familiar, since it treats the person like a child.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the highest and most sacred part of the body in Buddhist belief; touching it is a serious faux pas."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"An elder blessing a child by touching their head is common and positive, but a stranger touching an adult's head can be seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Patting someone on the head is generally seen as affectionate or playful, especially with children, though context matters."}],"sources":[{"title":"Filipino Etiquette Tips","url":"https://thesmartlocal.ph/filipino-etiquette-tips/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Philippines Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/philippines-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Normal, but soften with elders","confidence":"medium","tip":"Maintain normal, friendly eye contact in business and social settings; don't read a junior colleague's brief downward glance as disinterest — it's often a sign of respect.","explanation":"Direct eye contact is normal and expected in everyday Filipino greetings and conversation — Commisceo Global notes that greetings typically involve a handshake and direct eye contact. That said, many Filipinos briefly soften or break eye contact out of deference when speaking with elders, superiors, or during a scolding, reflecting the broader cultural value of hiya (sense of propriety/shame) rather than any rule against eye contact itself.","regional_note":"In more traditional or rural settings, deferential eye-lowering toward elders or superiors is more common; in urban, corporate, or English-speaking business settings, direct eye contact is the norm.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can feel intimidating; brief glances or looking slightly away is more comfortable."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Sustained eye contact is expected as a sign of confidence and engagement in business settings."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Acceptable between men, but between men and women it should be minimal and respectful."}],"sources":[{"title":"Philippines Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/philippines-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Filipino values","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filipino_values","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Respectful and commonly expected","confidence":"high","tip":"Look for a pile of shoes at the entrance, or simply ask your host whether to take your shoes off, to be safe.","explanation":"Removing shoes before entering a home is a widely observed sign of respect and cleanliness in the Philippines, especially in homes with polished or tiled floors. It's expected in most households, though some modern urban homes may be more relaxed about it.","regional_note":"In rural areas and traditional homes, removing shoes is nearly universal; in some modern condos or apartments in Metro Manila, hosts may be more lenient, but offering to remove them is still polite.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes is mandatory before entering any home, often with specific slippers for different rooms."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal varies widely by household; it's best to ask or observe what the host does."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoes are removed before entering homes and many temples, but not typically in shops or offices."}],"sources":[{"title":"Filipino Etiquette Tips","url":"https://thesmartlocal.ph/filipino-etiquette-tips/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Filipino Culture and Traditions: Complete Guide","url":"https://bestphilippinestravelguide.com/filipino-culture-and-traditions/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Avoid pointing at people","confidence":"medium","tip":"To indicate a person or a sensitive topic, purse your lips toward them instead of pointing; for objects or directions, an open hand or a regular finger-point is generally fine.","explanation":"Pointing directly at a PERSON with your index finger can come across as accusatory or confrontational in the Philippines, similar to many other cultures; the common polite alternative is 'pointing with the lips' (a slight purse of the lips toward the direction) or gesturing with an open hand. Pointing at objects or places with a finger is much more neutral and unlikely to cause offense.","regional_note":"Among younger, urbanized Filipinos, pointing with the index finger at a person is tolerated more among close friends, but it's still best avoided with elders or in formal settings.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger is considered impolite; an open hand or a nod toward the object/person is preferred."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing at a person with the index finger is considered offensive; many Indonesians also point with the lips or thumb."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing is common and generally acceptable in casual contexts, though pointing at a person can read as aggressive in formal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Filipino Culture and Traditions: Complete Guide","url":"https://bestphilippinestravelguide.com/filipino-culture-and-traditions/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Pointing","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointing","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Conservative, keep it modest","confidence":"medium","tip":"Limit physical affection to brief hand-holding, and save kissing or prolonged embraces for private settings, especially around elders or in conservative provinces.","explanation":"The Philippines' Catholic, Spanish-colonial heritage shaped a conservative dating culture in which couples are generally expected not to show overt public affection — Wikipedia's entry on Filipino courtship explicitly notes couples are 'expected to be conservative and not perform public displays of affection,' a trait traced to the Spanish-era 'Maria Clara' ideal of reserved comportment. Holding hands or a quick peck is usually fine; prolonged kissing or embracing in public is not.","regional_note":"In more conservative provinces (parts of Mindanao or rural Visayas), even hand-holding may draw attention in formal gatherings, while in Metro Manila younger couples are more openly affectionate.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Light PDA such as hand-holding or a kiss hello (la bise, though that's a greeting, not romantic PDA) is socially unremarkable."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Any PDA, including hand-holding between unmarried couples, is restricted and can draw legal consequences."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"PDA is uncommon in public; even hand-holding is often reserved for more private moments."}],"sources":[{"title":"Filipino courtship","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filipino_courtship","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Philippines Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/philippines-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine socially, not in business","confidence":"medium","tip":"For business meetings, arrive on time or a few minutes early; save the relaxed 'Filipino time' mindset for purely social occasions.","explanation":"Social gatherings in the Philippines often start later than the stated time — widely known as 'Filipino time' — and arriving around 30 minutes late to a purely social event is culturally tolerated. Business meetings are different: punctuality is increasingly expected, especially with multinational companies or foreign partners, so the stated meeting time should be treated as firm.","regional_note":"In more rural areas or traditional family-run businesses, a short grace period is more tolerated, but urban corporate settings expect punctuality.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is strictly observed; arriving even a few minutes late to a meeting is considered disrespectful."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"A short delay is often tolerated socially, but business meetings still expect punctuality."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality signals reliability; lateness is seen as unprofessional."}],"sources":[{"title":"Filipino Culture and Traditions: Complete Guide","url":"https://bestphilippinestravelguide.com/filipino-culture-and-traditions/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Philippines Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/philippines-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Hesitate, then accept a little","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you're full or need to decline, accept at least a small token portion or ask to take some home, rather than refusing outright.","explanation":"Filipino hosts typically insist more than once when offering food, so politely hesitating before accepting — rather than flatly refusing — is the expected dance. Declining outright, especially on a first visit, can come across as rejecting the host's hospitality and may cause quiet offense (hiya), but a brief 'no, I'm okay' before accepting a small portion is normal and not rude.","regional_note":"In more urban or Westernized households, a polite one-time refusal is usually accepted; in traditional or provincial settings, hosts are more likely to insist and expect you to eventually accept.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"A polite refusal is acceptable, but hosts may insist once or twice, so accepting a small amount is often easier."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food from a host, especially in Southern Italy, can be taken as an insult to their generosity and cooking."},{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food, particularly religious offerings (prasad) in a Hindu home, can offend the host's sense of hospitality."}],"sources":[{"title":"Filipino Culture and Traditions: Complete Guide","url":"https://bestphilippinestravelguide.com/filipino-culture-and-traditions/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Philippines Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/philippines-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Generally understood as positive","confidence":"medium","tip":"Use the OK sign casually among friends or in informal settings to indicate agreement or that something is fine.","explanation":"The OK hand sign (thumb and forefinger forming a circle) carries its common Western meaning of 'fine' or 'good' in the Philippines, with no documented negative association like it has in Brazil or Turkey. As with most casual gestures, it's best reserved for informal rather than solemn or formal contexts.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"Considered an obscene gesture, similar to showing the middle finger elsewhere."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"Can be read as a vulgar insult, similar to an extended middle finger."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Commonly understood to mean 'money' or 'coin' as well as 'OK'; generally positive."}],"sources":[{"title":"OK gesture","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OK_gesture","kind":"reference"},{"title":"6 hand gestures in different cultures (& what they mean)","url":"https://blog.busuu.com/what-hand-gestures-mean-in-different-countries/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Not a compliment here, but minor","confidence":"low","tip":"Try to eat soup-based noodle dishes like mami or batchoy quietly with a spoon, saving enthusiastic slurping for places where it's a compliment to the chef.","explanation":"No Philippines-specific source was found that directly addresses slurping noodles. Unlike in Japan or Korea, where slurping signals enjoyment, Filipino table manners lean more Western — a legacy of Spanish and American colonial influence that established spoon-and-fork dining as the norm — so quiet eating is generally seen as more refined. This is a soft social preference rather than a hard rule, and it's rarely treated as a real breach, especially among family or close friends.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is a compliment to the chef and is thought to enhance the flavor."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping is common and shows appreciation, though less ritualized than in Japan."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta is considered poor table manners."}],"sources":[{"title":"Table manners","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_manners","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Filipino cuisine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filipino_cuisine","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Common among friends and family","confidence":"medium","tip":"If greeting a Filipino woman (or a man greeting a woman) you already know, lean in for a light cheek-to-cheek touch without actually kissing the skin, often with a soft 'mwah' sound.","explanation":"Cheek-to-cheek greeting (locally called 'beso-beso') is common among Filipino women and between men and women who know each other socially, though less common between two men, who typically shake hands or pat each other on the back. It's a sign of warmth reserved for people you already know, not usually used in a first, formal introduction.","regional_note":"In more conservative or rural areas, and in Muslim-majority regions like parts of Mindanao, cheek kissing between unrelated men and women may be avoided.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing isn't customary and can feel overly familiar to a Japanese counterpart; a bow or handshake is the standard greeting."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"La bise (cheek kissing) is a standard greeting among friends and acquaintances, with the number of kisses varying by region."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing between men is common, but between unrelated men and women it is avoided in public."}],"sources":[{"title":"Filipino Etiquette Tips","url":"https://thesmartlocal.ph/filipino-etiquette-tips/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Filipino Culture and Traditions: Complete Guide","url":"https://bestphilippinestravelguide.com/filipino-culture-and-traditions/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Seen as demeaning, not illegal","confidence":"medium","tip":"To call someone over, extend your hand palm-down and wave your fingers toward yourself in a scratching motion, instead of curling one finger upward.","explanation":"Curling the index finger to call someone over is strongly associated in the Philippines with calling animals, not people, so using it on a person — especially an elder or stranger — reads as demeaning and disrespectful. A widely repeated claim that this gesture is 'punishable by law' in the Philippines appears to be an internet myth: no actual statute or credible legal source backs it up. The offense is a matter of social custom, not criminal law.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a single finger is considered vulgar and animal-like; a palm-down hand wave is used instead."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a finger is seen as condescending and is associated with calling dogs."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Common in casual contexts, but can be perceived as aggressive or dismissive in formal or professional settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"List of gestures","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"6 hand gestures in different cultures (& what they mean)","url":"https://blog.busuu.com/what-hand-gestures-mean-in-different-countries/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcome, but keep it modest","confidence":"medium","tip":"Bring a modest, well-wrapped gift (local sweets, fruit, or a small specialty item) when invited to a Filipino home or for a first business meeting, and don't take it personally if it isn't opened right away.","explanation":"Giving a thoughtful, modest gift helps build the personal rapport ('pakikisama') that underlies good relationships in the Philippines, whether visiting someone's home or after an initial business meeting. Avoid anything expensive enough to look like a bribe, and don't be surprised if it's set aside to open later rather than in front of you — gifts are often opened privately.","regional_note":"In Muslim-majority communities in Mindanao, avoid alcohol or pork-based gifts and favor halal-friendly items instead.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized; gifts are presented and received with both hands, often with a polite initial refusal before accepting."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts are less central to business dealings; if given, keep them small and practical."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts are appreciated but should avoid alcohol, pork, or anything with intimate connotations; use the right hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Philippines Guide","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/philippines-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Filipino Superstitions","url":"https://filipiknow.net/filipino-superstitions/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Egypt":{"slug":"egypt","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Baksheesh is expected, flexible","confidence":"medium","tip":"Tip roughly 10% of the bill in cash directly to the server, even if a service charge already appears on the receipt.","explanation":"Tipping, known locally as baksheesh, is deeply embedded in Egyptian service culture and meaningfully supplements low service wages. Restaurants commonly add a service charge (often around 12%) plus VAT to the bill, but a separate cash tip of around 10% directly to the server is still customary since the printed service charge doesn't always reach staff.","regional_note":"Baksheesh culture is especially pronounced in Cairo and tourist-heavy resort areas, where tipping is actively expected for small services beyond just dining.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Tipping isn't local custom and can confuse staff, since good service is already the norm without extra payment."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is expected and forms a real part of server income."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Service is included by law, but leaving a little extra for good service is still a polite gesture."}],"sources":[{"title":"Travel advice for Egypt","url":"https://roughguides.com/egypt/customs-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Tipping in Egypt: What to Expect, When to Tip & How Much to Give","url":"https://www.egypttoursplus.com/tipping-in-egypt/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Context-dependent, use carefully","confidence":"low","tip":"With elders or in formal or religious settings, use a verbal acknowledgment rather than a thumbs-up.","explanation":"The thumbs-up was historically viewed as a rude, almost obscene gesture in parts of the Arab world, though Western media exposure has since made it widely read as simply positive by many Egyptians. Because its reception genuinely varies by generation, setting, and manner of use, it's best treated as situational rather than clearly safe or clearly risky.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"The thumbs-up is still widely treated there as an obscene insult."},{"country":"Greece","verdict":"caution","note":"An abrupt, thrusting thumbs-up can be read as rude rather than complimentary by some."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"It's understood positively as 'good' or 'OK', though used less often than in the West."}],"sources":[{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Offensive International Travel Hand Gestures","url":"https://www.globalrescue.com/common/blog/detail/offensive-international-travel-hand-gestures/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Feet should stay down","confidence":"high","tip":"Keep your feet flat on the floor or tucked beneath you, and avoid resting an ankle on a knee in a way that points your sole at anyone.","explanation":"Cultural Atlas notes it is considered impolite in Egypt to point the toe, heel, or sole of the foot toward another person, or to show the sole of your shoe, since the foot is viewed as the lowest and least clean part of the body. This applies in homes and any seated social setting, not just formal occasions.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The feet are considered the lowest and most impure part of the body, making it a serious insult to point them at someone."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing the soles of your feet at someone is disrespectful, especially in traditional or formal settings."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Generally seen as casual, though it can seem out of place in a formal setting."}],"sources":[{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"What behaviours are rude in the Middle East","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/blog/what-behaviours-are-rude-in-the-middle-east","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"rude","label":"Right hand for eating","confidence":"high","tip":"Eat and pass food with your right hand, and if you're left-handed, make a deliberate effort to switch at the table.","explanation":"The right hand is used for eating and handling food in Egypt, while the left is reserved for less clean, personal tasks — a distinction most sources trace to Islamic practice that is widely observed, including by many non-Muslim Egyptians in mixed settings. Directly putting food into your mouth with the left hand is seen as unhygienic and can cause offense.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is reserved for toilet hygiene, so eating or passing food with it is considered unclean."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"There is no stigma around which hand you use; chopstick technique matters far more."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"There is no cultural taboo against eating with the left hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Travel advice for Egypt","url":"https://roughguides.com/egypt/customs-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Why Left-Handed Eating Is Frowned Upon In The Middle East","url":"https://www.foodrepublic.com/1573656/why-left-handed-eating-frowned-upon-middle-east","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine for kids, not adults","confidence":"medium","tip":"Reserve head-patting for children you know, and offer a handshake or warm word instead with adults.","explanation":"There is no basis for treating the head as the body's 'most sacred and dignified part' tied to Islamic teaching — a well-known hadith actually encourages gently stroking an orphan's head as an act of kindness, so the gesture toward children reads as affectionate. Toward an adult, head-patting is more a matter of overfamiliarity and perceived disrespect than a religious violation.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body, so touching it, child or adult, is a serious breach of etiquette."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head is often affectionate, but touching an adult's head is generally seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Patting a child's head on the head usually reads as friendly, though context still matters with adults."}],"sources":[{"title":"Hadith on Orphans: Be kind to orphans for a soft heart","url":"https://www.abuaminaelias.com/dailyhadithonline/2019/12/15/be-kind-to-orphans/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Sunnah.com – Kindness, Mercy and Compassion Towards Children","url":"https://sunnah.com/adab/19","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Positive in greetings, moderate in business","confidence":"medium","tip":"Use warm, direct eye contact when greeting someone, but soften it when listening to a senior colleague or interacting across genders.","explanation":"In everyday greetings, Cultural Atlas describes Egyptian handshakes as often prolonged and accompanied by a wide smile and direct eye contact, making it a positive, expected part of a warm introduction. In business or mixed-gender settings, however, sustained eye contact should be moderated, particularly with someone senior or of the opposite gender, where a softer gaze signals respect rather than challenge.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged eye contact can feel confrontational; a softer, briefer gaze is preferred."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct, steady eye contact is expected in business as a sign of confidence and honesty."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Direct eye contact between men is normal, but prolonged eye contact between men and women is avoided."}],"sources":[{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Greetings","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-greetings","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Business Culture","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-business-culture","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected in most homes","confidence":"medium","tip":"Watch what your host and other guests do at the entrance, and remove your shoes if you see a shoe rack or others barefoot.","explanation":"It is customary to remove your shoes before entering an Egyptian home, reflecting respect for the host's space and the practice of floor seating, though it is not a strict universal rule enforced everywhere. The safest approach is to follow your host's lead at the door.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoe removal before entering a home is mandatory and expected, not optional."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal is optional and depends on the individual household's preference."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes at the door is standard practice in most homes."}],"sources":[{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Countries That Always Take Their Shoes Off Inside","url":"https://dailypassport.com/countries-always-take-shoes-off-inside/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Use your whole hand instead","confidence":"medium","tip":"Indicate a person or direction with your whole hand rather than a single pointed finger.","explanation":"Pointing directly at someone with a single index finger is widely described as impolite across Arab countries, including Egypt, since it can come across as accusatory. The more common and accepted alternative is to gesture with the whole open hand toward the person or direction.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with a single finger is considered impolite; gesturing with the whole hand is preferred."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing with the index finger is common and generally unremarkable in casual settings."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Index-finger pointing is considered offensive; many instead point with the thumb."}],"sources":[{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Common Gestures People Use in Arab Countries","url":"https://www.theculturetrip.com/asia/united-arab-emirates/articles/11-common-gestures-people-use-in-arab-countries","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Keep affection private","confidence":"medium","tip":"Stick to a handshake or verbal greeting in public, and save affection for private settings.","explanation":"Rough Guides describes intimate public behavior like kissing and cuddling in Egypt as a clear no-no, with even hand-holding between an opposite-gender couple drawing disapproval in most settings. Cultural Atlas notes the norm is primarily social rather than a strict legal one: close friends of the same gender may hold hands or kiss cheeks in public without issue, while mixed-gender affection is the behavior to avoid.","regional_note":"Cairo and Alexandria's more secular social circles tend to be somewhat more tolerant of mild PDA than rural areas and Upper Egypt.","also_check":[{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"caution","note":"Public affection carries real legal risk, with past cases resulting in arrest and jail time for kissing in public."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek-kissing as a greeting and casual public affection are widely accepted."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Public affection is uncommon and can draw uncomfortable looks even among young couples."}],"sources":[{"title":"Travel advice for Egypt","url":"https://roughguides.com/egypt/customs-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Fluid schedules, stay patient","confidence":"medium","tip":"Arrive on time yourself, confirm the meeting a day or two beforehand, and expect the agenda to move slower than planned.","explanation":"Cultural Atlas describes Egyptian business meetings as often running longer than planned and beginning with coffee, tea, and personal conversation to build trust, so punctuality is appreciated but schedules are genuinely fluid. It is considered rude to try to rush a meeting, and it may take several visits to accomplish something that would be a single meeting elsewhere.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is strictly observed; even a short delay is seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Arriving late is seen as unprofessional and a sign of poor planning."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"Some delay is tolerated socially, but punctuality is still appreciated for business meetings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Business Culture","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-business-culture","kind":"reference"},{"title":"6 Cultural Etiquette Tips from Egypt","url":"https://www.westernunion.com/blog/en/6-cultural-etiquette-tips-egypt/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"rude","label":"Accept some, don't finish the plate","confidence":"medium","tip":"Accept what's offered, and leave a small amount on your plate when you're done rather than scraping it clean.","explanation":"Declining food offered by an Egyptian host is seen as rejecting their hospitality and can cause genuine offense, so it's customary to accept at least a small portion. Interestingly, completely finishing your plate can be read as a sign you're still hungry and prompt your host to keep refilling it, so leaving a little food behind is actually the polite way to signal you've had enough.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"A polite decline with a brief apology is acceptable, though hosts may insist once or twice first."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food offered by a host is seen as rejecting their generosity."},{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food is often seen as disrespectful, with hosts expected to insist multiple times before guests accept."}],"sources":[{"title":"Travel advice for Egypt","url":"https://roughguides.com/egypt/customs-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"How To Follow Business Etiquette in Egypt","url":"https://clickup.com/p/business-etiquette/egypt","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Ambiguous, avoid relying on it","confidence":"low","tip":"Use a thumbs-up-free verbal affirmation instead of the OK hand sign to avoid ambiguity.","explanation":"The Western 'OK' meaning of this gesture doesn't reliably carry over in Egypt: general references on obscene gestures confirm it carries vulgar or offensive connotations in parts of the Middle East, though solid Egypt-specific evidence is thin. Because interpretations vary by generation and audience, the safest approach is to avoid relying on it rather than assume it reads as approval.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture is considered a serious obscene insult."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"It commonly signals 'money' or 'OK' and is generally positive."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"It can be read as a vulgar insult there rather than a sign of approval."}],"sources":[{"title":"Obscene gesture","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obscene_gesture","kind":"reference"},{"title":"OK gesture","url":"https://en.dharmapedia.net/wiki/OK_gesture","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"No specific rule, stay modest","confidence":"low","tip":"If eating noodles at a restaurant, favor quiet bites over loud slurping, since restrained table manners are generally valued.","explanation":"Noodles aren't part of traditional Egyptian cuisine, and no source documents a specific Egyptian rule about slurping them. General Egyptian dining etiquette values modest, composed table manners over loud eating noises, so audible slurping would likely read as a minor lapse rather than a serious offence.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is read as a sign of enjoyment and appreciation for the chef."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping is common and often indicates you're savoring the food."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta is considered uncivilized in Italian dining culture."}],"sources":[{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Arab Table Manners: Business Lunch Etiquette in Arabic Countries","url":"https://www.arabicpod101.com/blog/2018/11/27/arab-table-manners/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Normal between friends, same gender","confidence":"high","tip":"Let a same-gender Egyptian friend initiate a cheek-kiss greeting if they choose to; with the opposite gender or someone you've just met, stick to a handshake (if they extend first) or a verbal greeting.","explanation":"Cultural Atlas notes that close friends of the same gender in Egypt may hold hands or kiss cheeks when greeting in public, consistent with the broader pattern that touching during greetings depends heavily on how well people know each other. Between people of the opposite gender, especially on a first meeting, a handshake or verbal greeting is the safer default.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek-kissing (la bise) is a standard greeting among friends and acquaintances of any gender."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek-kissing is common among the same gender, but cross-gender greetings in public are strictly avoided."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Cheek-kissing isn't a traditional greeting; a bow is expected instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Greetings","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-greetings","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Seen as a gesture for animals","confidence":"high","tip":"To call someone over, turn your palm face-down and wave your fingers toward yourself rather than curling one finger upward.","explanation":"Beckoning with a single raised finger is seen across much of the Arab world, including Egypt, as a gesture used to call animals or servants, making it demeaning when directed at a person. The accepted alternative is to turn your palm face-down and make a gentle scooping motion with your fingers toward yourself.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"A single raised finger to beckon is seen as crude; a palm-down wave is the polite alternative."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"The same gesture is used only for animals and is considered very offensive directed at a person."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"It's common casually but can seem condescending in a formal or professional setting."}],"sources":[{"title":"Egyptian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/egyptian-culture/egyptian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Traditional Gestures Around The World That Mean The Opposite In The US","url":"https://www.worldatlas.com/articles/traditional-gestures-around-the-world-that-mean-the-opposite-in-the-us.html","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcome, skip the flowers","confidence":"medium","tip":"Bring chocolates or pastries rather than flowers, and present the gift with your right hand.","explanation":"Gift-giving is appreciated in Egyptian business and social settings as a gesture of goodwill, with quality chocolates or pastries as safe, well-regarded choices — but flowers are best avoided since they're associated with weddings or illness rather than casual gift-giving. Gifts should be given with the right hand or both hands, and avoiding alcohol or pork products is a safe default.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized, with careful attention to wrapping and presentation."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Business gifts can seem inappropriate, especially if given before a deal is finalized."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is common, with the same right-hand presentation and alcohol/pork avoidance rules."}],"sources":[{"title":"Travel advice for Egypt","url":"https://roughguides.com/egypt/customs-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Gift Giving Etiquette in the Middle East","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/blog/gift-giving-in-the-middle-east","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Saudi Arabia":{"slug":"saudi-arabia","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcomed but optional","confidence":"medium","tip":"Check the bill for a service charge first; if there isn't one, leaving 10-15% in cash is a safe, appreciated amount.","explanation":"Tipping is not mandatory in Saudi Arabia and service-sector wages aren't structured around gratuities, but leaving roughly 10-15% in cash is increasingly common and appreciated, especially where no service charge is listed. Reporting on local attitudes notes views are genuinely mixed — some Saudis see it as generous hospitality, others as closer to alms for foreign workers, and Saudis rarely tip fellow Saudis.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Tipping is not local custom and can confuse staff, since excellent service is already expected without extra payment."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is standard and forms a real part of server income."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"Service is included by law, but leaving a little extra for exceptional service is a polite touch."}],"sources":[{"title":"Tips for women travelling to Saudi","url":"https://visitsaudi.com/en/stories/tips-for-women-traveling-to-saudi","kind":"reference"},{"title":"www.arabnews.com (tipping in Saudi restaurants)","url":"https://www.arabnews.com/node/380632","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Meaning has shifted, stay cautious","confidence":"low","tip":"With elders or in formal and religious settings, use a verbal acknowledgment rather than a thumbs-up to be safe.","explanation":"The thumbs-up was historically viewed as a rude, almost obscene gesture in parts of the Arab world, but decades of Western media exposure have made it widely understood as simply positive to many Saudis today. When then-candidate Donald Trump gave a thumbs-up during a 2017 visit to Saudi Arabia, commentary on whether it was offensive came mostly from Western observers rather than Saudis themselves, which illustrates how contested and generation-dependent the gesture's reception really is.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"The thumbs-up is still widely treated as an obscene insult there."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"It's understood as 'good' or 'OK' and used positively, especially among younger people."},{"country":"Greece","verdict":"caution","note":"An abrupt or thrusting thumbs-up can be read as rude rather than complimentary by some."}],"sources":[{"title":"Trump Made A 'Rude' Thumbs Up Sign In Saudi Arabia — But Really Everyone Needs To Calm Down","url":"https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/davidmack/two-trumps-up","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Communication","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-communication","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Feet should stay down","confidence":"high","tip":"Sit with both feet flat on the floor or crossed at the ankles, never pointed toward another person.","explanation":"The sole of the foot is viewed as the dirtiest part of the body across the Gulf, so deliberately pointing it at someone is read as a strong insult rather than a religious rule. This matters in majlis seating and any formal gathering in Saudi Arabia where people sit on low cushions or face each other directly.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The feet are considered the lowest and most impure part of the body, making it a serious insult to point them at someone."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Sitting with soles facing others is disrespectful, particularly in traditional settings."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Showing the soles of your feet is generally neutral and only seems informal in unusually formal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"What behaviours are rude in the Middle East","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/blog/what-behaviours-are-rude-in-the-middle-east","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Ask Ali: Why it is rude to show the soles of your feet","url":"https://www.thenationalnews.com/arts-culture/ask-ali-why-it-is-rude-to-show-the-soles-of-your-feet-1.295633/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"rude","label":"Right hand for eating","confidence":"high","tip":"Always eat and pass food with your right hand, even for small items like bread, and keep your left hand in your lap.","explanation":"The left hand is reserved for personal hygiene in Saudi custom, rooted in Islamic practice, while the right hand is used for eating, passing food, and greeting. Using the left hand to eat or hand someone something is considered unhygienic and disrespectful, including when sharing food from a communal dish.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is reserved for toilet hygiene, so eating or passing food with it is considered unclean."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Either hand is acceptable; chopstick technique matters far more than hand preference."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"There is no stigma against eating with the left hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Why Left-Handed Eating Is Frowned Upon In The Middle East","url":"https://www.foodrepublic.com/1573656/why-left-handed-eating-frowned-upon-middle-east","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine for kids, not adults","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep head-patting to children you know, and greet adults with a handshake (same gender) or a verbal greeting instead.","explanation":"There's no basis for treating the head as 'the seat of the soul' in Islamic teaching — a well-known hadith actually encourages gently stroking an orphan's head as an act of kindness, so the gesture toward children reads as affectionate rather than offensive. Toward an adult, patting the head is more about overfamiliarity and perceived disrespect for someone's dignity or status than any religious violation.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body, so touching it, child or adult, is a serious breach of etiquette."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head is often affectionate, but touching an adult's head is seen as disrespectful, especially by a stranger."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting an adult's head generally reads as condescending outside of close friendships."}],"sources":[{"title":"Hadith on Orphans: Be kind to orphans for a soft heart","url":"https://www.abuaminaelias.com/dailyhadithonline/2019/12/15/be-kind-to-orphans/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Sunnah.com – Kindness, Mercy and Compassion Towards Children","url":"https://sunnah.com/adab/19","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Gender-dependent, use sparingly","confidence":"medium","tip":"Maintain normal eye contact with people of your own gender; with the opposite gender, keep it brief and polite, especially in business settings.","explanation":"Between men, direct eye contact in Saudi Arabia is a normal sign of sincerity and respect. Muslims generally avoid physical contact with the opposite gender, and the same caution extends to eye contact: prolonged eye contact between men and women who are not closely related can be seen as inappropriately forward, so a softer gaze is the safer default in mixed-gender settings.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged eye contact can feel confrontational; a softer, briefer gaze is preferred."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct eye contact is expected as a sign of confidence and honesty in business."},{"country":"Nigeria","verdict":"caution","note":"Sustained eye contact with an elder or superior can be read as a challenge rather than respect."}],"sources":[{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Greetings","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-greetings","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Communication","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-communication","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected in most homes","confidence":"medium","tip":"Watch what your host does at the entrance, and never step onto a prayer rug with your shoes on.","explanation":"Removing your shoes before entering a home is a widely observed courtesy in Saudi Arabia, tied to floor seating and respect for the host's space. There is no single universal rule enforced everywhere, so the safest approach is to follow your host's lead at the door and avoid stepping onto a prayer rug with shoes on.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoe removal is mandatory in most homes, often with dedicated slippers for different rooms."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal is optional and depends on the individual household."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes before entering a home is standard, and also required at temples and some shops."}],"sources":[{"title":"Tips for women travelling to Saudi","url":"https://visitsaudi.com/en/stories/tips-for-women-traveling-to-saudi","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Countries That Always Take Their Shoes Off Inside","url":"https://dailypassport.com/countries-always-take-shoes-off-inside/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Use your whole hand instead","confidence":"medium","tip":"Gesture with your whole hand, palm open, rather than pointing with a single finger.","explanation":"Pointing directly at someone with a single index finger is widely described as impolite across Arab countries, including Saudi Arabia, because it can read as accusatory. Saudis typically gesture with the whole hand, consistent with the broader custom of using both hands together to offer or indicate things rather than a single finger.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with a single finger is impolite; gesturing with the whole hand is preferred."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"caution","note":"Single-finger pointing is tolerated informally but seen as impolite in more formal settings."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing with the index finger is common and mostly unremarkable in casual contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Common Gestures People Use in Arab Countries","url":"https://www.theculturetrip.com/asia/united-arab-emirates/articles/11-common-gestures-people-use-in-arab-countries","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Illegal under decency law","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep any physical affection, including extended hand-holding, for private spaces regardless of marital status.","explanation":"Saudi Arabia's 2019 Public Decency Regulations explicitly list public displays of affection as a finable offence, alongside immodest dress and unauthorized photography, with reported fines in the range of roughly 50 to 6,000 riyals. This sits alongside a separate track of 2019-era social reforms that relaxed strict gender segregation in cinemas, concerts, and some mixed venues, so 'everything is gender-segregated' is no longer an accurate summary — but affection in public specifically remains a punishable decency violation.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"caution","note":"Public affection carries real legal risk, with past cases resulting in arrest and jail time, though enforcement is inconsistent."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"caution","note":"PDA is common in cosmopolitan Istanbul but best avoided in more conservative or rural areas."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Public affection is uncommon and can draw uncomfortable looks even among young couples."}],"sources":[{"title":"All you need to know about the new Saudi public decency code","url":"https://www.arabnews.com/node/1561206/amp","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Saudi Arabia defangs religious police but plans to enforce 'decency'","url":"https://www.egyptindependent.com/saudi-de-fangs-religious-police-but-plans-to-enforce-decency/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Patience expected, punctuality too","confidence":"medium","tip":"Arrive on time yourself, and treat any wait as normal rather than a sign of disrespect toward you.","explanation":"Saudi business culture is genuinely more flexible about scheduling, and it's common for a meeting to start late or be preceded by extended coffee and conversation before business begins. Foreign visitors are still expected to arrive on time themselves as a sign of respect, even while being patient if their Saudi counterparts run late.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is taken very seriously; even a one-minute delay can be seen as disrespectful."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Arriving 15-30 minutes late to a meeting is common and generally accepted."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Strict punctuality is expected, and lateness is seen as unprofessional."}],"sources":[{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Communication","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"A guide to business etiquette in Saudi Arabia","url":"https://cibtvisas.com/blog/business-etiquette-saudi-arabia","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"rude","label":"Accept first, shake cup to stop","confidence":"high","tip":"Accept the first offer of coffee or food, and when you've had enough coffee, shake the cup lightly as you return it instead of saying no.","explanation":"Declining a host's initial offer of food or coffee in Saudi Arabia is seen as rejecting their hospitality (hafawa) and can cause real offense, so it's customary to accept at least a token amount. For Arabic coffee specifically, there's a built-in way to stop refills politely: gently shake your empty cup from side to side as you hand it back, rather than verbally declining.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"A polite decline with a brief apology is acceptable, though hosts may insist once or twice first."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food from a host is seen as rejecting their hospitality and effort."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Politely declining if full or restricted by diet is generally fine, and hosts don't typically take offense."}],"sources":[{"title":"Tips for women travelling to Saudi","url":"https://visitsaudi.com/en/stories/tips-for-women-traveling-to-saudi","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Brewing a tradition of honour and trust","url":"https://gulf-times.com/story/416875/brewing-a-tradition-of-honour-and-trust","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Ambiguous, avoid in public","confidence":"low","tip":"Use a thumbs-up-free verbal 'tamam' (fine) instead of the OK hand sign to avoid ambiguity.","explanation":"The Western 'OK' meaning of this gesture doesn't reliably carry over in Saudi Arabia: general references on obscene gestures note it carries vulgar or offensive connotations in parts of the Middle East, and some Gulf sources associate the gesture, especially when shaken toward someone, with invoking the evil eye. Evidence specific to Saudi Arabia is thin, so treat the gesture as genuinely unpredictable rather than clearly safe or clearly offensive.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture is considered a serious obscene insult."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"It commonly signals 'money' or 'OK', though context still matters."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"It can be read as a vulgar insult rather than a sign of approval there."}],"sources":[{"title":"Obscene gesture","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obscene_gesture","kind":"reference"},{"title":"OK gesture","url":"https://en.dharmapedia.net/wiki/OK_gesture","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"No specific rule, stay modest","confidence":"low","tip":"If eating noodles at a restaurant, favor quiet bites over loud slurping, since restrained table manners are generally valued.","explanation":"Noodles aren't part of traditional Saudi cuisine, and no source documents a specific Saudi rule about slurping them. General Gulf dining etiquette favors restrained, quiet eating over loud table noises, so audible slurping would likely be seen as a minor lapse in manners rather than a serious offence.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is read as a compliment to the chef and is expected in ramen shops."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping is common and often read as enjoying the food."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta is considered a clear breach of table manners."}],"sources":[{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Arab Table Manners: Business Lunch Etiquette in Arabic Countries","url":"https://www.arabicpod101.com/blog/2018/11/27/arab-table-manners/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Common between men, not across genders","confidence":"high","tip":"With someone of the same gender who initiates a cheek-kiss greeting, follow their lead; with the opposite gender, default to a verbal greeting unless they extend a hand first.","explanation":"Among friends of the same gender, greetings in Saudi Arabia often include cheek-kissing — typically the left hand rests on the other person's right shoulder while leaning in for three kisses, either right-left-right or repeated on the same cheek. Between men and women who are not closely related, physical greetings are avoided altogether; it's best to greet verbally and wait to see if a hand is extended.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"caution","note":"Same-gender cheek-kissing and warm embraces are common, while cross-gender greetings stay more reserved."},{"country":"Egypt","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek-kissing between close friends of the same gender is common, though cross-gender greetings in public are more conservative."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Cheek-kissing isn't a traditional greeting; a bow or handshake is expected instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Greetings","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-greetings","kind":"reference"},{"title":"A Handshake is Worth 1000 Words: Body Language in Dubai Business Culture","url":"https://www.arabicpod101.com/blog/2019/02/14/body-language-in-dubai-business-culture/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Seen as a gesture for animals","confidence":"high","tip":"To call someone over, turn your palm face-down and wave your fingers toward yourself rather than curling one finger upward.","explanation":"Beckoning with a single raised finger is seen across much of the Arab world, including Saudi Arabia, as a gesture used to call animals or servants, making it demeaning when directed at a person. This fits the broader Saudi custom of offering or gesturing with the whole hand, or both hands together, rather than a single finger.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"A single raised finger to beckon is seen as crude; a palm-down wave is the polite alternative."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"The same gesture is used only for animals and is considered very offensive directed at a person."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"It's common casually but can seem condescending in a formal or professional setting."}],"sources":[{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Traditional Gestures Around The World That Mean The Opposite In The US","url":"https://www.worldatlas.com/articles/traditional-gestures-around-the-world-that-mean-the-opposite-in-the-us.html","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcome, kept modest","confidence":"medium","tip":"Present the gift with your right hand or both hands, and keep it modest rather than extravagant.","explanation":"Gift-giving in Saudi business culture is a welcomed, trust-building gesture as long as it stays modest and avoids being perceived as a bribe, with items like dates, high-quality coffee, or sweets as safe choices. Gifts should be given and received with the right hand or both hands together, never the left, and should avoid alcohol, pork products, or anything with explicit religious imagery.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized; wrapping and presentation matter greatly, and both hands are used to present a gift."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Business gifts are often seen as unnecessary; if given, keep them small and practical."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Gifts help build relationships, but clocks, umbrellas, or sharp objects are avoided as symbols of bad luck."}],"sources":[{"title":"Gift Giving Etiquette in the Middle East","url":"https://commisceo-global.com/blog/gift-giving-in-the-middle-east","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Saudi Arabian Culture – Etiquette","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/saudi-arabian-culture/saudi-arabian-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Greece":{"slug":"greece","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Appreciated, not obligatory","confidence":"high","tip":"Leave a small cash tip (round up the bill or about 5-10%) directly on the table rather than adding it to a card payment.","explanation":"Tipping in Greek restaurants is voluntary rather than obligatory; locals typically round up the bill or leave a small amount in cash. At casual tavernas a couple of euros per person (roughly 5-10%) is typical, while upscale restaurants may see 10-15% for excellent service, well below American-style tipping norms.","regional_note":"In touristy island towns and high-end Athens restaurants, tipping closer to 10-15% is more common, while in neighborhood tavernas rounding up is enough.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is considered unnecessary and can even cause confusion or offense; excellent service is the baseline norm."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is standard and expected for good service, as servers rely on tips for income."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Service charge is included by law, but leaving small change for good service is still a nice, common gesture."}],"sources":[{"title":"Decoding the Tipping Etiquette in Greece: A 2024 Guide","url":"https://greekcitytimes.com/2024/07/14/decoding-the-tipping-etiquette-in-greece-a-2024-guide/amp","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Tipping In Greece – Do You Tip In Greece And How Much","url":"https://realgreekexperiences.com/tipping-in-greece","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Contested: old insult, mixed use now","confidence":"low","tip":"With older Greeks, in rural areas, or in any tense moment, skip an emphatic thumbs-up and use a nod or say 'endaxi' (okay) instead; in casual, urban, or tourist-facing settings it's normally read as plain approval.","explanation":"Traditionally in Greece the thumbs-up has carried a vulgar meaning similar to the middle finger, particularly when thrust abruptly or among older Greeks, and some travel-advice sources still list it as an insult to avoid. However, due to international media, texting culture, and younger generations, it is now also very commonly used and understood as plain approval — one Greek gesture guide even recommends it as a safe way to signal agreement. Because reputable sources genuinely disagree, treat this as situational rather than a flat rule.","regional_note":"Older generations and more traditional or rural communities are more likely to read it as an insult than younger, urban Greeks.","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"Thumbs-up is a well-known obscene insult, roughly equivalent to the middle finger."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Thumbs-up generally reads as 'good job' or 'OK' and is a positive, low-controversy gesture."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Thumbs-up is a universally positive, uncontroversial sign of approval."}],"sources":[{"title":"10 Innocent Gestures You Shouldn't Use Overseas","url":"https://www.smartertravel.com/10-innocent-gestures-you-shouldnt-use-overseas/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Avoid This Common American Gesture At All Costs When Traveling In Greece","url":"https://www.islands.com/1784578/avoid-common-hand-america-gesture-traveling-greece-country/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Casual faux pas, not a major taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep your feet off tables and furniture in someone's home or in formal settings, but don't worry that an accidental foot gesture will be read as a deep insult the way it would be in Thailand.","explanation":"Greece does not have the strict foot taboo found in Thai/Buddhist or Gulf Arab cultures, where feet are viewed as spiritually unclean; comprehensive Greek etiquette guides do not list foot display among the country's notable taboos. That said, propping your feet on furniture or pointing the soles directly at someone is still seen as casual or mildly impolite in formal settings or someone else's home, similar to general Western manners.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"Feet are considered the lowest and spiritually 'dirtiest' part of the body; showing soles or pointing feet at someone is a real insult."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the soles of your feet is seen as disrespectful and implying uncleanliness."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Not a taboo, but propping feet up in formal settings can be seen as overly casual or rude."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greece Guide (Commisceo Global)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/greece-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Culture of Thailand","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Thailand","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"No left-hand taboo in Greece","confidence":"high","tip":"Eat normally with whichever hand is comfortable; there's no left-hand rule in Greek dining, though keeping both hands visible above the table is good general manners.","explanation":"Unlike the Middle East, South Asia, and parts of Africa — where the left hand is reserved for hygiene tasks and avoided at the table — Greece has no such taboo. Greeks eat in standard Continental style (fork in the left hand, knife in the right), and left-handed diners face no social penalty.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is reserved for hygiene; always eat and pass food with the right hand."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is considered unclean, so food should be eaten and passed with the right hand."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Eating or passing food with the left hand is taboo in most traditional settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greece Guide (Commisceo Global)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/greece-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Why Left-Handed Eating Is Frowned Upon In The Middle East","url":"https://www.foodrepublic.com/1573656/why-left-handed-eating-frowned-upon-middle-east","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Patronizing, not sacred-taboo","confidence":"medium","tip":"Reserve head-patting for small children or close family; greet adults with a handshake, a cheek kiss (if appropriate), or a hand on the shoulder instead.","explanation":"No Greek etiquette source documents the spiritual 'head is sacred' belief found in Thai/Buddhist cultures — that framing doesn't apply to Greece. However, patting an adult's head is still generally seen as patronizing or overly familiar in Greek social settings, much as it would be across most of Europe; it reads as normal and affectionate mainly with young children or very close family.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered sacred; even a light touch is a real taboo, not just impolite."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting an adult's head can seem condescending, though it's fine with children or close family."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Seen as infantilizing with an adult, but normal and affectionate with children."}],"sources":[{"title":"Culture of Thailand","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Thailand","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Greece Guide (Commisceo Global)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/greece-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected sign of honesty","confidence":"high","tip":"Maintain steady, natural eye contact during conversation and meetings — looking away too much can be misread as dishonesty or disinterest.","explanation":"In Greek business and social settings, direct eye contact is expected and read as a sign of honesty, confidence, and genuine engagement; avoiding it can come across as evasive or disinterested. Greece is also often cited as one of the more physically expressive, high-eye-contact cultures in Europe.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can feel intimidating; brief glances are generally preferred."},{"country":"Nigeria","verdict":"caution","note":"Direct eye contact with elders or superiors can be seen as challenging; younger people often lower their gaze."},{"country":"Finland","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct eye contact is valued as a sign of sincerity, similar to Greece."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greece Guide (Commisceo Global)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/greece-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Business Culture in Greece","url":"https://businessculture.org/southern-europe/business-culture-in-greece/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Host-dependent, not required","confidence":"medium","tip":"Watch what the host and other guests do, or simply ask 'should I take my shoes off?' when entering someone's home.","explanation":"Removing shoes before entering a home is not a standard Greek custom the way it is in Japan, Scandinavia, or many Asian households; Greeks commonly wear shoes indoors. It is increasingly common in modern homes with rugs or hardwood floors for a host to prefer guests remove their shoes, so it's best to take the host's cue rather than assume either way.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoes are always removed at the entrance; wearing outdoor shoes inside is considered unclean."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoes are typically removed at the door, and slippers are often offered to guests."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Varies by household; some expect shoes off, many don't mind either way."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greece Guide (Commisceo Global)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/greece-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Greece (Wikivoyage, Respect section)","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Greece","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Can seem blunt or confrontational","confidence":"medium","tip":"When indicating a person or direction, gesture with an open hand rather than a single extended finger.","explanation":"As in much of the world, pointing directly at a person with the index finger can come across as rude or confrontational in Greece, with an open-hand gesture viewed as more polite. This is a general social-courtesy norm rather than a severe taboo, and pointing at objects or directions is far less sensitive than pointing at a person.","regional_note":"In casual settings among friends or in cosmopolitan cities, this is treated more loosely than in formal or elder-respect contexts.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Index-finger pointing is seen as very rude; an open hand or gentle nod is preferred."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing is taboo, especially with the left hand; an open palm or thumb is used instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Index-finger pointing is common and generally acceptable in casual contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"Pointing","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pointing","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Greek Culture — Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/greek-culture/greek-culture-communication","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Common but modesty expected","confidence":"medium","tip":"Hand-holding and a brief kiss are fine in most places, but save more intimate affection for private settings, especially around religious sites or older relatives.","explanation":"Greece is a tactile, warm culture — friends (including male friends) commonly touch arms or shoulders, and light affection like hand-holding or a quick kiss between couples is broadly unremarkable in cities and tourist areas. More passionate or prolonged PDA can draw disapproval, especially around family, in religious settings, or in more conservative/rural communities, so moderation is the safer approach.","regional_note":"Rural areas, smaller islands, and older generations tend to be more conservative about PDA than Athens or Thessaloniki.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"La bise and light PDA like hand-holding or a kiss are broadly accepted in most settings."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"PDA is generally discouraged in public, even between spouses."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Any public display of affection between unmarried couples is illegal and strictly taboo."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greece Guide (Commisceo Global)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/greece-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Public display of affection","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_display_of_affection","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Punctual for business, flexible socially","confidence":"medium","tip":"Arrive on time (or only a few minutes late) for business meetings; for a casual dinner or party invitation, arriving 15-30 minutes after the stated time is normal and not considered rude.","explanation":"For business meetings, Greek professionals are expected to arrive on time — a leading etiquette guide explicitly advises punctuality for meetings, even though the meeting's pace may still run flexibly once underway. Social gatherings are different: Greece, like other Mediterranean cultures, follows a more relaxed, relationship-first sense of time where arriving somewhat late to a casual dinner or party is common and rarely offends.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Germany","verdict":"rude","note":"Lateness is seen as unprofessional and a sign of poor planning, especially for business."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality is critical for both business and most social plans; even a few minutes late is notable."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Arriving somewhat late to social gatherings is similarly common and accepted, though business punctuality is expected."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greece Guide (Commisceo Global)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/greece-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Polychronicity","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polychronicity","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Expected to accept, politely","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you need to decline, do so warmly and briefly ('I'm so full, it was delicious') rather than a flat no, and try to accept at least a small taste if you can.","explanation":"Greek hospitality (rooted in the ancient tradition of xenia/philoxenia) places strong value on guests accepting food, and etiquette guides note that accepting second helpings and finishing what's on your plate is seen as a compliment to the host. A flat refusal can come across as rejecting the host's generosity, but a polite decline with a brief, genuine reason (allergy, diet, fullness) is generally understood and accepted in modern Greek life, especially in urban settings.","regional_note":"In traditional rural households and with older generations, hosts may insist more persistently, while urban or younger hosts are generally more accepting of a polite decline.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"A gentle initial refusal is often expected as modesty, though hosts will insist."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Declining is acceptable if you cite religious or health reasons, though hosts may insist you take at least something."},{"country":"France","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing a host's food is generally seen as impolite, since it can undermine the host's culinary effort."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greece Guide (Commisceo Global)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/greece-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Philoxenia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philoxenia","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Vulgar insult, distinct from the moutza","confidence":"high","tip":"Don't use the OK hand sign to mean 'all good'; say 'endaxi' (okay) or simply nod and smile instead.","explanation":"Forming a circle with the thumb and index finger (the Western 'OK' sign) carries a vulgar, sexually-charged insult in several Mediterranean countries including Greece and Turkey. It is not the same gesture as the moutza (an open, outstretched palm thrust toward someone), which is Greece's other, unrelated insulting gesture — the two are sometimes mixed up, but they have separate origins and meanings.","regional_note":"Younger, urban Greeks are often aware the sign means something positive internationally, but it is still widely considered rude to make it locally.","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The OK sign is a vulgar insult, similarly to Greece."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"Also considered an offensive, sexually-charged gesture."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Generally understood to mean 'money' or 'OK,' with no offensive meaning."}],"sources":[{"title":"18 Gestures That Can Cause Offense Around the World","url":"https://www.shermanstravel.com/2015/18-gestures-that-can-cause-offense-around-the-world","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Greece: Cultural Differences (CIS Abroad)","url":"https://go.greece.cisabroad.com/cultural-differences","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Generally seen as poor manners","confidence":"medium","tip":"Eat pasta or soup quietly, the way you would in most European dining settings, rather than assuming Asian noodle-slurping norms apply.","explanation":"Greece follows the broader European norm that eating noisily — including slurping — is considered poor table manners, in contrast to countries like Japan, China, or Korea where slurping noodles can be acceptable or even a compliment to the cook. It isn't singled out as a uniquely Greek taboo in etiquette guides, but quiet eating is generally expected, especially in more formal settings.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles shows appreciation and enjoyment of the food."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles or soup is generally acceptable and can show you're savoring the meal."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta is considered a breach of proper table manners, similar to Greece."}],"sources":[{"title":"Table manners","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_manners","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Greece Guide (Commisceo Global)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/greece-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Warm, common greeting","confidence":"high","tip":"Follow the other person's lead — offer a handshake in business or first-meeting contexts, and go for a light cheek kiss or cheek-touch with friends and family once a relationship is established.","explanation":"Cheek kissing (typically light kisses or cheek-touches, often starting on the right) is a standard warm greeting among friends and family in Greece, especially between women and between men and women; close male friends may also do this, though handshakes are the default in business or first meetings. It reflects Greece's generally warm, physically expressive culture.","regional_note":"Older or more conservative/rural Greeks, especially men, may prefer handshakes over cheek kissing with those outside family.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"La bise is standard, though the number of kisses varies by region."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Cheek kissing isn't customary; bowing is the standard greeting."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"caution","note":"Common between close male friends, but avoided between unrelated men and women in public."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greece Guide (Commisceo Global)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/greece-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Business Culture in Greece","url":"https://businessculture.org/southern-europe/business-culture-in-greece/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Seen as brusque, not codified","confidence":"medium","tip":"To call someone over politely, use an open-hand, palm-down wave rather than a single curled finger.","explanation":"Greece doesn't have a single codified, widely-cited taboo against beckoning with a curled index finger the way it does for the moutza or the OK sign — it isn't named in the main Greek-gesture guides. That said, it fits the broader European pattern where a curled 'come here' finger (especially palm-up) can come across as brusque or commanding, with an open-hand, palm-down wave seen as the more polite way to call someone over.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Curled-finger beckoning is considered very rude and can be seen as a challenge."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with one finger is disrespectful; a palm-down hand wave is preferred."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Common casually, but can seem condescending in formal or professional contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"List of gestures","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Avoid This Common American Gesture At All Costs When Traveling In Greece","url":"https://www.islands.com/1784578/avoid-common-hand-america-gesture-traveling-greece-country/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Welcome but kept modest","confidence":"high","tip":"Bring a modest, thoughtful gift — wine, sweets, or flowers — when invited to someone's home, and present or receive it with your right hand or both hands rather than the left hand alone.","explanation":"Bringing a gift when invited to a Greek home or for a business relationship is a warmly received gesture, though not strictly required; it's best kept modest (good wine, sweets, flowers, or something from your home region), since an overly expensive gift can create pressure to reciprocate or appear like an attempt at influence. In business, gifts are more about relationship-building than ritual.","regional_note":"Among more traditional or older business circles, gifts may be opened privately rather than in front of others.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized; avoid certain colors, numbers, and overly lavish gifts."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Gifts in formal business settings can be seen as unnecessary or viewed with suspicion."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"polite","note":"Gifts are welcome but should avoid alcohol or pork and are offered with the right hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"Greece Guide (Commisceo Global)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/greece-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Greece: Cultural Differences (CIS Abroad)","url":"https://go.greece.cisabroad.com/cultural-differences","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Netherlands":{"slug":"netherlands","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Optional, modest tip appreciated","confidence":"high","tip":"Round up the bill or leave 5-10% in cash directly with the server if the service was good; otherwise, don't feel obligated to tip.","explanation":"Service costs have been folded into Dutch menu prices since the 1970s, so restaurants rarely add a separate service charge and tipping is not required. Most people leave around 5-10% of the bill, or simply round up, when they're happy with the service, while leaving nothing for average service is completely normal and not seen as rude.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Tipping isn't expected in Japan and can confuse or embarrass staff since good service is already assumed and included in the price."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% is standard practice in US restaurants, where service staff often rely on tips to supplement a lower base wage."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"A service charge is legally included in French bills, but rounding up or leaving small change for good service is a common courtesy."}],"sources":[{"title":"Tipping in Amsterdam: all you need to know","url":"https://dutchreview.com/culture/a-guide-to-tipping-culture-in-the-netherlands/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Tipping in Amsterdam","url":"https://radicalstorage.com/travel/tipping-in-amsterdam/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Positive, widely understood gesture","confidence":"high","tip":"Use it freely for quick, informal approval, but back it up with words in a formal business setting.","explanation":"The thumbs-up is a positive, casual sign of approval across Western Europe, and the Netherlands is no exception — it's used freely among friends, colleagues, and even in business messaging. In a culture that values directness and informality, it is not seen as rude, though it remains a casual gesture best paired with words in very formal settings.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Greece","verdict":"caution","note":"The thumbs-up has historically carried a dismissive or rude connotation in some Greek contexts, though it is now widely used positively, especially among younger people."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"A raised thumb can simply mean the number 'one' in context, which can cause brief confusion rather than offense."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"The thumbs-up generally reads as 'good' or 'OK', though it's a casual gesture best avoided in very formal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"Thumb signal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thumb_signal","kind":"reference"},{"title":"List of gestures","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Casual, not a strong taboo","confidence":"low","tip":"Keep your feet off furniture and avoid resting them near someone's face, especially in someone's home or a formal setting.","explanation":"Unlike in parts of Asia or the Middle East, there is no cultural or religious taboo around feet or their soles in the Netherlands. Propping your feet on furniture or pointing them at someone can still come across as careless or overly casual in someone's home or a formal setting, simply as ordinary manners rather than a deep cultural offense.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"Feet are considered the lowest and most unclean part of the body, so pointing them at people or sacred objects is a serious offense."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the sole of your foot to someone is highly disrespectful, as it implies they are beneath you."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Putting feet on furniture or pointing soles at others is seen as overly casual or rude in more formal settings, similar to the Netherlands."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Are there any cultural do's and don'ts in the Netherlands?","url":"https://debollenstreek.com/general-questions/cultural-dos-and-donts-the-netherlands/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"No taboo, personal preference","confidence":"high","tip":"Feel free to use either hand; if you want to match local table manners, hold your fork in your left hand and knife in your right while cutting.","explanation":"There is no religious or cultural taboo against using the left hand in the Netherlands, unlike in parts of South Asia, the Middle East, or North Africa. Most Dutch people simply follow continental European table manners — fork in the left hand, knife in the right — regardless of which hand they naturally favor.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is considered unclean in many traditional and religious contexts, so eating or passing food with it can cause offense."},{"country":"Egypt","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is traditionally considered unclean and is not used for eating or passing food, especially in traditional settings."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"In Muslim-majority areas of Indonesia, the left hand is considered impure for eating."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Are there any cultural do's and don'ts in the Netherlands?","url":"https://debollenstreek.com/general-questions/cultural-dos-and-donts-the-netherlands/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Okay for kids, not adults","confidence":"medium","tip":"Stick to a handshake or a brief nod with adults, and save head pats for children or people you know very well.","explanation":"Patting a child on the head is normal and affectionate, but doing the same to an adult you don't know well can come across as patronizing. Dutch social culture keeps a degree of personal space and formality with people outside close relationships, so unsolicited head-touching outside that circle can feel overly familiar.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered the most sacred part of the body, so touching it — even a child's — is a serious offense."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Patting a child's head is common and affectionate, but doing so to an adult can be seen as condescending."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Head-patting is generally reserved for children or very close relationships; doing it to an adult acquaintance can seem patronizing."}],"sources":[{"title":"Are there any cultural do's and don'ts in the Netherlands?","url":"https://debollenstreek.com/general-questions/cultural-dos-and-donts-the-netherlands/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Dutch Business Etiquette Guide","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/dutch-business-etiquette-guide-2026","kind":"community"},{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected sign of honesty","confidence":"high","tip":"Maintain steady, natural eye contact when speaking and listening, without staring fixedly.","explanation":"Direct eye contact is expected and valued in the Netherlands, seen as a sign of honesty, confidence, and engagement in both business and social conversation. Avoiding eye contact can come across as evasive or disinterested rather than respectful.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can feel intimidating; brief, softer eye contact is generally preferred, especially with someone senior."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact with elders or superiors can be seen as challenging or disrespectful in hierarchical settings."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"polite","note":"Direct eye contact is similarly valued as a sign of honesty and confidence in business and social settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Are there any cultural do's and don'ts in the Netherlands?","url":"https://debollenstreek.com/general-questions/cultural-dos-and-donts-the-netherlands/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Depends on the household","confidence":"low","tip":"Watch what your host does, or simply ask if you should take your shoes off when you arrive.","explanation":"There's no single national rule in the Netherlands — some households expect guests to remove their shoes, especially if it's wet outside or there are young children, while others don't mind shoes staying on. It comes down to the individual household rather than a strong cultural expectation either way.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes at the entrance (genkan) is mandatory and deeply embedded in etiquette."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"polite","note":"Removing shoes before entering a home is standard practice and considered basic etiquette."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoes-off is common in some homes, especially in snowy regions, but it's not a universal rule — always follow the host's lead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Are there any cultural do's and don'ts in the Netherlands?","url":"https://debollenstreek.com/general-questions/cultural-dos-and-donts-the-netherlands/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine for directions, not people","confidence":"medium","tip":"Point at objects or directions freely, but avoid pointing your finger directly at a person.","explanation":"Pointing at objects or to give directions is unremarkable in the Netherlands, much like elsewhere in Western Europe — Dutch culture's famous directness means gesturing plainly isn't seen as impolite. Pointing directly at a person, however, can come across as confrontational, which is a near-universal mild courtesy rather than anything uniquely Dutch.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with a single finger, especially at a person, is considered rude; an open hand is used instead."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing at objects or directions is common and unremarkable in casual contexts."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"rude","note":"Pointing with the index finger, especially at a person, is considered offensive; the thumb is used instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"List of gestures","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Are there any cultural do's and don'ts in the Netherlands?","url":"https://debollenstreek.com/general-questions/cultural-dos-and-donts-the-netherlands/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Mild affection fine, not overt","confidence":"low","tip":"Keep public affection brief and low-key, and take your cues from how comfortable the locals around you seem.","explanation":"Light affection such as hand-holding or a quick kiss between couples is unremarkable in Dutch public life, consistent with a generally relaxed, pragmatic culture. More prolonged or overt displays aren't illegal or shocking, but can draw a little more attention in a culture that otherwise favors reserved, understated public behavior.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"rude","note":"Public displays of affection, even hand-holding, can be illegal and lead to fines or arrest."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing as a greeting and casual affection between couples are widely accepted and common in public."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Public displays of affection are uncommon and can draw attention; even hand-holding tends to be discreet."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Dutch Business Etiquette Guide","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/dutch-business-etiquette-guide-2026","kind":"community"},{"title":"Foreign travel advice: Netherlands - local laws and customs","url":"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/netherlands/local-laws-and-customs","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"rude","label":"Punctuality expected, lateness unprofessional","confidence":"high","tip":"Aim to arrive a few minutes early, and message ahead immediately if you expect even a slight delay.","explanation":"Punctuality is taken seriously in Dutch business culture and is viewed as a basic sign of respect and reliability. Meetings are structured and expected to start and end on time, so arriving late — even by a few minutes — can genuinely damage your credibility with Dutch colleagues or clients.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Extreme punctuality is expected in business; even a few minutes' lateness is a serious breach of etiquette."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"Lateness of up to 15-30 minutes is often tolerated in business settings, though this varies by region and company."},{"country":"Spain","verdict":"caution","note":"Business meetings may start a little later than scheduled, and brief delays are tolerated more than in the Netherlands."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Are there any cultural do's and don'ts in the Netherlands?","url":"https://debollenstreek.com/general-questions/cultural-dos-and-donts-the-netherlands/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Dutch Business Etiquette Guide","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/dutch-business-etiquette-guide-2026","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"A direct decline is fine","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you don't want something, say a direct 'no, thank you' when it's offered rather than taking a small portion and leaving it unfinished.","explanation":"Dutch culture places a high value on directness, and that extends to hospitality — a simple, polite 'no, thank you' to an offer of food is generally taken at face value rather than seen as an insult, unlike in more insistence-driven hosting cultures. That said, once food has actually been served to you, leaving a lot of it on your plate can read as wasteful, so it's better to decline upfront if you don't want it rather than accept and not finish it.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"It's common and often expected to initially decline food out of modesty before accepting, with hosts sometimes insisting."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Declining food is often seen as rejecting the host's generosity; accepting at least a small portion is expected."},{"country":"India","verdict":"caution","note":"Declining food can seem impolite, but explaining dietary or religious reasons is accepted, and hosts may still insist you try something."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Dutch customs and etiquette","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_customs_and_etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Dutch Business Etiquette Guide","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/dutch-business-etiquette-guide-2026","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Positive, harmless gesture","confidence":"high","tip":"Use it freely to mean 'all good' or 'okay' in casual conversation.","explanation":"The OK sign (thumb and index finger forming a circle) is understood as a positive, everyday gesture for 'fine' or 'good' in the Netherlands, as in most of Western Europe. It carries none of the vulgar or offensive meanings it has in some other countries.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture is considered a vulgar insult, similar in severity to showing the middle finger."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"In parts of the Mediterranean and Middle East, including Turkey, the gesture can be read as a vulgar or homophobic insult."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"The gesture commonly means 'money' as well as 'OK', and is generally understood positively."}],"sources":[{"title":"OK gesture","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OK_gesture","kind":"reference"},{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Fine casually, not formally","confidence":"medium","tip":"Save the slurping for casual Asian noodle spots, and eat quietly at more formal Dutch restaurants.","explanation":"Dutch table manners generally favor quiet, tidy eating, so slurping noodles at a typical formal meal can come across as poor manners. At Asian restaurants, though, where slurping is understood as a nod to the chef, it's generally tolerated or simply unremarkable, especially in the more multicultural cities.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is a sign of enjoyment and appreciation for the chef, not bad manners."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping is generally seen as poor table manners and out of place in most dining settings."},{"country":"China","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is common and can signal that the food is delicious."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Are there any cultural do's and don'ts in the Netherlands?","url":"https://debollenstreek.com/general-questions/cultural-dos-and-donts-the-netherlands/","kind":"community"},{"title":"Dutch Business Etiquette Guide","url":"https://trykaiwa.com/blog/dutch-business-etiquette-guide-2026","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"polite","label":"Common among friends, not strangers","confidence":"medium","tip":"Offer a handshake by default, and only move to cheek kisses if the other person initiates it or you already know them socially.","explanation":"Three alternating cheek kisses are a common greeting between friends, family, and acquaintances in the Netherlands, especially on birthdays and other celebratory occasions, but it isn't used with strangers or in business settings. Men typically shake hands with other men and with people they don't know well, while women more often exchange cheek kisses with both women and men they're already acquainted with.","regional_note":"Cheek kissing is somewhat more common in the southern Netherlands than in the north.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing is a standard greeting, though the number of kisses varies by region, typically two to four."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Physical greetings like cheek kissing are uncommon; a bow is the traditional and expected greeting instead."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"One or two cheek kisses are common even among people who've just met, typically starting with the right cheek."}],"sources":[{"title":"Cheek kissing","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheek_kissing","kind":"reference"},{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Standard everyday gesture","confidence":"medium","tip":"Use it normally with friends, family, or colleagues, but opt for a simple wave or open hand when beckoning someone more senior or a stranger.","explanation":"Beckoning with a curled index finger, palm up, is the ordinary way people in the Netherlands and the rest of Northern Europe wave someone over — it is not seen as rude or dismissive the way it is in parts of Asia. It's best reserved for peers and casual situations rather than used toward someone senior or a stranger, where it can come across as a bit too familiar.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a curled finger is highly offensive, as it's associated with calling animals; a hand wave with the palm down is used instead."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"The index-finger, palm-up beckon can be seen as rude or childish; a palm-down waving motion is preferred."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Common in casual settings, but it can come across as aggressive or dismissive in more formal or professional contexts."}],"sources":[{"title":"List of gestures","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_gestures","kind":"reference"},{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Modest gifts, mind the flowers","confidence":"high","tip":"If you bring flowers, give an odd number (never 13) and avoid white lilies or chrysanthemums, which are associated with funerals; present any gift simply and open it right away to thank the giver.","explanation":"Gift-giving isn't a strong expectation in Dutch business culture and is more at home in social settings, like being invited to someone's house. When a gift is given, it should be modest and presented without ceremony — lavish or very personal gifts can feel like they're buying influence rather than showing appreciation.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is a deeply ingrained ritual in Japanese business culture, with strict rules on wrapping, presentation, and reciprocity."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Similar to the Netherlands, business gifts aren't expected; if given, they should be modest and professional rather than personal."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is common and appreciated in UAE business culture, but should respect Islamic customs, such as avoiding alcohol and pork and using the right hand."}],"sources":[{"title":"The Netherlands Guide (Business Culture)","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/netherlands-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Are there any cultural do's and don'ts in the Netherlands?","url":"https://debollenstreek.com/general-questions/cultural-dos-and-donts-the-netherlands/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]},"Singapore":{"slug":"singapore","items":[{"key":"tipping-at-a-restaurant","gesture":"Tipping at a restaurant","short":"Tipping","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Not expected, not rude","confidence":"high","tip":"Check your bill for the \"++\" service charge before adding anything extra; if you do want to tip for excellent service, hand it directly to the staff member rather than leaving it on the table.","explanation":"Singapore has no real tipping culture; many sit-down restaurants already add a 10% service charge (shown as \"++\" on menus) plus GST, so an extra tip is not expected. Leaving a small amount for great service is not rude, just unnecessary, and it is never expected at hawker centres, cafes, or bars.","regional_note":"Hawker centres, food courts, and casual cafes rarely add a service charge and tipping there is unusual; upscale restaurants and hotels catering to tourists or expats see occasional voluntary tipping.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Tipping is considered confusing or insulting since good service is already the standard; servers may try to return the money."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Tipping 15-20% at sit-down restaurants is standard and service staff rely on it as part of their income."},{"country":"France","verdict":"caution","note":"A service charge is included by law, so extra tipping is optional but rounding up or leaving small change is appreciated."}],"sources":[{"title":"Tipping Etiquette - Visit Singapore","url":"https://www.visitsingapore.com/travel-guide-tips/essential-information/tipping-etiquette/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"How to Tip in Singapore - Tripmasters","url":"https://www.tripmasters.com/tipping_in_singapore/cms?cms=6326","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"thumbs-up","gesture":"Thumbs up","short":"Thumbs up","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Normal friendly gesture","confidence":"medium","tip":"Use it freely for everyday approval (food, service, agreement) - there's no need to avoid it the way you might in parts of the Middle East or West Africa.","explanation":"In Singapore the thumbs-up is understood the same way as in the West: approval, agreement, or encouragement. It is used casually in daily life, service settings, and on social media without causing offense.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Iran","verdict":"rude","note":"The thumbs-up is an obscene insult there, comparable to the middle finger in the US."},{"country":"Greece","verdict":"caution","note":"A thrust-forward thumbs-up can read as a vulgar gesture rather than simple approval."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"polite","note":"The thumbs-up is understood as positive and causes no offense, similar to Singapore."}],"sources":[{"title":"Singaporean Culture - Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/singaporean-culture/singaporean-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Offensive Gestures by Country - World Population Review","url":"https://worldpopulationreview.com/country-rankings/offensive-gestures-in-other-countries","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"showing-the-soles-of-your-feet","gesture":"Showing the soles of your feet","short":"Showing soles of feet","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Mind your feet, context matters","confidence":"medium","tip":"When sitting on the floor or low seating in a Malay or Indian home, tuck your feet under you or sit cross-legged rather than stretching your legs out toward others.","explanation":"Showing the soles of your feet or pointing them at someone is considered impolite mainly within Singapore's Malay and Indian communities, where feet are viewed as the lowest, least clean part of the body. It is not a universal taboo across all Singaporeans, especially in casual or Chinese-majority secular settings, but it's safest to keep your feet down and avoid pointing them at people or religious items.","regional_note":"The taboo is strongest in Malay-Muslim and Indian-Hindu households (feet seen as unclean, never pointed at people, prayer mats, or images); it is much less observed among Chinese or Westernized Singaporeans in everyday settings.","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The feet are the lowest part of the body; pointing them at a person or a Buddha image is a serious taboo."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Showing the soles of your feet, even on furniture, is seen as casual rather than offensive."},{"country":"Saudi Arabia","verdict":"rude","note":"Showing the soles of your feet to someone, such as while sitting cross-legged, is considered a clear insult."}],"sources":[{"title":"Malaysian Culture - Communication (Cultural Atlas, shared Malay cultural norms)","url":"https://culturalatlas.sbs.com.au/malaysian-culture/malaysian-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Cultural etiquette tips for travelling South East Asia - SilverKris (Singapore Airlines)","url":"https://silverkris.singaporeair.com/inspiration/nature-adventure/travel-tips/cultural-etiquette-tips-travelling-south-east-asia/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"eating-with-your-left-hand","gesture":"Eating with your left hand","short":"Eating with the left hand","context":"dining","verdict":"caution","label":"Right hand in Malay/Indian settings","confidence":"medium","tip":"At a Malay or Indian household, or when eating traditional dishes with your hands, use only your right hand for eating, passing dishes, and giving or receiving items.","explanation":"Eating, passing food, or gesturing with the left hand is considered unclean specifically in Singapore's Malay and Indian communities, since the left hand is traditionally reserved for personal hygiene. In Chinese-majority or Westernized settings (most restaurants, hawker centres, offices), it draws no attention at all since cutlery or chopsticks are used.","regional_note":"Strongest in Malay-Muslim and Indian households that eat with their hands; largely irrelevant in Chinese or international dining contexts where cutlery or chopsticks are standard.","also_check":[{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"Using the left hand for eating or passing food is considered unclean and impolite in most traditional households."},{"country":"Morocco","verdict":"rude","note":"The left hand is reserved for hygiene; eating or passing food with it is a clear faux pas."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"There is no left-hand taboo; chopstick etiquette matters far more than which hand you use."}],"sources":[{"title":"Etiquette in Singapore - Frommer's","url":"https://www.frommers.com/destinations/singapore/in-depth/etiquette/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Singapore Country Reports - CountryReports.org","url":"https://www.countryreports.org/country/Singapore.htm","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"patting-someone-on-the-head","gesture":"Patting someone on the head","short":"Patting someone's head","context":"social","verdict":"rude","label":"Don't touch anyone's head","confidence":"medium","tip":"Even an affectionate pat on a child's head can cause offense; if you want to show warmth, smile, wave, or lightly touch a shoulder instead.","explanation":"Touching or patting someone's head, including a child's, is considered disrespectful in Singapore across all ethnic communities, since the head is viewed as the most sacred, highest part of the body. It carries extra religious weight for Malay-Muslim and Indian-Hindu Singaporeans, but general etiquette guides treat it as a no-go regardless of who you're with.","regional_note":"The religious weight is strongest for Malay-Muslim and Indian-Hindu Singaporeans (the head as spiritually sacred), but general etiquette guidance treats head-touching as impolite for all groups, not just these two.","also_check":[{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is the most sacred part of the body; touching it, even a child's, is a serious offense."},{"country":"India","verdict":"rude","note":"The head is considered sacred, especially in Hindu tradition, and touching it without reason is disrespectful."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Patting a child's head is usually seen as a harmless, friendly gesture."}],"sources":[{"title":"Singaporean Culture - Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/singaporean-culture/singaporean-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Shoving incident in Singapore over man patting girl's head stirs heated debate - SCMP / The Vibes","url":"https://www.thevibes.com/articles/world/126263/singapore-police-probe-assault-of-73-year-old-man-after-he-patted-toddlers-head-at-food-court","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"direct-eye-contact","gesture":"Direct eye contact","short":"Direct eye contact","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Moderate, respectful eye contact","confidence":"medium","tip":"Keep eye contact natural and intermittent, especially with older or more senior people; don't read a lowered gaze from a Malay or Indian colleague as disengagement.","explanation":"Eye contact in Singapore generally signals confidence and attentiveness, similar to Western norms, but prolonged or intense eye contact can come across as challenging or confrontational, especially with someone senior. Some Malay Muslims and Hindus may lower or avert their eyes more when speaking with elders or superiors as a sign of respect, not disinterest.","regional_note":"Muslim Malay and some Hindu Singaporeans may avert their eyes more often with superiors or elders as a mark of deference; this is a sign of respect, not avoidance or dishonesty.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Prolonged direct eye contact can feel confrontational; brief, intermittent eye contact is preferred, especially with seniors."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Steady eye contact is expected and read as a sign of confidence and honesty."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"caution","note":"Lowering the eyes with elders or superiors is a sign of respect rather than dishonesty or disinterest."}],"sources":[{"title":"Singaporean Culture - Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/singaporean-culture/singaporean-culture-communication","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"removing-your-shoes-indoors","gesture":"Removing your shoes indoors","short":"Removing shoes indoors","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Expected in most homes","confidence":"high","tip":"Look for a shoe rack or a pile of shoes by the door as your cue, and if in doubt, just ask \"should I take my shoes off?\" before stepping inside.","explanation":"Removing your shoes before entering someone's home is standard practice across Singapore's communities, reflecting both cultural norms and practicality in a tropical climate. It's considered rude to walk into a home in outdoor shoes unless your host explicitly tells you not to bother.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoe removal is mandatory before entering any home, with indoor slippers often provided."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Shoe removal is optional and varies by household; many hosts don't expect it unless asked."},{"country":"Thailand","verdict":"polite","note":"Shoes are removed before entering homes and most temples, following the same feet-related cleanliness norms."}],"sources":[{"title":"Singaporean Culture - Etiquette (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/singaporean-culture/singaporean-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"pointing-with-your-index-finger","gesture":"Pointing with your index finger","short":"Pointing with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"caution","label":"Use an open hand instead","confidence":"high","tip":"Gesture with an open hand (palm up) or a nod of the head toward what you mean, instead of pointing your index finger at people.","explanation":"Pointing directly at someone or something with your index finger is seen as impolite in Singapore; multiple etiquette guides describe it as rude and recommend gesturing with an open hand or thumb instead. It's a soft social norm rather than a severe taboo, so casual slip-ups are unlikely to cause lasting offense.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Pointing with the index finger is seen as impolite; gesturing with an open hand is preferred."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"polite","note":"Pointing with the index finger is common and unremarkable in everyday, casual interactions."},{"country":"Indonesia","verdict":"caution","note":"Pointing at people with the index finger is considered impolite; the thumb is commonly used instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Singaporean Culture - Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/singaporean-culture/singaporean-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Culture Shock in Singapore - Expat Exchange","url":"https://expatexchange.com/advice/95/102139/6/Singapore/Culture-Shock-in-","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"public-displays-of-affection","gesture":"Public displays of affection","short":"Public displays of affection","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Keep it modest in public","confidence":"medium","tip":"Casual hand-holding or a quick hug is fine almost anywhere; save more intimate affection for private settings, especially around family-oriented or religious spaces.","explanation":"Singapore isn't especially strict about affection in public compared to its neighbors, but overt displays like prolonged kissing or groping are generally seen as inappropriate and can draw disapproving looks, particularly from older generations. Brief hand-holding or a quick hug is unlikely to bother anyone; there's no law against ordinary PDA between consenting adults, unlike some neighboring countries.","regional_note":"Older and more religiously conservative Singaporeans (including many Malay-Muslim and Indian families) tend to view public affection less favorably than younger, more Westernized Singaporeans.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"PDA is uncommon and can draw attention; even hand-holding is often reserved for younger couples."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Kissing and casual affection in public are common and not considered inappropriate."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"rude","note":"Public affection beyond hand-holding between unmarried couples can be illegal and lead to fines or detention."}],"sources":[{"title":"Singaporean Culture - Etiquette (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/singaporean-culture/singaporean-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Culture Shock in Singapore - Expat Exchange","url":"https://expatexchange.com/advice/95/102139/6/Singapore/Culture-Shock-in-","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"arriving-late-to-a-meeting","gesture":"Arriving late to a meeting","short":"Arriving late","context":"business","verdict":"rude","label":"Punctuality is expected","confidence":"high","tip":"Aim to arrive 5-10 minutes early, and if you're running late, notify your host as soon as you know rather than waiting until the meeting time has passed.","explanation":"Singapore's business culture places a high value on punctuality, and arriving late to a meeting is seen as a lack of respect for the other person's time. If you're unavoidably delayed, calling or messaging ahead is expected and softens the impact considerably.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Punctuality norms are even stricter; arriving even a few minutes late is seen as disrespectful and often requires a sincere apology."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"caution","note":"Social gatherings allow more flexibility, but arriving late to a first business meeting is still risky."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"caution","note":"Social events often start later than scheduled, but business meetings generally still expect punctuality."}],"sources":[{"title":"Singaporean Culture - Etiquette (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/singaporean-culture/singaporean-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Laws and Etiquette to Keep in Mind on a Business Trip to Singapore","url":"https://smbceo.com/2020/09/22/laws-and-etiquette-to-keep-in-mind-on-a-business-trip-to-singapore/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"refusing-food-offered-by-a-host","gesture":"Refusing food offered by a host","short":"Refusing offered food","context":"family","verdict":"caution","label":"Decline gently, not flatly","confidence":"medium","tip":"If you don't want more food, say something like \"I'm so full, but it was delicious, thank you\" rather than a flat \"no\" - and expect your host to offer at least once more before taking your answer at face value.","explanation":"Repeatedly refusing food offered by a host in Singapore can come across as rejecting their hospitality, since sharing food is central to how relationships are built there. A polite decline with a brief, warm explanation (fullness, dietary restriction) is well understood and won't cause lasting offense, especially given how common dietary restrictions already are across Singapore's communities.","regional_note":"Halal, vegetarian, and other dietary restrictions are common and well understood across Malay-Muslim, Hindu, and other communities, so a dietary-based refusal is rarely seen as impolite; it's blunt or repeated refusal without explanation that can land badly.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"Refusing food can be seen as rejecting the host's effort and generosity; it's better to at least taste what's offered."},{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Declining food when full is unremarkable; overeating to be polite isn't expected."},{"country":"South Korea","verdict":"caution","note":"A polite refusal with a clear reason is accepted, but an outright 'no' without explanation can seem dismissive of the host's care."}],"sources":[{"title":"Singapore Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/singapore-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Top 5 Tips for Proper Singapore Etiquette - Absolute Internship","url":"https://absoluteinternship.com/blog/top-5-tips-for-proper-singapore-etiquette/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"the-ok-hand-sign","gesture":"The OK hand sign","short":"The OK hand sign","context":"general","verdict":"polite","label":"Understood as positive","confidence":"medium","tip":"Feel free to use the OK sign casually; it's not considered rude or vulgar in Singapore the way it is in some other countries.","explanation":"The OK hand sign is understood in Singapore the same way as in most Western countries - meaning \"okay\" or \"good\" - without the offensive connotations it carries in places like Brazil or Turkey. It's used casually and isn't flagged as a taboo gesture in Singapore-specific etiquette guides.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"rude","note":"The OK sign is a vulgar insult, roughly equivalent to showing the middle finger."},{"country":"Turkey","verdict":"rude","note":"The gesture can be read as a derogatory insult in some contexts."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Commonly understood to mean 'okay' or sometimes 'money,' with no offensive connotation."}],"sources":[{"title":"Singaporean Culture - Communication (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/singaporean-culture/singaporean-culture-communication","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Offensive Gestures by Country - World Population Review","url":"https://worldpopulationreview.com/country-rankings/offensive-gestures-in-other-countries","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"slurping-your-noodles","gesture":"Slurping your noodles","short":"Slurping noodles","context":"dining","verdict":"polite","label":"Not frowned upon, informal","confidence":"low","tip":"Eat noodle soups however feels natural at a hawker centre - nobody will bat an eye at a bit of noise; just dial it back in a quiet, upscale restaurant.","explanation":"Singapore's hawker and noodle-dish culture (influenced heavily by Chinese and Malay food traditions) is informal, and no etiquette source specifically flags slurping noodles as rude. It isn't treated as a special compliment to the cook the way it is in Japan, but it isn't a breach of manners either, especially at hawker centres.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Slurping noodles is a genuine compliment to the chef and considered normal, even in nicer restaurants."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"Audible slurping is generally seen as poor table manners, especially outside very casual settings."},{"country":"Italy","verdict":"rude","note":"Slurping pasta or any food is seen as a clear breach of table manners."}],"sources":[{"title":"Foodie Culture - Visit Singapore","url":"https://www.visitsingapore.com/editorials/foodie-culture/","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Etiquette in Singapore - Frommer's (chopstick/noodle dining customs)","url":"https://www.frommers.com/destinations/singapore/in-depth/etiquette/","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"kissing-on-the-cheek-when-greeting","gesture":"Kissing on the cheek when greeting","short":"Cheek-kiss greeting","context":"social","verdict":"caution","label":"Not the standard greeting","confidence":"high","tip":"Default to a light handshake; if someone of the opposite gender doesn't extend their hand, follow their lead and offer a nod or a hand over your heart instead.","explanation":"Cheek kissing is not a standard greeting in Singapore; a handshake is the default across all communities, and some Malay or Indian Singaporeans may prefer not to shake hands with the opposite gender at all for religious or cultural reasons. Cheek kissing does happen among close friends in more Westernized or expatriate circles, but it isn't the norm for a first meeting.","regional_note":"Malay and Indian Singaporeans may avoid cross-gender handshakes for religious reasons; Chinese Singaporeans may add a slight nod to a handshake. None of these groups treat cheek kissing as a standard greeting.","also_check":[{"country":"France","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing (la bise) is a standard greeting among friends and acquaintances, with the number of kisses varying by region."},{"country":"Japan","verdict":"caution","note":"Physical greetings are minimal; a bow is standard and cheek kissing would be seen as unusually intimate."},{"country":"Brazil","verdict":"polite","note":"Cheek kissing is a common greeting, even between new acquaintances."}],"sources":[{"title":"Singaporean Culture - Greetings (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/singaporean-culture/singaporean-culture-greetings","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Top 5 Tips for Proper Singapore Etiquette - Absolute Internship","url":"https://absoluteinternship.com/blog/top-5-tips-for-proper-singapore-etiquette/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"beckoning-someone-with-your-finger","gesture":"Beckoning someone with your finger","short":"Beckoning with a finger","context":"general","verdict":"rude","label":"Seen as disrespectful, even hostile","confidence":"medium","tip":"To call someone over, wave with your whole hand, palm facing down, rather than curling a single finger toward yourself.","explanation":"Beckoning someone with a curled index finger (palm up) is considered rude and even aggressive in Singapore, similar to Japan, since the gesture is associated with calling animals or, in some tellings, has a connotation linked to death. A polite alternative is to wave with the palm facing down.","regional_note":"","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"rude","note":"The same curled-finger beckoning gesture is considered rude and is associated with calling animals rather than people."},{"country":"Philippines","verdict":"rude","note":"Beckoning with a curled finger can be seen as a serious insult and is even linked to past legal trouble when used toward police."},{"country":"United States","verdict":"caution","note":"The gesture is used casually among friends but can come across as arrogant or demanding in formal settings."}],"sources":[{"title":"10 Innocent Hand Gestures You Should Never Use Abroad - Boston.com","url":"https://boston.com/travel/travel/2014/03/26/10-innocent-hand-gestures-you-should-never-use-abroad","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Hand gesture cross-cultural guides (aggregated web search)","url":"https://acutrans.com/understanding-non-verbal-communication-gestures-that-vary-across-cultures/","kind":"community"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"},{"key":"giving-a-gift","gesture":"Giving a gift","short":"Giving a gift","context":"business","verdict":"caution","label":"Know the community-specific rules","confidence":"high","tip":"When in doubt, a high-quality food gift (confirmed halal if for a Malay host) or a nicely presented item in red/gold wrapping for a Chinese host is a safe default; avoid clocks, sets of four, leather, and alcohol unless you know your host well.","explanation":"Gift-giving customs in Singapore vary sharply by community rather than following one uniform rule. Chinese Singaporeans avoid clocks, items in sets of four, and white or yellow flowers (funeral associations); Malay Muslims require halal gifts and avoid alcohol or pork; Indian/Hindu Singaporeans avoid leather and alcohol. Across all groups, gifts are usually given and received with both hands (or the right hand) and not opened in front of the giver.","regional_note":"Chinese Singaporeans: avoid clocks, sets of four, sharp objects, and white/yellow flowers (funeral associations), and favor red or gold wrapping. Malay Muslims: avoid alcohol and pork, ensure gifts are halal, and give with the right hand. Indian/Hindu Singaporeans: avoid leather and alcohol, and use bright wrapping.","also_check":[{"country":"Japan","verdict":"polite","note":"Gift-giving is highly ritualized, with strict expectations on wrapping, presentation, and reciprocation."},{"country":"Germany","verdict":"caution","note":"Business gifts are often unnecessary or even viewed with suspicion as an attempt to curry favor."},{"country":"United Arab Emirates","verdict":"caution","note":"Gift-giving is welcomed but must avoid alcohol and pork and should be given with the right hand, per Islamic custom."}],"sources":[{"title":"Singapore Guide - Commisceo Global","url":"https://www.commisceo-global.com/resources/country-guides/singapore-guide","kind":"reference"},{"title":"Singaporean Culture - Etiquette (Cultural Atlas)","url":"https://theculturalatlas.org/singaporean-culture/singaporean-culture-etiquette","kind":"reference"}],"reviewed":"2026-10-18"}]}};
